window.nttNotebookSearchIndex = [{"title": "Project & setup", "summary": "Build, test, and understand the correctness-first CKKS GPU project.", "group": "Start here", "url": "project.html", "text": "ntt-gpu ¶ Exact GPU kernels for CKKS: NTT first, then base conversion, Hybrid key switching, and OpenFHE integration. The target workload is N=65,536 on RTX 5090, with RTX 4090 as the compatibility control. Current state: the CPU reference, Python oracle, portable GPU-schedule simulator, and OpenFHE transform/representation/Hybrid graph gates are implemented. CUDA binaries compile for sm_89 and sm_120 with a compiler-only toolkit; execution on a real GPU remains the next hardware gate. CPU simulation does not validate GPU barriers or launch behavior. Build & test ¶ Requires CMake, a C++20 compiler, and Python 3. CUDA is optional and detected automatically. cmake -S . -B build -DNTT_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF cmake --build build -j ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure The fifteen portable checks cover vector generation at N=64/1024/65536, unit tests, the independent Python oracle, the shared device schedule, column and tile index/twiddle proofs in both directions, arithmetic exactness and full-size adversarial transforms, backend metadata, host CUDA preflight, session failure reporting, and the N=8 debug example. Small fixtures keep the quadratic oracle and schoolbook multiplication practical. With nvcc, three GPU gates compare CUDA with the same oracle and CPU reference. Enabling OpenFHE adds a fourth direct CUDA/OpenFHE gate. A separate compiled-wrapper constructor check requires no GPU. Array ordering, forward/inverse results, round trips, and polynomial multiplication must agree exactly. See run and debug to step through the N=8 example. Exact OpenFHE compatibility ¶ The optional gate pins OpenFHE 1.6.1 at b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f : bash scripts/openfhe_setup.sh It compares N=65536 NativePoly coefficient/evaluation conversions with our reference, including bit-reversed evaluation order. Its FirstPrime(50, ...) and LastPrime(59, ...) fixtures are 51- and 59-bit primes. Cases include zero, maximum, ramp, random coefficients, and independent random inverse inputs. A second gate, openfhe_representation_contract , checks exact evaluation indices, format-changing APIs, root selection, and three-tower DCRT packing at 50/55/60-bit primes. Read the D7 contract for the integration boundary and remaining scope. A third gate, openfhe_hybrid_graph , traces the exact Hybrid digit extension, key MAC, ModDown, and ciphertext wrapper graph at N=65536. It checks full and reduced levels with deterministic diagnostic keys and actual generated keys, using independent unreduced-CRT formulas. It requires the PKE library and the 64-bit, non-reduced-noise build; see the D8 graph . The normal CPU build also runs backend_contract_check , which checks D9's buffer/layout metadata, stream dependencies, cache identities and preflight fallback policy. The D9 backend proposal includes compile-checked abstract interfaces; it does not implement a CUDA runtime backend or OpenFHE adapter. D10 accepts the behavioral design in ADR 0001 and tracks the remaining native GPU/runtime work in the implementation backlog . P1 is complete; the next task is D11's GPU environment record, not a claimed CUDA result. To reuse an installation, configure with -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON and set CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH to its install prefix. OpenFHE is not required for the standalone build. First session on a rented GPU ¶ Finish the offline preparation first. On the GPU image, use the selected clean checkout and an existing pinned OpenFHE prefix: bash scripts/autodl_setup.sh The script records hardware/toolchain details, requires CUDA and OpenFHE test registration, builds, runs exact tests and sanitizer smoke checks, and collects the 46-bit transfer-inclusive baseline benchmark. Each session writes logs and report.json under an ignored bench-results-* directory. Any failed stage returns nonzero. It does not update source, install packages or change drivers. Select paths with NTT_REPO_DIR , NTT_CUDA_ROOT , and NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX ; NTT_CUDA_ARCH defaults to native . See the preparation guide for full commands and the remaining P2 gates. The default build targets sm_89 and, with CUDA 12.8+, sm_120 . For one known GPU, use -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=native (CMake 3.24+). Design ¶ Both backends use the same negacyclic transform convention: psi is a primitive 2N-th root modulo q; psi^N = -1 forward: A_j = sum_i a_i * psi^((2j+1)*i) mod q input: natural coefficient order; output: bit-reversed evaluation order The CUDA schedule splits at 256. Pass 1 gathers 256 strided columns; pass 2 loads 256 contiguous rows. At N=65536, each pass handles eight stages in shared memory. One forward uses two launches and four logical array movements. The strided pass is uncoalesced, so logical bytes are not measured DRAM traffic. Stage bodies in dev_core.hpp are compiled for both CPU simulation and CUDA. The current __uint128_t remainder arithmetic is a correctness baseline. Shoup/Barrett reduction, coalesced layouts, and register staging are planned optimizations, subject to exact checks and profiling. Use the work-distribution explorer to inspect blocks, threads, pairs, and twiddle exponents. Roadmap ¶ Deliverable State CPU reference, oracle, schedule simulation Complete CUDA kernels and automatic GPU test registration Implemented; hardware validation pending Named-device baseline and tuning Planned Base conversion and Hybrid key switching Planned OpenFHE integration with CPU fallback Planned The v0.1 target is 2027-01-31; v0.2 key-switch/automorphism work targets 2027-04-30. See the tracked integration plan for working-day gates and the benchmark plan for measurements. Performance claims require named hardware and matching timing boundaries. Docs ¶ Start with NTT and butterflies , then CPU to CUDA . The implementation survey and GPU comparison explain the design choices. python3 -m venv .venv .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r docs/requirements.txt .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory build/docs-site Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ . See writing and publishing for editing, journals, and GitHub Pages export. Provenance ¶ Independent clean-room implementation. No code, data, or artifacts from past or present employers. Development history is recorded in git. License ¶ TBD before accepting outside contributions."}, {"title": "NTT & butterflies", "summary": "Direct sums, recursive butterflies, handwritten derivations, and an interactive stage/twiddle calculator.", "group": "Learn", "url": "butterfly-explained.html", "text": "ntt-gpu · a beginner's field guide NTT and butterflies. An NTT turns one expensive polynomial multiplication into many small, repeated butterfly operations. no prerequisites examples use mod 5 and mod 17 interactive diagrams × w a b a + w·b a − w·b the only shape this whole page needs The idea in four steps Read this first. The chapters below explain why each step is valid. 1 · Start A polynomial is stored as a list of coefficients. 2 · Transform The NTT evaluates that list at special modular points. 3 · Reuse A butterfly computes one shared sum-and-difference pair. 4 · Repeat N/2 butterflies across log₂N stages finish the transform. Shortest path: data → NTT → sum to butterfly → butterfly → network → negacyclic . The other chapters are optional detail. Contents 01 Input: coefficients 02 Special points 03 What the NTT does 04 Cost & savings 05 Sum → butterflies 05+ Derivation and twiddle factors 06 One butterfly 07 Full N = 8 network 09 Negacyclic NTT 10 Code mapping 01 Start with coefficients The input is a list of N numbers . In this page, that list represents a polynomial: 3 slot 0 7 slot 1 1 slot 2 9 slot 3 4 slot 4 0 slot 5 5 slot 6 2 slot 7 For this project, N is 65,536. We need to multiply two such lists efficiently: multiply two lists of length N, get back a list of length N The NTT (Number Theoretic Transform) is how that multiplication gets done fast. (How the product stays length N — the fold rule — is chapter 9's story.) 02 Choose special modular points Modulo 17, start at 1 and keep multiplying by 2. After eight different values, the sequence returns to 1: The wheel — the eight powers of 2, mod 17 · start at 1, keep multiplying by 2 1 2 4 8 16 15 13 9 ω⁰ ω¹ ω² ω³ ω⁴ = −1 ω⁵ ω⁶ ω⁷ dashed diameters: opposite points sum to 17 = q — each is the other's negative (property iii, halfway = minus) Eight different numbers, then the ninth step lands back on 1. The powers of 2 form a closed loop of exactly 8 points — a wheel . Such a number is a root of unity (\"unity\" just means 1): \\(ω^N = 1\\) , and primitive if no earlier power comes home. Everything the NTT relies on is a property of this wheel: Nothing sacred about 2 — mod 17 offers four primitive 8th roots: 2, 8, 9, 15. Each walks the same eight points in a different order; 2 just gives the easiest multiplication table. Optional: why these points make the NTT fast i. Closed: multiply = move Powers never leave the wheel: \\(ω^{a}\\,ω^{b} = ω^{a+b}\\) — multiplying just adds positions, a clock for multiplication (the logarithm trick). Why: one number generates all N plug-in points, one multiplication each. ii. Distinct: nothing is lost A primitive root visits N different points, so N plugged-in values pin the list down exactly — the coordinates are reversible. Why: the transform has an inverse. The one demand on the prime: N | q−1 ( ntt.hpp:61 ). iii. Halfway = minus \\(ω^{N/2} = -1\\) : opposite points are negatives (above: 1↔16, 2↔15, 4↔13, 8↔9 — each pair sums to 17). Why: the ± that makes output pairs — the butterfly's two wings (chapter 6). iv. Squaring = a half wheel Every second point closes after N/2 steps: a half-size wheel with root ω². Why: wheels nest — the transform builds itself from half-size copies, the source of every \"repeat\" in chapter 5. Why not just any N distinct numbers? Any N distinct points make chapter 3's trick correct . Only wheel points make it fast : the shared brackets of chapter 5 are this wheel's symmetries showing up as algebra. The speed of the NTT is wheel symmetry. 03 What the NTT does A list like \\((2, 1)\\) is a tiny machine: plug a number t in for x, get a value out. And plugged-in values multiply one by one: \\[(A\\cdot B)(t) = A(t)\\times B(t)\\] One multiplication, no crossing terms — and it holds for every t. Both roads below use \\(t = 2\\) (mod 5); change any coefficient and they still agree: Interactive · two roads, one answer (mod 5) A slot 0 − 2 + A slot 1 · x − 1 + B slot 0 − 3 + B slot 1 · x − 2 + ROAD 1 · EXPAND BY HAND ROAD 2 · PLUG IN t = 2 ✓ the two roads agree Road 1 costs four multiplies and still knows the product at only one point. Road 2 costs one multiply — and the equation below runs Road 2 at every wheel point at once. To pin the product down we need its values at N different points — chapter 2's wheel supplies them. the definition · the whole transform in one line \\[A_j = a_0+a_1\\omega^j+a_2\\omega^{2j}+a_3\\omega^{3j}+\\cdots \\pmod{q}\\] Evaluating the list at all N wheel points is the NTT . Output j is the list plugged in at wheel point ωʲ — and because the points are distinct (wheel property ii), the transform inverts: evaluate → multiply pointwise → invert replaces a list multiplication with N plain multiplies. The rest of this page makes that round trip cheap. Why \"NTT\" and not \"FFT\"? The FFT does this with decimals — but decimals round, and rounding errors compound. The NTT does the same idea with whole numbers mod a prime : exact forever. Its wheels exist whenever N | q−1 — checked at ntt.hpp:61 . 04 Direct evaluation vs butterflies: work saved The equation demands N outputs, each a sum of N products — computed one by one, that's \\(N \\times N\\) multiplications per transform: Interactive · feel the N² pain list length N = 65,536 2 8 … 2 18 Radix-2 uses \\(N/2\\) butterflies in each of \\(\\log_2 N\\) stages. At N=65536, direct evaluation forms 4,294,967,296 products ; the network uses 524,288 butterflies . The ratio is 8192:1 in these different counting units. Interactive · direct N² vs butterflies (N/2)·log₂N — log–log plot direct — each output on its own (N² products) butterflies — shared brackets ((N/2)·log₂N units) hover the plot — at N = 65,536 the direct line stands 8,192× above the butterfly line This compares arithmetic counts, not measured speed. A cyclic butterfly uses one multiply, one add and one subtract; this repository's folded negacyclic device butterfly uses two multiplies. CPU twiddle advancement adds another multiply. Doubling N multiplies the direct count by 4, and the butterfly count by \\(2(\\log_2N+1)/\\log_2N\\). The butterfly curve is slightly curved on a log–log plot. 05 Turn the NTT sum into butterflies Start from the ordinary NTT definition. For output index k, direct evaluation forms one N-term sum: \\[ A_k = \\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} a_r\\,\\omega^{rk}, \\qquad 0\\leq k<N \\] Split the exact sum into even and odd input indices. Write \\(r = 2m\\) for even indices and \\(r = 2m+1\\) for odd indices: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} &= \\sum_{m=0}^{N/2-1} a_{2m}\\,(\\omega^2)^{mk} \\\\ \\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} &= \\sum_{m=0}^{N/2-1} a_{2m+1}\\,(\\omega^2)^{mk} \\\\[0.5em] A_k &= \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} + \\omega^k\\,\\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} \\end{aligned} \\] Now look at the output halfway around the wheel. Since \\(ω^{N/2} = -1\\) , its odd half changes sign while its even half does not: \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_{k+N/2} &= \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} - \\omega^k\\,\\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} \\\\ t &= \\omega^k O_k \\\\[0.5em] (A_k,\\ A_{k+N/2}) &= (E_k+t,\\ E_k-t) \\end{aligned} \\] That final pair is one butterfly. The two outputs reuse the same \\(E[k]\\) , the same \\(O[k]\\) , and—most importantly—the same multiplication \\(t = ω^k\\,O[k]\\) . 1 · DIRECT Compute N independent sums, each with N terms. \\(\\text{cost: } O(N^2)\\) 2 · SPLIT Separate every sum into its even-index and odd-index terms. \\(A=E+\\omega^k O\\) 3 · SHARE Pair outputs k and k+N/2. They share everything except a sign. \\(E+t,\\ E-t\\) 4 · RECURSE E and O are smaller NTTs using the root ω². Split them again. \\(\\log_2 N\\text{ stages}\\) Why the optimization keeps working \\(ω^{2}\\) is an N/2-th root of unity, so E and O have exactly the same shape as the original problem, only half as large. Repeating the split reaches two-value problems. Each level contains N/2 butterflies, and there are log₂N levels. What exactly happens in each stage? For this cyclic DIT explanation, first place input coefficients in bit-reversed order. Stage s starts at zero, uses group width M and distance d, and combines two completed half-transforms. Every pair reads the previous stage and writes two new values: \\[ M=2^{s+1},\\quad d=M/2,\\quad U=gM+j,\\quad V=U+d,\\quad 0\\le j<d, \\qquad t=\\omega_N^{jN/M}X^{(s)}_V. \\] \\[ X^{(s+1)}_U=X^{(s)}_U+t,\\qquad X^{(s+1)}_V=X^{(s)}_U-t. \\] Stage 0 combines pairs into 2-point transforms; stage 1 combines two 2-point results into a 4-point result; stage s combines two d-point results into one M-point result. A stage has N/M groups and d butterflies per group, giving N/2 disjoint pairs. A later stage must wait until its input stage finishes. The complete numerical example in the negacyclic section uses the repository's opposite, DIF stage order. The handwritten derivation below expands N=4, recurses into the second split, and derives the twiddle exponent for every stage. Try its four-point arithmetic here first: Interactive · both levels, real numbers — mod 17, ω = 4 (ω² = 16 ≡ −1) a₀ − 3 + a₁ · x − 1 + a₂ · x² − 5 + a₃ · x³ − 2 + Change any coefficient: level 1 feeds level 2, four butterflies total — and the result is checked against the direct definition. One multiply, one add, one subtract; two values in, two out. That is the fast algorithm's only moving part. At N = 8 it simply nests one level deeper — chapter 7's machine, where we go next. 05+ Recursive decomposition and twiddle factors This derivation follows the handwritten notes from the even/odd split through a second recursion, a four-point network, and the twiddle exponent at every stage. It then connects those results to this repository's negacyclic DIF implementation. Notation In the ordinary NTT portion of the notes, φ denotes a primitive N-th root of unity ; here we write ω N , with ω N N/2 = −1. This repository uses ψ, a primitive 2N-th root of unity , with ψ N = −1 and ω N = ψ². The ordinary NTT requires N to divide q−1 and works modulo x N −1; the negacyclic form requires 2N to divide q−1 and works modulo x N +1. All arithmetic below is modulo a prime q, and N is a power of two. 1. Split one N-point NTT into two half-length NTTs Start with the definition: i indexes the input coefficients and j indexes the outputs. Split i into 2r and 2r+1: \\[ \\begin{aligned} b_j&=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i\\omega_N^{ij}\\\\ &=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r}\\omega_N^{2rj} +\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r+1}\\omega_N^{(2r+1)j}\\\\ &=\\underbrace{\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r}(\\omega_N^2)^{rj}}_{A_j} +\\omega_N^j\\underbrace{\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r+1}(\\omega_N^2)^{rj}}_{B_j}. \\end{aligned} \\] A is the N/2-point NTT of the even-index coefficients; B is the N/2-point NTT of the odd-index coefficients. Both use the root ω N ² = ω N/2 . Both are periodic in j with period N/2, so only 0 ≤ j < N/2。 \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_{j+N/2}&=A_j,\\qquad B_{j+N/2}=B_j,\\\\ \\omega_N^{j+N/2}&=-\\omega_N^j,\\\\ b_j&=A_j+\\omega_N^jB_j,\\\\ b_{j+N/2}&=A_j-\\omega_N^jB_j. \\end{aligned} \\] This produces one butterfly pair: compute t = ω N j B j once, then output A j +t and A j −t . The output indices differ by N/2 and share one multiplication. 2. Recurse: where do ω² and ω⁴ come from? Split A's input sequence again. Its even and odd positions correspond to a 4r and a 4r+2 in the original array: \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_j&=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r}\\omega_N^{4rj} +\\omega_N^{2j}\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r+2}\\omega_N^{4rj}\\\\ &=C_j+\\omega_N^{2j}D_j,\\\\ C_j&=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r}(\\omega_N^4)^{rj},\\qquad D_j=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r+2}(\\omega_N^4)^{rj},\\\\ A_{j+N/4}&=C_j-\\omega_N^{2j}D_j,\\qquad 0\\le j<N/4. \\end{aligned} \\] The minus sign follows from (ω N ²) N/4 = −1. Likewise, B splits into a 4r+1 and a 4r+3 . Each recursion halves the length and squares the root: ω N → ω N ² → ω N ⁴ → … , down to length 2. Bottom-up combination visits these lengths in the opposite order. 3. N=4: expand the sums and draw the network Let ω = ω 4 , so ω²=−1 and ω³=−ω. Expand all four outputs and regroup the shared intermediate values: \\[ \\begin{aligned} b_0&=a_0+a_1+a_2+a_3=(a_0+a_2)+(a_1+a_3),\\\\ b_1&=a_0+a_1\\omega+a_2\\omega^2+a_3\\omega^3=(a_0-a_2)+\\omega(a_1-a_3),\\\\ b_2&=a_0+a_1\\omega^2+a_2\\omega^4+a_3\\omega^6=(a_0+a_2)-(a_1+a_3),\\\\ b_3&=a_0+a_1\\omega^3+a_2\\omega^6+a_3\\omega^9=(a_0-a_2)-\\omega(a_1-a_3). \\end{aligned} \\] The first level computes P=a₀+a₂, Q=a₀−a₂, R=a₁+a₃ and S=a₁−a₃. The second combines P and R into b₀ and b₂, and Q and S into b₁ and b₃. Only the latter pair needs a multiplication by ω. Four-point NTT: two levels and four butterflies Inputs a0 and a2 produce P and Q; a1 and a3 produce R and S. P and R produce b0 and b2; Q and omega times S produce b1 and b3. Outputs are drawn in the order b0, b2, b1, b3. a₀ a₁ a₂ a₃ P = a₀+a₂ R = a₁+a₃ Q = a₀−a₂ S = a₁−a₃ b₀ = P+R b₂ = P−R b₁ = Q+ωS b₃ = Q−ωS Level 1: factor 1 Level 2: factor 1 or ω + − + − × ω Each pair shares its intermediate values; four butterflies complete the transform. Scroll horizontally on small screens. The drawing groups outputs as b₀, b₂, b₁, b₃ to show shared intermediates; the mathematical output index is still j. The drawing's vertical order does not prescribe memory order. Standard iterative DIT usually starts with bit-reversed inputs and produces natural-order outputs. This repository's forward DIF takes natural-order inputs and produces bit-reversed evaluations. See the N=8 network and the D7 representation contract for the exact ordering. 4. Derive the stage twiddle from its length Use the notes' bottom-up DIT combination , with s starting at zero. Stage s combines two 2 s -point results into one M=2 s+1 -point result. Here j is the offset within the current butterfly group. \\[ \\begin{aligned} M&=2^{s+1},\\qquad 0\\le s<\\log_2N,\\qquad 0\\le j<M/2,\\\\ \\omega_M&=\\omega_N^{N/M},\\\\ w_{s,j}&=\\omega_M^j=\\left(\\omega_N^{N/2^{s+1}}\\right)^j =\\boxed{\\omega_N^{jN/2^{s+1}}}. \\end{aligned} \\] A butterfly's factor follows from three quantities: stage s → group length M → local offset j . Groups in the same stage share the j-to-factor rule. The group number changes the array positions, not the exponent. N s M ω N exponent 8 0 2 j=0: exponent 0, factor 1 8 1 4 2j: 0, 2 8 2 8 j: 0, 1, 2, 3 65536 0 2 32768j, with j restricted to 0 65536 1 4 16384j: 0, 16384 65536 15 65536 j: 0…32767 Explore the twiddle exponent by stage and offset Convention Notes: cyclic DIT Repository: negacyclic DIF Length N 4 (teaching example) 8 16 65536 (project target) stage s (zero-based) Local offset j Group g ω N exponent E ψ exponent for repository w upper / lower / Pair distance d 5. Read the sequence 8; 4,12; 2,10,6,14 For N=16 and four-bit reversal, these entries are rev₄(1)…rev₄(15). For example, reversing 0001 gives 1000=8; reversing 0011 gives 1100=12. Table index k rev₄(k) 1 8 2, 3 4, 12 4…7 2, 10, 6, 14 8…15 1, 9, 5, 13, 3, 11, 7, 15 If a root table is defined as T[k]=r rev₄(k) , the table above lists its exponents. These numbers alone do not define the order of r, the transform direction, or the butterfly schedule. They are not a universal cyclic DIT twiddle list . In particular, this repository's natural power table and OpenFHE's bit-reversed root table are distinct. See D7's root and cache contract 。 6. Connect the derivation to the forward negacyclic loop The repository's forward transform is DIF. Stage t starts at zero, and distance d=N/2 t+1 decreases. For group length M=2d and local offset J: \\[ \\begin{aligned} E&=\\frac{N}{2d}J,\\qquad w=\\omega_N^E=\\psi^{(N/d)J},\\qquad c=\\psi^d,\\\\ (u,v)&\\longmapsto\\bigl(u+cv,\\;(u-cv)w\\bigr)\\pmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] The change-of-root rule still raises the global root to N/M, but stages run in the opposite direction and include c to fold the negacyclic twist. At N=65536 the first forward stage has d=32768 and w=ω N J =ψ 2J . In the final stage d=1, J must be 0 and w=1. DIT stage 0 is not the first stage of the repository's forward DIF. The inverse uses reciprocal w and c, reverses the stage order, and finally multiplies by N⁻¹. For the exact coefficient indices, bit-reversed output order, and CUDA groups, see the forward and inverse work distribution 。 06 Meet the butterfly A butterfly takes two inputs and produces a sum and a weighted difference. The crossing wires give the circuit its name. These two common forms place the multiplier on different legs: × w + − a b a + b·w a − b·w 1 2 DIT — ① multiply the input , ② add / subtract · what chapters 5–7 derive + − × w a b a + b (a − b)·w 1 2 DIF — ① add / subtract, ② multiply the difference output · the same unit, run the other way Same two wings — only the order of multiply vs ± differs (the gold ①②). DIT multiplies before the crossing, DIF after it. (The repo's negacyclic forward adds one refinement on top of DIF — a ladder constant ψ^d on the input leg; that is chapter 10's loop.) 07 Connect butterflies into stages Eight wires, values flowing left to right; every X is one butterfly. Press play (arithmetic mod 17, ω = 2), or click any butterfly to inspect it. The partner distance doubles each stage — 1, 2, 4 — the recursion of chapter 5, unrolled one level more and laid out flat. Interactive · the N = 8 butterfly network (mod 17) ▶ play all stages next stage ⏭ reset stage 0 / 3 — values not yet transformed Hover or click a butterfly (the X crossings) to see exactly what it computes. wire (a value flowing right) butterfly value chip (appears as stages complete) Why are the inputs scrambled? The leftmost values are in bit-reversed order (0,4,2,6,1,5,3,7 — index in binary, read backwards). That one upfront shuffle lets every stage read its partners from nearby positions and the outputs arrive in natural order. The repo ships the mirror image: a DIF forward (natural inputs, bit-reversed outputs ) paired with a DIT inverse that consumes exactly that order — so in the real pipeline the two permutations cancel and no bit-reversal pass exists at all ( bitrev.hpp documents the permutation itself). And the negacyclic variant? This network is the plain (\"cyclic\") transform. The negacyclic variant below uses the same pair network with a folded ψ factor on the lower input of each butterfly ( ntt.hpp:87 ). 09 CKKS uses the negacyclic form One loose end from chapter 1: the operation there was \"multiply two lists, get back a list of the same length N .\" But plain multiplication lengthens the list: \\[(2+x)(3+2x)=6+7x+2x^2\\] If every multiplication grew the list, nothing could chain. The fix is one fold rule, in exactly two flavors: \\[ \\begin{aligned} x^N &= +1 && \\text{cyclic: wrap with a plus} \\\\ x^N &= -1 && \\text{negacyclic: wrap with a minus} \\end{aligned} \\] The cyclic fold is precisely the world of chapters 3–7: every ω-wheel point satisfies x^N = ω^N = 1. The negacyclic fold is the one this repo implements. With \\(x^{2} = -1\\) (N = 2, mod 5), the overflow folds back like this: \\[ 6+7x+2x^2 \\;\\longmapsto\\; (6-2)+7x \\;\\equiv\\; 4+2x \\pmod{5} \\] All numbers live mod some prime (here mod 5) — only the remainder after dividing survives, so numbers stay tiny forever. Which points respect the minus fold? Those with \\(x^N = -1\\) — the roots of \\(x^N + 1\\) . They are the odd steps of a doubled wheel: a number ψ with \\(ψ^N = -1\\) closes after 2N points, and \\((ψ^{\\mathrm{odd}})^N = (-1)^{\\mathrm{odd}} = -1\\) . Mod 17, ψ = 6, and \\(ψ^{2} = ω\\) — its even steps are chapter 2's wheel: The doubled wheel · ψ = 6, a 16-th root of unity mod 17 (ψ⁸ = −1, ψ² = ω) even steps = the ω wheel: 1 ψ⁰ 2 ψ² 4 ψ⁴ 8 ψ⁶ 16 ψ⁸ 15 ψ¹⁰ 13 ψ¹² 9 ψ¹⁴ odd steps = negacyclic: 6 ψ¹ 12 ψ³ 7 ψ⁵ 14 ψ⁷ 11 ψ⁹ 5 ψ¹¹ 10 ψ¹³ 3 ψ¹⁵ spot-check one highlighted point: 6⁸ = (6²)⁴ = 2⁴ = 16 ≡ −1 (mod 17) ✓ · chapter 3's t = 2 mod 5 was exactly an odd step of the small doubled wheel there (2² ≡ −1, 2⁴ ≡ 1) The key substitution: pre-twist, then use the ordinary NTT Let \\(ω = ψ^{2}\\) and pre-twist coefficient r as \\(b[r] = a[r]\\,ψ^r\\) . An ordinary cyclic NTT of b then gives \\(\\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} b_r\\omega^{rk} = \\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} a_r\\psi^{(2k+1)r}\\) . In other words, the twist moves evaluation from the even powers of ψ to exactly the odd powers—the roots of x^N+1. The inverse runs the cyclic inverse and multiplies coefficient r by \\(ψ^{-r}\\) . Derive the factors from the first split Choose a primitive 2N-th root ψ in the current prime field, so ψᴺ=−1, and set ω=ψ². For power-of-two N, checking ψᴺ=−1 proves that ψ has order 2N. The prime must satisfy \\(2N\\mid(q-1)\\). OpenFHE compatibility additionally requires the exact same ψ as its tower parameters; another primitive root changes the output order. \\[ F_k=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i\\psi^{(2k+1)i} =\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}(a_i\\psi^i)\\omega^{ki}. \\] Now split input into a low half and a high half, d=N/2. This is the DIF split; the even/odd split above was DIT. The high-half factor contains a sign that depends only on k: \\[ \\begin{aligned} F_k&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\left[a_j+a_{j+d}\\psi^{(2k+1)d}\\right]\\psi^{(2k+1)j},\\\\ \\psi^{(2k+1)d}&=\\psi^d(\\psi^N)^k=(-1)^k\\psi^d. \\end{aligned} \\] Separate the even output indices k=2ℓ and odd indices k=2ℓ+1. Both smaller transforms use root ψ², but the odd branch needs an extra ψ²ʲ: \\[ \\begin{aligned} F_{2\\ell}&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\underbrace{(a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d})}_{x_j}(\\psi^2)^{(2\\ell+1)j},\\\\ F_{2\\ell+1}&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\underbrace{(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\psi^{2j}}_{y_j}(\\psi^2)^{(2\\ell+1)j}. \\end{aligned} \\] The first split gives c=ψᴺᐟ² and w=ψ²ʲ. To derive all later folded factors without guessing a root for each branch, compare with the ordinary cyclic DIF network applied to the pre-twisted coefficients. At a current block of length 2d, let its cyclic values and folded values be related by \\(b_j=\\psi^j a_j\\), where j is local to that block. The cyclic butterfly reads \\(b_{j+d}=\\psi^{j+d}a_{j+d}\\): \\[ \\begin{aligned} b'_j&=b_j+b_{j+d}=\\psi^j(a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d}),\\\\ b'_{j+d}&=(b_j-b_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)} =\\psi^j(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)}. \\end{aligned} \\] Both child blocks now have length d and local index j. Removing their common local twist ψʲ leaves the folded outputs \\(x_j=a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d}\\) and \\(y_j=(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)}\\). The same relation holds for the next split with d halved. Initially it is exactly the pre-twist; at length one j=0, so the remaining twist is 1. This proves every stage and the final result with no extra twist pass. What stays the same The radix-2 network, paired indices, number of stages, and N/2 butterflies per stage do not change. \\[ \\begin{aligned} u' &= u+v \\\\ v' &= (u-v)\\,w \\end{aligned} \\] What negacyclic changes The ψ twist changes the factors carried by the butterfly. This repo folds it into the same pass instead of storing a separate twist table. \\[ \\begin{aligned} u' &= u+cv \\\\ v' &= (u-cv)\\,w \\end{aligned} \\] For the repo's forward DIF loop, a stage pairs entries distance \\(d\\) apart. Its folded factors are: \\[ \\begin{aligned} c_d &= \\psi^d \\\\ w_{d,j} &= \\psi^{\\frac{N}{d}j} = \\omega^{\\frac{N}{2d}j} \\\\[0.5em] \\mathrm{upper}' &= \\mathrm{upper}+c_d\\,\\mathrm{lower} \\\\ \\mathrm{lower}' &= (\\mathrm{upper}-c_d\\,\\mathrm{lower})\\,w_{d,j} \\end{aligned} \\] The group number does not select the twiddle . At a fixed stage, every group repeats the same sequence: j starts at 0, so \\(w = 1\\) , then advances by \\(ψ^{N/d}\\) for each butterfly offset. Negacyclic therefore changes the factors, not the network's pairing pattern. The inverse uses reciprocal ψ factors, reverses the stage direction, and applies the final \\(N^{-1}\\) scale. 10 Map the picture to code Here is the actual loop from this repo's CPU reference ( include/ntt/ntt.hpp , the forward DIF sweep — twist folded in as ladder constants, natural values in, bit-reversed values out). Read the comments with chapter 7 open next to it — every line is a part of the picture: for ( uint64_t stage_pair_distance = n_ / 2 ; stage_pair_distance >= 1 ; stage_pair_distance >>= 1 ) { const uint64_t ladder_twiddle = pow_mod (psi_, stage_pair_distance, q_); // c_d = ψ^d const uint64_t twiddle_step = pow_mod (psi_, n_ / stage_pair_distance, q_); // ψ^(N/d) for ( uint64_t group_start_index = 0 ; group_start_index < n_; group_start_index += 2 * stage_pair_distance) { uint64_t twiddle_power = 1 ; // j = 0 for ( uint64_t pair_offset = 0 ; pair_offset < stage_pair_distance; ++pair_offset) { const uint64_t upper_index = group_start_index + pair_offset; const uint64_t lower_index = upper_index + stage_pair_distance; const uint64_t upper_value = values[upper_index]; const uint64_t scaled_lower_value = mul_mod (ladder_twiddle, values[lower_index], q_); values[upper_index] = add_mod (upper_value, scaled_lower_value, q_); // upper + c_d·lower values[lower_index] = mul_mod ( sub_mod (upper_value, scaled_lower_value, q_), twiddle_power, q_); // (upper − c_d·lower)·w_d,j twiddle_power = mul_mod (twiddle_power, twiddle_step, q_); } } } Three nested loops, one idea: for every butterfly in every stage — u+v, u−v. The CUDA port assigns independent pairs to threads and fuses several stages inside a shared-memory block. At N=65536, one forward transform uses two launches; it does not launch a separate kernel for every stage. Recap The NTT evaluates a polynomial at modular roots of unity. Reusing even/odd subtransforms reduces N² direct products to (N/2)·log₂N butterflies. At N=65,536 that is 524,288 butterflies. CKKS uses odd powers of a primitive 2N-th root, giving multiplication modulo x^N+1. ntt-gpu · docs/butterfly-explained.html mod-5 & mod-17 examples · local KaTeX · view source freely Nov 2026"}, {"title": "From CPU to CUDA", "summary": "Threads, blocks, shared memory, and this repository's two-pass GPU schedule.", "group": "Learn", "url": "gpu-ntt-explained.html", "text": "ntt-gpu · a beginner's field guide to the GPU port From CPU to CUDA. Each GPU thread handles a butterfly pair. Blocks keep small tiles in shared memory so several stages run without repeated VRAM traffic. target: RTX 5090 ring dimension: 65,536 256 threads per block every cook, same step, own ingredient The GPU plan in four steps This is the whole design. The chapters below explain the hardware and show the current code. 1 · SPLIT Give each independent butterfly pair to a GPU thread. 2 · LOAD A block gathers one 256-value group into shared memory. 3 · COMPUTE Run several stages locally, with a barrier between stages. 4 · REGROUP Use a far-pair pass and a near-pair pass to cover the full NTT. Current status: correctness-first baseline. Results must match the OpenFHE-compatible CPU reference exactly. The strided pass is not yet coalesced, so “four logical movements” does not mean four efficient VRAM transactions. Contents 01 CPU vs GPU 02 GPU vocabulary 03 Parallel butterflies 04 Two-pass design 05 Shared-memory tile 06 Code mapping 07 Synchronization 08 Memory cost 09 Current limits 01 CPU and GPU favor different work A CPU is good at a few complicated tasks. A GPU is good at many copies of the same small task. A GPU schedules groups of 32 threads called warps . Threads in a warp normally execute the same instruction on different data; different warps can make progress independently. Branch divergence can reduce efficiency within a warp. At each NTT stage, disjoint pairs use the same butterfly formula. Assigning these pairs to threads exposes parallel work without changing the result. 02 Six GPU terms These six terms connect the GPU model to this repository. Word Plain meaning In this repo thread One execution instance. It computes assigned pairs; it does not permanently own a coefficient. we use T = 256 per block block A team of threads sharing memory and block barriers. one block owns one tile grid All blocks in a launch. The x and y coordinates identify independent work. x = tile index, y = RNS limb shared memory Fast storage shared by the threads of one block. 256 values = 2 KB per limb global memory Device-wide memory holding coefficients, moduli, and twiddle tables. the whole polynomial, all limbs kernel A function launched across a grid of threads. six kernels in ntt_kernels.cu A barrier ( __syncthreads() in code) is the station's roll call: every cook in the block must arrive before anyone leaves. Chapter 7 is about the one way to misuse it. how the pieces nest · one picture HOST your Mac / the CLI starts the kernel launch GPU — one chip GLOBAL MEMORY (VRAM) — the warehouse · shared by every block BLOCK (x=0, y=1) its counter (shared) BLOCK (x=1, y=1) its counter (shared) BLOCK (x=2, y=1) its counter (shared) every block loads its tile from VRAM, computes on its counter, writes back — blocks never see each other's counters thread (a cook) → inside a block (a station, with its private counter) → blocks tile the grid (x = tile, y = limb) → all inside one GPU around one warehouse Optional: RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 capacity estimates the picture, counted · for both rentable cards RTX 4090 RTX 5090 station groups (SMs) 128 170 cooks in flight (resident threads) 196,608 261,120 our 256-cook stations, resident at once 768 1,020 max cooks per block / per station 1,024 / 1,536 1,024 / 1,536 counter (shared) per block · per station 48 KB · ~100 KB 48 KB · ~100 KB warehouse (VRAM) 24 GB GDDR6X 32 GB GDDR7 pipe into the chip — VRAM bandwidth ≈ 1.0 TB/s ≈ 1.8 TB/s traffic inside the chip — shared, aggregate ≈ 40 TB/s ≈ 50 TB/s The design fact hiding in the last two rows: on-chip traffic is 40–50× the pipe into the chip — the entire reason the communication ladder (chapter 5) exists. Aggregate shared figures are order-of-magnitude estimates (per-SM path × SMs × boost clock); the ratio is the point, not the decimals. // the first rows are one multiplication chain — the table explains itself: 4090: 128 SMs × 1,536 threads/SM = 196,608 in flight ÷ 256 (our T) = 768 of our blocks resident 5090: 170 SMs × 1,536 = 261,120 ÷ 256 = 1,020 These are thread-limit ceilings, not measured occupancy. Registers, shared memory, and block limits can reduce residency. At 40 limbs each pass launches 10,240 blocks; the scheduler runs them as resources become available. At N = 65,536 with 3 limbs, each pass launches 256 × 3 = 768 blocks . How many stay resident at once depends on threads, registers, shared memory, and the device's block limits; measure it instead of assuming one wave. 03 One stage has independent pairs Recall the NTT's shape: at every stage, the list is chopped into pairs, and each pair goes through one butterfly. At N = 16, one stage has eight disjoint pairs. Follow both the indices and the values: Follow all four stages at N=16 below. Every stage has eight independent pairs; stage boundaries carry dependencies. The two worked groups show how the first two stages stay inside strided columns and the last two stay inside contiguous rows. Earlier 8-point pairing animation one stage at N = 8 · click a stage stage d = 4 stage d = 2 stage d = 1 pick 0,2,4,6 → 2 stages Every element belongs to exactly one pair per stage — the four butterflies share nothing . Four cooks, zero talking, any order. The fourth button is pass 1 of the real port in miniature (chapter 4). That \"share nothing\" is the entire secret. Because the pairs are disjoint , the four butterflies of a stage can run simultaneously, interleaved, in any order, on any cook — the final list is identical every time . A GPU stage is just: hand each pair to a cook, let them all fly at once, wait for the slowest, next stage. Disjointness also buys locality : a stage with distance d only ever pairs values inside one 2d-wide group, so a group of 2 k values owns its k smallest stages outright — the fact chapter 4 turns into the two-pass split, taken once with strided groups and once with contiguous tiles. Hold onto that sentence — it is also why a plain CPU simulator, running the same stage bodies serially, can prove the whole schedule before any GPU is rented. 04 Split the NTT into two passes So why not one giant block that does the whole transform? Size — a block's counter is capped at 48 KB, and the data is far bigger: where \"1.5 MB\" comes from · why the data can't sit on one counter 1 value (one coefficient, one limb) = 64 bits = 8 bytes one limb = 2¹⁶ values × 8 bytes = 524,288 B = 512 KB this repo = 3 limbs × 512 KB = 1,536 KB ≈ 1.5 MB real FHE = ~40 limbs × 512 KB ≈ 20 MB = 427× a block's whole 48 KB counter More limbs ≠ harder: each limb is one independent column of the grid (the y address) doing the identical dance — real coefficients are thousands of bits, kept as ~40 machine-word residues, one transform each. So how should a GPU run this? Walk the same road the port had to walk — four steps, each one forced by the step before: ① How the CPU does it — and where it hurts The reference ( ntt.hpp ) is one cook walking the list in order: stage by stage, pair by pair. At N = 8 that's four butterflies, one after another — even though chapter 3 proved they're independent. The picture below is the whole problem in miniature: same four butterflies, four units of wall time versus one . Fine at lecture size — but a real forward is 32,768 × 16 ≈ 524,288 butterflies per limb , ~21 million across 40 limbs. One cook at a few GHz drowns; that's the wall. ② First GPU idea: every pair on a cook, everything from VRAM Hand each pair of each stage to its own cook, all cooks at once, everyone reading and writing the warehouse directly. This works — and it is what a naive port does for every stage. But now count truck trips: every stage fetches its partners from VRAM and writes them back — one round-trip per stage per value, and there are 16 stages. The cooks spend their day at the loading dock (chapter 8's ladder: warehouse ≈ ×400 the counter). Both modular arithmetic and memory traffic require profiling. ③ The hardware rule that shapes the fix — and the one choice we get to make The fast counter is private to a block — 256 cooks sharing 48 KB — and nobody outside the block can touch it. So a stage can escape the warehouse on one condition: all its pairs must sit inside data one block can gather onto its counter . The group size is ours to choose; the port picks 256 : counter budget: 48 KB ÷ 8 bytes per value = up to 6,144 values per group choose: 256 values (= 2 KB of the 48) — and 65,536 = 256 × 256 Why 256 exactly? It must be a power of two , so groups always contain whole pair-groups with nothing straddling an edge. And at N = 65,536 it makes the bookkeeping symmetric: 256 groups of 256 values , twice over — once strided, once contiguous (rung ④). Each block requests 2 KiB of shared memory; actual residency also depends on registers and hardware limits. one block's counter budget: 48 KB 2 KB spare — 46 KB of the allowance unused one SM station — neighbors bring their own tiles: 2 KB group block A 2 KB group block B 2 KB group block C ④ That number is the pair distance d Look at a pair's addresses when the list is viewed as a 256×256 grid (row = 256q + r): partners of a far stage (d a multiple of 256) have the same r — same column, different row — so each strided group {r, r+256, r+512, …} owns all far stages. Partners of a near stage (d ≤ 128) sit in the same row — a contiguous 256-tile owns all near stages. Two groupings, no third case. Stated cleanly, the four steps compress into one rule — the one structural idea of this port: the two-pass split · groups of 256, twice PASS 1 — the strided groups (d = 32,768 … 256) → 256 groups {r, r+256, …} each gather to a counter, run 8 stages, scatter PASS 2 — the contiguous tiles (d = 128 … 1) → 256 tiles of 256 each gather, run 8 stages, scatter Both passes run the same butterflies as the CPU reference. Pass 1 handles far pairs; pass 2 handles near pairs. This is four logical value movements, but pass 1 currently uses strided, uncoalesced addresses, so the hardware may issue many more memory transactions. 05 Keep a group in shared memory Global memory is the warehouse; shared memory is the counter. A shared-phase block does exactly four things: one block, one group · the kernel's whole life VRAM (global) the polynomial BLOCK — 256 cooks x = group id · y = limb 0..2 the block's COUNTER — 2 KB 256 values · 1 fetch in + 1 write out, total 1 2 4 3 8 stages · d = 128…1 · barrier each ① 256 cooks gather 256 values (1 each) · ② roll call — values land on the counter · ③ eight butterfly stages, barrier between · ④ carry the results back Naive code reads and writes VRAM at every stage. The two-pass design loads and stores each group once per pass. That reduces logical traffic, but pass 1 still needs a coalesced transpose or Four-Step layout to make those accesses efficient. A 256-value group uses 2 KB of shared memory per limb, leaving room for several blocks to reside on an SM. Actual occupancy on the RTX 5090 must be measured after register use and launch limits are known. The same trick, taken twice Notice what the tile trick actually was: a group of 2^k values hosts the k smallest-distance stages with zero outside communication . The port takes this trick twice — once with strided groups, once with contiguous ones — and that is the whole two-pass split: one forward at N = 65,536 · two passes, four truck trips PASS 1 strided groups {r, r+256, …} × 256 groups × 3 limbs 8 stages d = 32,768…256 8 stages on its counter 8 stages on its counter VRAM — the warehouse · the mix: pass 1 scatters strided, pass 2 gathers contiguous 8 stages d = 128…1 8 stages on its counter 8 stages on its counter PASS 2 contiguous tiles 256q … 256q+255 × 256 tiles × 3 limbs 1 2 3 4 per value: ① fetch + ② write-back + ③ fetch + ④ write-back = 4 warehouse trips — versus 16 naive Two things this shape fixes at once. The order differs from stage order only in grouping — the forward runs pass 1 then pass 2, the inverse runs the same ladder backwards. And every launch is self-contained: two kernel launches per forward (one per pass), each block needing nothing but its own 256 values. 06 Map the design to code Everything above exists in the repo in three small pieces. First, the butterfly body itself — written once , in include/ntt/dev_core.hpp , with the loop arranged as \"one cook's slice of the pairs\": // one DIF stage, one thread's slice — the same body the CPU simulator runs NTT_DEV void dev_dif_stage (uint64_t* tile, uint64_t tile_len, uint64_t d, uint64_t n, const uint64_t* pp, uint64_t q, uint32_t tid, uint32_t nthr) { const uint64_t lg = log2_of_pow2(d); for (uint64_t p = tid; p < tile_len / 2 ; p += nthr) { // MY pairs: start at my // thread id, stride by the thread count — chapter 3's disjoint pairs const uint64_t g = p >> lg, j = p & (d - 1 ); // which group, which offset const uint64_t u = (g << (lg + 1 )) + j, v = u + d; // the pair's two slots const uint64_t x = tile[u]; const uint64_t cv = mul_mod(pp[d], tile[v], q); // ladder constant ψ^d tile[u] = add_mod(x, cv, q); tile[v] = mul_mod(sub_mod(x, cv, q), pp[(n >> lg) * j], q); // twiddle } } Second, the pass-1 kernel — pure choreography around the stage bodies, in src/cuda/ntt_kernels.cu . One block owns one strided group, start to finish: __global__ void dif_col_kernel (uint64_t* a, const uint64_t* pp, const uint64_t* q, uint64_t n, uint32_t sub) { extern __shared__ uint64_t smem[]; // the counter const uint64_t r = blockIdx.x; // x = which strided group uint64_t* al = a + ( uint64_t )blockIdx.y * n; // y = which limb for (uint32_t t = threadIdx.x; t < sub; t += blockDim.x) // ① gather: value r+256t, smem[t] = al[r + ( uint64_t (t) << 8 )]; // one per cook __syncthreads(); // ② roll call for (uint64_t delta = sub / 2 ; delta >= 1 ; delta >>= 1 ) { // ③ the 8 far stages, dev_dif_col_stage(smem, sub, delta, n, pp, q, threadIdx.x, blockDim.x, r); __syncthreads(); // barrier each } for (uint32_t t = threadIdx.x; t < sub; t += blockDim.x) // ④ scatter back strided al[r + ( uint64_t (t) << 8 )] = smem[t]; } The pass-2 kernel ( dif_tile_kernel ) is the same shape with a contiguous gather — it is this kernel with r + (t<<8) replaced by base + t , running the near stages with dev_dif_stage . Third, the launches — where the grid's two addresses become the map of the whole problem. Remember RNS from the butterfly guide: the same transform runs once per limb (per prime), completely independently, so the y address is the limb: // one forward = TWO launches (grid = x × limbs): dif_col_kernel<<< dim3 ( 256 , num_limbs), T, sub * sizeof (uint64_t)>>>(buf, d_pp, d_q, n, sub); dif_tile_kernel<<< dim3 (n/ 256 , num_limbs), T, 256 * sizeof (uint64_t)>>>(buf, d_pp, d_q, n, 256 ); // pass 1: block (x=r, y=k) = strided group r of limb k — owns {r, r+256, …} // pass 2: block (x=q, y=k) = contiguous tile q of limb k — owns 256q … 256q+255 Three limbs produce 768 blocks per pass. The y grid coordinate batches limbs in one launch; hardware resource limits determine how many blocks run at once. And the communication between stages — the part that makes or breaks a GPU program — is different in each phase, by design: ⇄ Between the two passes: the mix is pure data movement Pass 2's inputs are pass 1's outputs, regrouped — strided scatter becomes contiguous gather. No thread waits on another explicitly: the two launches share one stream, and pass 2 cannot begin until pass 1's writes have landed in VRAM. The pass boundary and the kernel boundary are the same line. ⇄ Between stages inside a pass: the barrier, because partners swap On the counter, the values your thread reads at stage d were written at stage 2d by different threads of your block — the crossing re-shuffles ownership every stage. That is the entire reason for __syncthreads() after each stage: all 256 cooks finish, then anyone may read. ∅ Between blocks: silence — and that is the design Inside either pass, every block's 256 values are a self-contained problem — a strided group for pass 1, a tile for pass 2 — and no pair ever leaves them. With three limbs, each pass has 768 independent block-local stage groups, which is why one launch finishes eight whole stages , and why the GPU can pack blocks onto SMs in any arrangement it likes. 07 Synchronize between stages Two GPU habits bite newcomers, and both are already handled in the kernel — know them when you read it. the roll call rule · why barriers sit between stages the barrier — nobody passes alone cook A — done early cook B — working cook C — nearly there cook D — the slowest waiting… still working all cross together — when D arrives time → a block is exactly as fast as its slowest thread — so keep everyone's work the same shape Rule 1: the barrier is all-or-nothing Every thread of a block must reach every __syncthreads() . Put one inside an if that only some threads take, and the others wait forever — a hung kernel. That is why our barriers sit between stage loops, never inside the pair loop: all 256 cooks finish their slice, then everyone crosses together. Rule 2: idle cooks still attend roll call At N = 64 the tile is 64 values — only 32 pairs — but the block still has 256 threads. Most cooks hold no pair. Their loop body simply does zero iterations ( for (p = tid; p < 32; p += 256) never starts), and they walk straight to the barrier. Harmless — as long as they don't skip it . The repo's n=64 test fixture exists precisely to keep this edge honest. 08 Memory movement sets the pace The whole design — tiles, phases, staging — is really one decision: which shelf does each number live on? The shelves differ by orders of magnitude: cost to fetch one value · relative, order-of-magnitude Log-scale bars — each gridline is ×10. Exact cycles vary by GPU generation; the gaps are what matter. In kitchen terms: registers are in your hands, shared is on your station's counter, global is the warehouse across town, and copying from the host computer is a delivery truck. Our kernel's shape follows directly: start and end in the warehouse, stage the tile on the counter, keep loop counters and twiddles in hands — and never, ever truck data back and forth per stage. This ladder is also why the multiply pipeline stays on the GPU end to end: two forwards, pointwise, one inverse, then one truck trip home. 09 Current baseline and next optimizations Everything above is proven before any rental: the stage bodies live once in dev_core.hpp , compiled by a CPU simulator that must match the reference exactly — the rented GPU's gates then check only the thin kernel wrappers. One confession before you read the benchmarks: version 1 of the GPU arithmetic is deliberately unoptimized. The multiply inside every butterfly uses the same __uint128_t division as the CPU reference — because on the GPU that division is emulated : the hardware has no 128-bit divide, so the compiler builds one from many instructions. Every butterfly pays it. That is the right order of operations: make it right, measure the gap, then make it fast . The bench table's job is to quantify exactly how slow v1 is — the CPU reference column is right there in the same table. The fix (Barrett or Montgomery reduction: division-free modular multiplication, the standard trick every production NTT uses) swaps in later behind the same function signature, with its own dedicated cross-check. The first RTX 5090 run answers one question: does N=65536 match the reference exactly across the target modulus widths? Then benchmarks should isolate modular reduction, coalescing, radix, register pressure, and end-to-end transfer cost. Recap Each thread computes butterfly pairs. Each block loads one group into shared memory, runs several stages with barriers, and writes the group back. A far-pair pass followed by a near-pair pass covers the full transform; x identifies the group and y identifies the RNS limb . The CPU simulator proves the schedule before GPU testing. The current baseline favors exact results over speed; optimization comes next through division-free modular reduction, coalesced data layout, higher radix, and device-resident pipelines. Start here if you haven't: Butterflies all the way down — why the NTT is butterflies at all."}, {"title": "65536: work distribution", "summary": "Explore the 65536-point forward/inverse polynomial, two-pass blocks, threads, and global access patterns.", "group": "Learn", "url": "ntt-65536-work-distribution.html", "text": "CUDA execution · a worked example How 65,536 coefficients map to GPU threads. See how our 65536-point forward and inverse NTTs distribute data and butterflies. Start with what a block owns, then zoom into what a thread does. Polynomial → Pass → Block → Thread The whole job The two passes Resources & memory access Forward proof Inverse proof · D3 Row kernels & coverage · D4 Explore a thread Synchronization & RNS 01 Start with the data Consider one polynomial modulo one prime: a[0], a[1], …, a[65535] . Each residue uses 8 bytes, so one RNS limb occupies 512 KiB. 65536 coefficients per modulus 16 stages · 32768 butterflies each 524,288 butterflies · two kernel launches Imagine the same array as 256 rows × 256 columns . This is an index view; the stored array stays flat. global_index = row × 256 + column 02 Divide the work by distance Pass 1 · far stages One block owns one column 32768 → 16384 → 8192 → 4096 → 2048 → 1024 → 512 → 256 These distances preserve the index modulo 256. Each block gathers 256 strided values into shared memory, executes eight stages, and scatters the results back. 256 blocks per modulus. Every block launches 256 threads: 256 load/store values; 128 compute butterflies at each stage. 256 values · 2 KiB shared memory / block Pass 2 · near stages One block owns one row 128 → 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1 These butterflies stay inside an aligned 256-value row. Each block loads its contiguous row into shared memory, executes eight stages, and writes it back. 256 blocks per modulus. All 256 threads load/store values; 128 compute butterflies at each stage. 256 values · 2 KiB shared memory / block Both passes use twiddles from the same 65536-point transform . Threads 128–255 still load, store, and reach every barrier, even when they have no butterfly. 03 Resources & memory access The split width is 256: 256 rows × 256 columns . Each column and each row contains 256 values. The algorithm uses two launches, each handling eight stages. Forward schedule per modulus · all blocks launch 256 threads Task / resource N = 65536 Polynomial storage 512 KiB Stages / total butterflies 16 / 524,288 Pass 1 blocks / values per block 256 / 256 Pass 1 shared memory per block 2 KiB Pass 1 loading / butterfly threads 0–255 / 0–127 Pass 1 global distances 32768 down to 256 · 8 stages Pass 2 blocks / values per block 256 / 256 Pass 2 shared memory per block 2 KiB Pass 2 loading / butterfly threads 0–255 / 0–127 Pass 2 global distances 128 down to 1 · 8 stages Pass 1: column 3, thread 5 Block 3 gathers values[3 + 256 × t] into shared[t] , for t = 0…255. Thread 5 loads position 1283. At global distance 32768 (local distance 128), it computes shared positions 5 and 133: global positions 1283 and 34051 . At distance 256 it computes 2563 and 2819 instead. Pass 2: row 3, thread 5 Block 3 loads positions 768…1023. At distance 128, thread 5 computes 773 and 901 ; at distance 1, it computes 778 and 779. The final row, block 255, owns positions 65280…65535. Read the memory access map In pass 1, adjacent loading threads access global addresses 2048 bytes apart (256 × 8 bytes), but write adjacent 8-byte entries in shared memory. The scatter reverses this mapping. In pass 2, adjacent threads read and write adjacent 8-byte global entries. Each block loads once, reuses shared memory across all its stages, and stores once. Two reads and two writes of the value array give a logical minimum of 4 × N × 8 bytes per forward transform: 2 MiB at N=65536, excluding twiddle/modulus reads and host transfers. This is requested array traffic, not measured DRAM traffic . Strided accesses, caches, registers, and occupancy need real-GPU profiling; 128 active butterfly threads out of 256 is not a hardware occupancy measurement. Source map: CUDA kernels and launchers define the grid and gather/scatter; stage helpers map threads to pairs. For N=65536, the gather uses r + 256 × local_index ; near-stage access uses row × 256 + local_index . Within each stage, every position belongs to exactly one butterfly. 04 · D2 Why these inputs and twiddles? Our forward butterfly has two multipliers . Let ψ be the primitive 2N-th root modulo the current prime q. The ladder multiplier is \\(c=\\psi^d\\); the difference-leg multiplier is \\(w=\\psi^E\\). Both are powers from the global transform, not a smaller column NTT. \\[a=\\mathrm{values}[U],\\quad b=\\mathrm{values}[V],\\qquad x=a+cb,\\quad y=(a-cb)w\\pmod q.\\] The folded twist remains inside every butterfly a b × c × w x y a + cb a − cb \\[c=\\psi^d,\\quad w=\\psi^{(N/d)J},\\qquad x=a+cb,\\quad y=(a-cb)w\\pmod q.\\] At N=65536, the first column stage has d=32768 and c=ψ³²⁷⁶⁸. At the last column stage d=256, c=ψ²⁵⁶. The row pass continues the same transform with d=128 down to 1. It does not reset ψ or N. How the last two row stages finish the transform Stage (zero-based) d / offset J Factors Outputs 14 2 / 0 or 1 c=ψ²; w=1 or ψ³²⁷⁶⁸ Two size-2 children per size-4 group 15 1 / 0 only c=ψ; w=1 Upper=a+ψb; lower=a−ψb The last layer has no difference-leg multiply by a nontrivial w, but the lower input still carries ψ . Removing c would change the transform to the wrong ring. Final outputs are already in bit-reversed evaluation order. The derivation and full numerical network explain how the original coefficient twist is distributed through these stages. 1. Decode a thread into a local pair The split width B is 256. Column r is blockIdx.x ; p is the butterfly index. At N=65536, each active thread processes just one pair per stage, so p = threadIdx.x . More generally the stage helper loops over p = threadIdx.x + k × blockDim.x . Let δ be the local distance; the global distance is d = B × δ . \\[\\begin{aligned}g&=\\lfloor p/\\delta\\rfloor,&j&=p\\bmod\\delta,\\\\u_{\\rm local}&=2\\delta g+j,&v_{\\rm local}&=u_{\\rm local}+\\delta,\\\\U&=B u_{\\rm local}+r,&V&=Bv_{\\rm local}+r=U+d.\\end{aligned}\\] 2. Recover the CPU group and offset Substitute the local pair into the global index: \\[\\begin{aligned}U&=B(2\\delta g+j)+r\\\\&=2dg+(Bj+r),\\\\\\text{group start}&=2dg,\\qquad J=Bj+r.\\end{aligned}\\] Because 0 ≤ j < δ and 0 ≤ r < B , we have 0 ≤ J < Bδ = d . Thus U and V are exactly the CPU pair at offset J in group g. Conversely, every CPU offset has a unique decomposition J = 256j + r . Together with p = gδ + j , this gives one unique column/thread owner for every CPU butterfly. 3. Prove the exponent, not just the result The CPU starts w at 1 for each global group and multiplies it by \\(\\psi^{N/d}\\) after each offset. At offset J it therefore uses \\(w=\\psi^{(N/d)J}\\). Substitution gives: \\[c=\\psi^d,\\qquad E=\\frac Nd(256j+r),\\qquad w=\\psi^E.\\] This is precisely the stage helper: if ℓ = log2(δ) , then δ << 8 = d , N >> (8 + ℓ) = N/d , and (j << 8) | r = 256j + r . The OR equals addition because r occupies only the low eight bits. Also 0 ≤ E ≤ N − N/d < N , so the power-table access is in range. A subtle point: thread 0 in column 3 has J = 3, not 0. Its w is generally not 1. Only J = 0 gives exponent 0. The ladder c and w are distinct: the negacyclic ladder remains even when w = 1. 4. Worked pairs at N=65536 All examples use column r = 3. The ladder exponent is d; E is the exponent for the other multiplier. These are indices/exponents, not numeric residues. First, intermediate, and final column stages N d / δ p g / j U ↔ V J E 65536 32768 / 128 5 0 / 5 1283 ↔ 34051 1283 2566 65536 2048 / 8 21 2 / 5 9475 ↔ 11523 1283 41056 65536 256 / 1 5 5 / 0 2563 ↔ 2819 3 768 5. An exhaustive executable check column_mapping_tests.cpp enumerates every CPU group/offset in every column stage at N=65536. It executes the actual dev_dif_col_stage body with synthetic tagged power tables. An upper-input probe reveals the selected w exponent; a lower-input probe checks the ladder and both destinations. Whole-column comparisons catch unintended writes. The test also checks unique coverage and every inactive thread. There are 262,144 column butterflies at N=65536: 262,144 pairs and 524,288 active-pair probes . Tagged tables are diagnostic values, not valid root powers. The separate schedule gate checks real-root forward arrays, round trips, and multiplication. Both checks run on CPU; neither validates CUDA launch behavior or GPU races. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build --target ntt_column_mapping_tests ntt_device_sim ctest --test-dir build -R 'column_mapping_check|device_schedule_check' --output-on-failure 05 · D3 Undo the forward butterfly The inverse-column helper dev_dit_col_stage uses the same pair mapping as D2. Only the arithmetic and stage order change. It reads an inverse-power table: entry e is (ψ⁻¹)^e . The exponents d and E stay positive; we do not use negative table indices. Forward · DIF \\[\\begin{aligned}c&=\\psi^d,\\quad w=\\psi^E,\\\\x&=a+cb,\\\\y&=(a-cb)w.\\end{aligned}\\] Inverse · DIT (unscaled) \\[\\begin{aligned}t&=yw^{-1},\\\\a^{\\prime}&=x+t,\\\\b^{\\prime}&=(x-t)c^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\\] All operations are modulo the limb prime q. Substitute the forward outputs into the inverse: \\[\\begin{aligned}t&=(a-cb)ww^{-1}=a-cb,\\\\a^{\\prime}&=(a+cb)+(a-cb)=2a,\\\\b^{\\prime}&=((a+cb)-(a-cb))c^{-1}=2b.\\end{aligned}\\] So the inverse first untwiddles the difference leg by w⁻¹, then forms the sum/difference, then multiplies the lower result by c⁻¹. Swapping those multipliers or omitting either one is incorrect. To recover a and b from just one butterfly, divide both results by 2 modulo q. Why scale only once? Every coefficient traverses log₂N butterflies. Each reversed stage contributes a factor of 2. These factors commute with every stage because the operations are linear modulo q. Thus the entire unnormalized inverse of the forward transform gives 2^(log₂N) × input = N × input . recovered[i] = unnormalized_inverse[i] × N⁻¹ mod q The modulus q is an odd prime and N is a power of two, so N has an inverse modulo q. Our code applies inverse_scale_kernel once at the end, using the inverse length for the selected RNS limb—not floating-point division and not a separate coefficient twist. Reverse the execution, not the array Forward column distances decrease, then forward row distances decrease. The inverse reverses that sequence: row distances increase, then column distances increase, then normalization. Gather/scatter addresses, grid sizes, shared-memory sizes, and active-thread ranges are unchanged for each data subset. Execution order per modulus · the inverse has three launches N Forward (2 launches) Inverse (3 launches) 65536 columns: 32768…256 ↓ rows: 128…1 ↓ rows: 1…128 ↑ columns: 256…32768 ↑ scale by 65536⁻¹ Forward input is in natural coefficient order; its output is bit-reversed evaluation order. The inverse consumes that order directly and returns natural coefficient order. No extra bit-reversal pass is needed. Scale uses a flat grid over all limb coefficients; its block count is ceil(limb_count × N / 256). Worked column pairs: r = 3, thread p = 5 The first inverse-column stage follows the eight row stages N / inverse stage d U ↔ V c⁻¹ w⁻¹ 65536 / 9 of 16 256 2563 ↔ 2819 ψ^−256 ψ^−768 65536 / 16 of 16 32768 1283 ↔ 34051 ψ^−32768 ψ^−2566 A small numeric butterfly For arithmetic only, take N=8, q=17, ψ=3, d=2, J=1: E=4, c=9, w=13. Let a=4 and b=5. Forward gives x=15, y=11 . Since w⁻¹=4 and c⁻¹=2, inverse gives t=10, a′=8, b′=10 , exactly 2a and 2b modulo 17. Multiplying by 2⁻¹=9 recovers 4 and 5. This toy example illustrates one butterfly; it is not a column launch for N=8. What the D3 checks establish inverse_column_check executes the real inverse-column helper on CPU. It checks every column pair at N=65536 with tagged tables (262,144 pairs, two basis-input probes each), and every inactive thread. With valid roots for two 46-bit primes, it checks DIT(DIF(stage)) = 2 × input at every column stage and column. A full near→far inverse fed by the independent CPU forward transform must equal N × input before scaling and the original array after scaling. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build --target ntt_column_mapping_tests ntt_device_sim ctest --test-dir build -R 'column_mapping_check|inverse_column_check|device_schedule_check' --output-on-failure These checks establish the shared stage arithmetic and CPU-reproduced ordering. They do not compile CUDA, exercise actual launch/barrier behavior, or measure GPU performance. The D4 section below examines contiguous kernels and stage coverage in detail. 06 · D4 Why two passes equal the full transform The column proof handles far stages. The remaining kernels— forward_contiguous_tile_kernel and inverse_contiguous_tile_kernel —assign one contiguous tile to each block. For our target sizes, tile length L is 256; for smaller transforms it is min(256,N) . 1. Map a row thread to the CPU pair Let b be the row block, p the pair index, and d a near-stage distance (128 down to 1 for forward, 1 up to 128 for inverse). Each active thread has p = threadIdx.x for these tiles. \\[\\begin{aligned}g&=\\lfloor p/d\\rfloor,\\quad j=p\\bmod d,\\\\u_{\\rm local}&=2dg+j,\\quad v_{\\rm local}=u_{\\rm local}+d,\\\\U&=Lb+u_{\\rm local},\\quad V=U+d,\\\\\\text{group start}&=Lb+2dg,\\quad J=j,\\quad E=(N/d)j.\\end{aligned}\\] Why is this a valid CPU group? Since d is a power of two and 2d ≤ L , 2d divides L . Therefore Lb + 2dg is a multiple of 2d, exactly where the CPU group loop begins. Also 0 ≤ j < d and the final local lower index is at most L−1. Every CPU group fits entirely inside an aligned tile. Notice what disappears: the row number b does not appear in J or E. Moving to another aligned row changes the group number, not the offset within that group. But N still appears: these are global-transform powers, not the twiddles of an independent 256-point NTT. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Forward: }&c=\\psi^d,\\quad w=\\psi^{(N/d)j},\\\\\\text{Inverse: }&c^{-1}=(\\psi^{-1})^d,\\quad w^{-1}=(\\psi^{-1})^{(N/d)j}.\\end{aligned}\\] 2. Trace row 3, thread 5 Row indices and global twiddle exponents at N=65536 d g / j U ↔ V E at N=65536 128 0 / 5 773 ↔ 901 2560 8 0 / 5 773 ↔ 781 40960 1 5 / 0 778 ↔ 779 0 At N=65536, row 255/thread 127/distance 128 reaches the final coefficient: U=65407, V=65535, J=127, E=65024 . Inverse uses the same pairs and reciprocal powers, visited in ascending d order. 3. Account for every stage exactly once The CPU distances are precisely the powers of two from N/2 down to 1. At N≥512, they partition into two disjoint sets: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Far: }&d=32768,16384,\\ldots,256,\\\\\\text{Near: }&d=128,64,\\ldots,1.\\end{aligned}\\] Forward runs far distances descending, then near distances descending. Inverse reverses both passes and scales by the inverse of 65536. For other supported N≥512, far distances begin at N/2. There is no missing distance between 128 and 256: no power of two lies between them. Neither set overlaps the other. The far count is log₂N − 8 ; the near count is 8. Each stage has exactly N/2 disjoint butterflies, so the full count is (N/2) × log₂N per direction. Scaling is not an additional butterfly stage. N=65536 · forward and inverse share these stage counts N Far + near stages Total butterflies / direction 65536 8 + 8 = 16 524,288 At N≤256, the far pass is skipped and one tile covers all log₂N stages. The coverage check also exercises N=2, 64, 256 and 512, including the tile clamp and first column-pass boundary. 4. Why block-local stage fusion is safe During all far stages, every dependency stays inside the same column; during all near stages, every dependency stays inside the same tile. A block may therefore finish all its stages before another block finishes its first: those blocks do not consume each other's intermediate values within that pass. This reorders independent operations without changing the CPU result. Each tile loads values[Lb+t] into shared[t] . All threads reach the load barrier and every stage barrier. Threads 0…127 compute butterflies at L=256, while all 256 threads participate in loading/storing and synchronization. The block stores its tile once after the last stage. Within a stage, pair endpoints do not overlap; between stages, the shared-memory barrier makes the preceding writes visible. The dependency rule changes between passes. Results must be scattered before the next pass gathers them; our same-stream kernel sequence provides that boundary. Thus gather/scatter is a change in block ownership, not a transpose or extra arithmetic transform . Together with D2/D3's identical pair arithmetic, exact ordered stage coverage establishes equivalence to the CPU transform. 5. Executable evidence tile_mapping_check runs the actual row-stage helpers on CPU, comparing against independent CPU group/offset enumeration. At N=65536 it checks every row, stage and pair in both directions, using two tagged-table input probes per pair, plus inactive-thread checks. That is 262,144 pairs per direction and 1,048,576 active probes in total . The same test checks forward and inverse stage lists and per-stage global endpoint coverage at N=65536, plus the small-size boundaries. Existing real-root schedule and inverse-normalization gates remain complementary checks. This is not a CUDA compile, barrier/race check, or GPU performance measurement. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure 07 Follow one thread An active thread computes one butterfly per stage and writes both results to shared memory. Its pair changes with the stage. Loading a value and computing a butterfly are separate assignments; a thread does not permanently own one coefficient. Keep block 3 and thread 5. Try distances 32768, 128, then 1. Try thread 200 to see the difference between load/store work and butterfly work. Polynomial size · one RNS limb N = 65536 Transform direction Forward · DIF Inverse · DIT Pass 1 · column blocks · butterfly distance Pass 2 · row blocks · butterfly distance Block index Thread index Global array positions · 256 rows × 256 columns Block-owned positions Upper input Lower input The matrix shows positions, not coefficient values . Contents change during the transform; the index layout stays the same. 08 What keeps the work ordered? Inside a block All 256 threads reach __syncthreads() after loading and after each stage—even threads without a butterfly. This barrier synchronizes only that block. Between the passes The kernels use the same CUDA stream, so pass 2 starts after pass 1 finishes. Blocks within a pass are independent; SM scheduling does not guarantee they all execute simultaneously. For CKKS with multiple RNS primes, repeat this arrangement per limb. Storage is limb-major: values[limb × N + index] . blockIdx.y selects the limb; blockIdx.x selects the column in pass 1 or the row in pass 2. The mental model: a block owns a data subset; a thread computes a butterfly within it at each stage. Next, follow the constructor, buffers, and host/device dataflow (D5) ."}, {"title": "CUDA buffers & dataflow", "summary": "D5: constructor lifecycle, buffer ownership, host/device copies, fused operations, and integration gaps.", "group": "Learn", "url": "cuda-buffer-dataflow.html", "text": "CUDA buffers and host/device dataflow — D5 ¶ This is a map of the current implementation, not a proposed device-resident API. Read it alongside the public interface , implementation and launchers , and kernel schedule . Updated after offline GPU preparation : ownership, validation and device guards now compile with nvcc; real GPU behavior is pending. The big picture: N=65536, three towers ¶ One CudaNttTransform represents one fixed ring size and a fixed list of RNS moduli/roots. It owns two reusable operand buffers and four constant buffers. The public interface accepts host vectors , not device pointers. For this page, N=65536 and K=3 . One polynomial contains 196,608 64-bit residues: 1,572,864 bytes (1.5 MiB) . Each tower occupies 512 KiB. ① Construct once Allocate six GPU buffers: 6,291,552 B total. Upload four constant arrays: 3,145,824 B, four copies. ② Reuse for each call Host inputs → GPU A/B → kernels → host result. A and B are each 1.5 MiB. Constants stay on the GPU. ③ Destroy Free all six allocations after successful construction. CPU vectors and GPU allocations have separate ownership. Constants and scratch allocations persist across calls. Operand contents are scratch state and are overwritten by the next call. Only the internal chain inside multiply() remains device-resident between its arithmetic operations. Calling public forward() , pointwise_multiply() , and inverse() separately does not preserve a transfer-free pipeline. Constructor: what happens, in order ¶ Create Impl with make_unique ; all six CUDA pointers start as null and the owning-device sentinel is −1. Constructor exceptions destroy this partial state. Validate N in [2,65536], matching nonempty tower lists, prime q<2⁶³, canonical primitive roots and checked buffer extents. Store N and limb count K. Set tile length min(256,N) and column length N/256 . For every limb, construct a NegacyclicNtt CPU reference and build powers of ψ and ψ-inverse for exponents 0 through N inclusive . Compute N-inverse. Capture the current CUDA device, then allocate the six persistent buffers. Host allocations have already occurred; parameter checks precede device allocation. Upload both power tables, the moduli, and inverse lengths: four H2D copies. Record row-grid count max(1,N/256) and elementwise-grid count ceil(K*N/256) . All kernels launch 256 threads per block. The CPU constructor checks power-of-two N≥2, (q−1) % (2N) == 0 , ψ^N mod q == q−1 , and q>N. It assumes prime q; it does not itself run a primality test. The wrapper now adds that prime/range/extent validation in cuda_validation.hpp before any CUDA call. After construction, temporary host power-table/inverse-length vectors are released. Impl retains the moduli vector and per-limb CPU references, not a second permanent host copy of every power table. Persistent allocation and ownership map ¶ For N=65536/K=3, define S=1,572,864 B, T=1,572,888 B, R=24 B. In general, \\(S=8KN,\\ T=8K(N+1),\\ R=8K\\) bytes. Every stored element is uint64_t . Device allocation Elements / bytes Layout and role Writer / reader device_operand_a 196,608 / 1,572,864 B [limb][coefficient or evaluation position] ; working input/output Public input copy and kernels write; result copy reads device_operand_b 196,608 / 1,572,864 B Second operand; separate from A Input copy and its forward transform write; pointwise kernel reads device_twiddle_powers 196,611 / 1,572,888 B [limb][exponent] , \\(\\psi^e\\) Constructor uploads; forward kernels read device_inverse_twiddle_powers 196,611 / 1,572,888 B [limb][exponent] , \\((\\psi^{-1})^e\\) Constructor uploads; inverse kernels read device_moduli 3 / 24 B q for each limb Constructor uploads; arithmetic kernels read device_inverse_transform_lengths 3 / 24 B N-inverse modulo each q Constructor uploads; final scale kernel reads CudaNttTransform owns unique_ptr<Impl> ; Impl owns the CUDA pointers. They are private: callers cannot retain a borrowed device-buffer view. Copy construction and copy assignment are deleted. No move operations are declared; this class is not currently movable either. Do not use one instance concurrently: public operations share and overwrite A/B and there is no locking or per-call scratch. On normal destruction or constructor unwinding, unique_ptr destroys Impl . Its destructor selects the owning device, calls cudaFree on each pointer and restores the previous device. Host-preflight failure skips CUDA entirely. Device-selection/free errors cannot be reported by this nonthrowing destructor; a failed device selection skips freeing rather than using a different device. Do not infer safe concurrent destruction or synchronization with unrelated GPU work. Exact byte counts (requested allocations, not measured device usage) ¶ \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Operands}&=2S=3{,}145{,}728\\ \\mathrm{B},\\\\ \\text{Constants}&=2T+2R=3{,}145{,}824\\ \\mathrm{B},\\\\ \\text{All six}&=2S+2T+2R=6{,}291{,}552\\ \\mathrm{B}. \\end{aligned} \\] For three limbs , the source formulas give: N One operand S Both operands Constants / constructor upload All six allocations 65536 1,572,864 B (1.5 MiB) 3,145,728 B 3,145,824 B 6,291,552 B The extra 96 bytes comprise 48 bytes for the two power tables' final entries and 48 bytes for the moduli and inverse lengths. CUDA context/allocator overhead, registers, per-block shared memory, host vectors, and CPU-reference objects are not included. These are formulas, not a VRAM measurement or a memory-capacity benchmark. Public API contracts ¶ For operands, flat index k*N+i belongs to limb k. Power-table index k*(N+1)+e belongs to exponent e of that limb. Transform kernels select limbs with blockIdx.y . Pointwise and scale kernels instead use a flat grid over K*N entries and recover the limb by integer division by N. Public call Input → output representation Host-visible effect Copies / launches at N≥512 forward(values) Natural coefficients → bit-reversed evaluations Mutates host vector H2D A, column + row, D2H A: 2 copies, 2 launches inverse(values) Bit-reversed evaluations → natural coefficients Mutates host vector H2D A, row + column + scale, D2H A: 2 copies, 3 launches pointwise_multiply(left,right) Matching bit-reversed evaluations → same order Replaces left; right is a const input H2D A+B, pointwise, D2H A: 3 copies, 1 launch multiply(left,right) Natural coefficients → natural negacyclic product Left is passed by value; returns its result buffer H2D A+B, 2 forwards + pointwise + inverse, D2H A: 3 copies, 8 launches Inputs must contain K*N residues in their corresponding [0,q[k]) ranges. Methods now check lengths and scan canonical residue ranges before upload, then select the owning device. They do not store a runtime coefficient/evaluation tag. The caller is responsible for representation and parameter compatibility. A vector from another modulus set with the same length is not rejected merely for having different provenance. n() and limbs() return scalar geometry without a device copy. q(k) and ref(k) use bounds-checked host vectors; ref(k) is a borrowed const reference valid only while the transform object exists. These getters do not expose the GPU allocation or transfer data. Follow a complete multiplication ¶ HOST · input left: 196,608 residues right: 196,608 residues ↓ H2D · 2 copies · 3 MiB total GPU · reusable A and B A · 1.5 MiB Coefficients ↓ column DIF · 256 blocks/tower ↓ row DIF · 256 blocks/tower Bit-reversed evaluations B · 1.5 MiB Coefficients ↓ column DIF · 256 blocks/tower ↓ row DIF · 256 blocks/tower Bit-reversed evaluations ↓ A × B, write A · 1 kernel · 768 blocks A · product evaluations ↓ row DIT → column DIT → inverse scale 3 kernels; result: 196,608 coefficient residues Constants stay here: forward powers 1,572,888 B + inverse powers 1,572,888 B + moduli 24 B + inverse lengths 24 B. ↓ D2H · 1 copy · 1.5 MiB HOST · returned left 196,608 residues · 1.5 MiB · natural coefficient order For this three-tower example, each transform launch has a 256×3 grid, 256 threads per block, and 2 KiB shared memory per block. The pointwise and scale grids each have 768 blocks. A complete multiply uses eight launches and three operand copies, totaling 4,718,592 B (4.5 MiB) . The four constant buffers are read throughout this chain; they are not re-uploaded per operation. B is scratch after the call, not a cached operand that the next public operation is entitled to reuse. Every new multiply() uploads both input operands again. Passing an lvalue as left creates a host copy; passing a moved vector can transfer its host allocation into the by-value parameter instead. Each public method uses cudaMemcpy , not cudaMemcpyAsync , and returns the downloaded host result. Launches specify no explicit stream; there is no public stream/event parameter, asynchronous completion handle, or device-resident buffer API. cudaGetLastError() follows launches; copies are also checked by CHECK_CUDA . There is no automatic CPU fallback on a CUDA error. Transfer boundary: fused versus separate calls ¶ Excluding construction, each forward/inverse transfers 2S bytes and pointwise or fused multiply transfers 3S bytes. For a fair comparison of two inputs : Route for two input polynomials Copies Requested transfer payload Kernel launches Four public calls: forward A, forward B, pointwise, inverse 9 14,155,776 B (13.5 MiB) 8 One multiply(A,B) call 3 4,718,592 B (4.5 MiB) 8 Both paths perform eight kernels at the target sizes. Fusion removes six operand-sized transfer copies (9S→3S), not six kernels. At N=65536/K=3 this is 14,155,776 B versus 4,718,592 B of requested H2D+D2H payload. It is not a measured latency speedup. Host vector copies, allocator costs and PCIe/cache effects are separate. At N≤256 forward uses one kernel, inverse two and multiply five; the host-copy counts are unchanged. The benchmark wraps public calls in wall-clock timing, so its GPU columns include operand transfers. Construction/table uploads occur outside that per-call region; see run_bench in the implementation. It is not kernel-only timing. Open ownership and integration issues ¶ Constructor cleanup: the original raw-Impl ownership gap is repaired with unique_ptr . Partial device state now reaches the cleanup destructor during unwinding. Real allocation/upload failure injection remains required; host rejection tests do not prove CUDA cleanup under a failed runtime/context. The wrapper captures its owning device and guards operations/destruction. Native wrong-device, restore-failure and cleanup-error tests are still open. No stream/event lifetime contract, buffer view, representation/level tag, evaluation-key cache, or OpenFHE Q/P base-switch integration exists in this interface. Reusable allocations are not the final integration API. The wrapper compiles and links for sm_89/sm_120 with stock CUDA 13.0. Launch/barrier, arithmetic and driver behavior remain unverified on hardware. D9's backend proposal , accepted by D10's ADR , specifies these ownership/API decisions; the production device API and baseline RAII changes still require explicit native failure/lifetime tests. The D6 arithmetic review examines arithmetic bounds, parameter assumptions, and adversarial exactness. Verification and limits ¶ Source-contract tests check that this map matches current allocation sizes, frees, public copy directions, operation ordering and host-only interface shape. They also pin the N=65536 arithmetic above. These are deliberately static source checks , not CUDA-runtime tests or proof of constructor failure safety. Existing CTests verify CPU arithmetic and shared stage helpers. No new claim of actual GPU support/performance is made by completing D5."}, {"title": "Arithmetic bounds & tests", "summary": "D6: canonical residues, overflow proofs, bigint checks, full-size adversarial cases, and remaining exactness gates.", "group": "Learn", "url": "modular-arithmetic-contract.html", "text": "Modular arithmetic: bounds and exactness gates ¶ D6 connects the butterfly formulas to the integer operations they actually execute. The current target is N=65536, with 50–60-bit prime towers stored in uint64_t . This is a correctness contract, not a GPU performance result. Read the buffer/dataflow map first for where the arrays live. The implementation is in mod_arith.hpp , ntt.hpp , and dev_core.hpp . 1. What callers must supply ¶ Canonical means an integer in \\([0,q)\\) . None of the hot arithmetic functions validates this range at runtime. Operation Required input Output add_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{63}\\) Canonical \\((a+b)\\bmod q\\) sub_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{63}\\) Canonical \\((a-b)\\bmod q\\) mul_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{64}\\) Canonical \\(ab\\bmod q\\) pow_mod(a,e,q) Any uint64_t a,e; q>0 Canonical \\(a^e\\bmod q\\) ; q=1 returns 0 inv_mod(x,q) Prime q≥2; x not divisible by q Canonical inverse via \\(x^{q-2}\\) Add/sub retain their documented conservative bound; this review does not expand the public contract. Multiplication's wider bound does not make a full transform with q≥2^63 part of the supported contract. For a transform, also require a power-of-two N≥2, prime q with \\(2N\\mid(q-1)\\) , and the same primitive 2N-th root ψ as the comparison implementation. At the target N, \\(2N=131072\\) and q>N. Forward consumes canonical coefficients in natural order; inverse consumes canonical evaluations in bit-reversed order. There is no Montgomery representation or lazy residue format in this code. The constructor checks size shape, divisibility and \\(\\psi^N=q-1\\) , but does not test primality or the advertised modulus-width limit . Public array methods check length, not residue bounds. Trusted parameter selection and canonical input are caller obligations today, not rejection guarantees. 2. Why intermediates fit ¶ For canonical a,b: Addition: \\(0\\le a+b\\le 2q-2\\) . One subtraction is sufficient. The code forms the sum in 128 bits; after subtraction it is below q and fits uint64_t. Subtraction: if a≥b, use a-b. Otherwise \\(0\\le a+(q-b)<q\\) ; the selected branch therefore cannot overflow uint64_t. Do not compute unsigned a-b first and assume wraparound implements reduction modulo q. Multiplication: \\(0\\le ab\\le(q-1)^2<2^{128}\\) . The cast happens before multiplication, preserving the full product; remainder produces a value below q. Multiplying in uint64_t first would lose the high half. At q<2^60, the product is below 2^120 and a sum below 2^61. Storage in uint64_t is safe, but a uint64_t product is still insufficient. Exponentiation reduces a before its first multiply. Each multiplication preserves canonical range. A uint64_t exponent takes at most 64 loop iterations. Even e=0 computes 1 % q , so q=0 is never legal. These are bounds for the current exact-remainder code. Barrett, Shoup, Montgomery, 32-bit limbs, or lazy reduction will need their own proofs, including quotient error, carries, temporary width, and final correction. 3. The invariant through every butterfly ¶ Forward and inverse use these canonical modular operations: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Forward: }&t=cv\\bmod q,\\quad u'=u+t\\bmod q,\\quad v'=(u-t)w\\bmod q,\\\\ \\text{Inverse: }&t=yw^{-1}\\bmod q,\\quad u'=x+t\\bmod q,\\quad v'=(x-t)c^{-1}\\bmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] Tables contain canonical powers. Every operation above returns canonical values, so induction over all 16 stages preserves \\([0,q)\\) . Inverse's final multiplication by N-inverse does too. Pointwise multiplication has the same invariant. There is no stage where values are intentionally in \\([0,2q)\\) or \\([0,4q)\\) . Feeding lazy OpenFHE/internal buffers directly into this interface without an explicit representation contract would violate its assumptions. What arithmetic work is actually counted ¶ N=65536 has \\(32768\\times16=524288\\) butterflies per direction and per tower. The CPU reference uses three modular multiplications per butterfly: two for data and one to advance the twiddle. Shared device-stage helpers use two, reading precomputed twiddles instead. Thus the loop bodies perform 1,572,864 and 1,048,576 multiplications respectively; inverse adds 65,536 normalization multiplies. Setup powers/table generation are additional work. The old arithmetic-header comment counted the CPU loop incorrectly; D6 fixes that comment without changing the algorithms. __uint128_t remainder is the correctness baseline. Its CUDA lowering and cost must be checked in a native build/profile, not inferred as one hardware division instruction. 4. Executable adversarial coverage ¶ Two new gates run in the normal CPU CTest suite: Gate Inputs and comparison What it establishes arithmetic_bigint_check 14,956 compiled results versus Python arbitrary-precision addition, subtraction, multiplication, power and modular inverse Exact host primitive answers, not a copy of the same C++ reduction expression arithmetic_transform_check N=65536 at generated 50-, 55-, 60-bit primes; six patterns in both coefficient/evaluation roundtrips Canonical forward/inverse results and two-sided roundtrip identity Same transform gate \\(x^{N-1}\\cdot x\\) must produce q-1 at index 0 and zero elsewhere Closed-form negacyclic wrap sign, not merely roundtrip consistency Primitive inputs include 0, 1, 2, q/2, q-2, q-1, values around 2^32, and 256 seeded random pairs per modulus. Moduli include small, even, composite, 50/55/60-bit boundaries, just below 2^63, and full-width uint64_t boundaries. The latter use mul/pow only, respecting the add/sub contract. Exponents include 0, 1, 2, 63, 64 and UINT64_MAX; pow also receives an unreduced base. Inverse checks small and generated 50/55/60-bit primes against Python's modular-inverse algorithm, and confirms the host exception for zero/multiples of q. Parameter generation is reused; this is not an independent primality certification test. The six transform patterns are zero, all q-1, alternating zero/q-1, a final coefficient impulse, ramp and deterministic pseudorandom. A roundtrip alone cannot prove spectral ordering or OpenFHE compatibility; use the other gates. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build -j 8 ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure ctest --test-dir build -R 'arithmetic_' -V Sources: C++ probe and full-size tests , independent primitive oracle . 5. Exactness ladder: do not substitute one gate for another ¶ Existing gate Scope Limit unit_tests Small arithmetic and NTT product against schoolbook Schoolbook shares arithmetic primitives oracle_crosscheck N=1024 forward arrays against direct Python NTT sum, plus CRT and identity Not a full-size GPU test Column/inverse/tile mapping checks Exhaustive target-size index/twiddle probes of shared CPU/CUDA helpers CPU execution; no CUDA barriers/races tested device_schedule_check Exact simulator/reference arrays, including N=65536 fixtures Current fixtures use 46-bit primes openfhe_exact_compat (opt-in) N=65536 NativePoly forward/inverse arrays, including independent evaluation input; OpenFHE selects parameters via FirstPrime(50) and LastPrime(59) Pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1 CPU comparison, not a CUDA/OpenFHE pipeline Native CUDA oracle/reference gates (when CUDA is available) Actual kernel outputs versus reference/oracle Must be run on named hardware; not verified on this Mac The OpenFHE gate includes zero, all q-1, ramp, random coefficients and random evaluation arrays. Same q and root are essential: another valid root may give a different evaluation permutation. The D7 contract freezes the representation and tower boundary. CKKS approximate decryption is not a replacement for these exact per-index comparisons. 6. Deferred adversarial checks and unsafe inputs ¶ D6's review is complete; these are explicit later implementation gates: P2/P3, real GPU: repeat the 50/55/60-bit patterns through actual kernels, compare each forward/inverse/pointwise result and canonical range, then run compute-sanitizer race/memory checks and test launch failures. CPU success does not establish nvcc support, device reduction correctness, or timing. API hardening: D9/D10 define the contracts. Later offline preparation adds baseline rejection of noncanonical residues, invalid roots, composite moduli, unsafe dimensions and vector lengths, with host negative tests. Runtime format/provenance tags, device failure paths and the production resident API remain later gates; current APIs do not reject every possible misuse. Parameter helpers: find_primes currently assumes safe shifts and a nonzero, nonoverflowing 2N . Invalid bit counts can cause undefined shifts; find_psi assumes a compatible prime q>3 and has no attempt limit. These helpers are not hardened untrusted-input parsers. Raw primitive misuse: q=0 causes invalid remainder/division operations; it is not a test input to execute. Composite q violates Fermat inverse's premise even if x is invertible: for example q=15,x=2 returns 2, not 8. Host zero-input inverse throws; the device branch returns 0 and is not a supported inverse/error-reporting API. Device kernels currently never invert. Future reduction optimization: exercise maximum correction counts, carry/borrow transitions, high-word products and every accepted lazy range against the independent bigint gate before allowing an optimized dispatch. These gaps do not block the D6 source-review deliverable. They do block claims of a hardened production API or GPU/OpenFHE integration. The D8 Hybrid graph and D9 buffer/stream/cache contract are complete. D10's ADR closes P1's design gate; native hardening and implementation are tracked in the backlog ."}, {"title": "OpenFHE representation", "summary": "D7: exact coefficient/evaluation ordering, root selection, DCRT tower packing, format APIs, and executable compatibility checks.", "group": "Learn", "url": "openfhe-representation-contract.html", "text": "OpenFHE representation contract — D7 ¶ This freezes the NTT/DCRT boundary for OpenFHE 1.6.1 , commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , at N=65536 . This contract uses the 64-bit NativeInteger backend . It covers coefficient/evaluation format, exact array order, per-tower parameters, and packing into the current CUDA interface. The D8 Hybrid operation graph builds on this boundary. The executable evidence is openfhe_representation_tests.cpp . It uses the real OpenFHE core library on CPU. No native CUDA result is claimed. 1. The boundary in one table ¶ Property Required contract Ring \\(\\mathbb Z_q[x]/(x^N+1)\\) , \\(N=65536\\) ; cyclotomic order \\(m=2N=131072\\) Coefficient index i Coefficient of \\(x^i\\) , in natural order Evaluation memory index k Evaluation at \\(\\psi^{2\\operatorname{rev}_{16}(k)+1}\\) , with 16-bit index reversal Tower modulus q The exact modulus in that tower's OpenFHE parameters Root ψ The exact primitive 2N-th root in those same parameters Residue representation Unsigned canonical values in \\([0,q)\\) ; no external twist or Montgomery conversion Forward Natural coefficients → bit-reversed evaluations, in place Inverse Bit-reversed evaluations → natural coefficients; N-inverse normalization included GPU buffer order values[limb*N + index] , preserving the supplied tower list These evaluation indices are not CKKS's decoded complex-slot indices . Encoding/decoding uses another embedding and is outside this NTT contract. 2. What each format means ¶ OpenFHE's Format enum names COEFFICIENT and EVALUATION . The label alone does not express index ordering. For the power-of-two ring, NativePoly::SwitchFormat calls ForwardTransformToBitReverseInPlace or InverseTransformFromBitReverseInPlace . The NTT's evaluation array is therefore bit-reversed even though its public format is simply EVALUATION . Our reference and CUDA entry points do not store an OpenFHE format flag. The caller must know the current domain before invoking forward() or inverse() . Calling forward twice is not an idempotent format assignment. No explicit input twist or output permutation is needed at this boundary. The negacyclic twist is already folded into the butterfly schedule. Ordinary bit reversal changes an index , not byte order or the bits of a residue. 3. Exact evaluation and inverse formulas ¶ Let rev16(k) reverse the low 16 bits of k and define \\(r_k=\\psi^{2\\operatorname{rev}_{16}(k)+1}\\pmod q\\) . Then: \\[ \\mathrm{forward}(a)[k]=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i r_k^i\\pmod q, \\qquad \\mathrm{inverse}(y)[i]=N^{-1}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}y_k r_k^{-i}\\pmod q. \\] The first natural evaluation indices in memory are 0, 32768, 16384, 49152, 8192, ... . Thus the corresponding root exponents begin 1, 65537, 32769, 98305, 16385, ... . In particular, slot 0 contains \\(a(\\psi)\\) and slot 1 contains \\(a(-\\psi)\\) . Probe Expected array \\(a(x)=1\\) Every evaluation is 1 \\(a(x)=x\\) Slot k is \\(r_k\\) \\(a(x)=x^{17}\\) Slot k is \\(r_k^{17}\\) \\(a(x)=x^{N-1}\\) Slot k is \\(r_k^{N-1}\\) Evaluation array with 1 only at slot s Coefficient i is \\(N^{-1}r_s^{-i}\\) These closed forms distinguish order and scaling errors that a shared forward/inverse round trip could conceal. The D7 gate checks all N outputs, not just the first few slots. 4. Changing format versus changing its label ¶ The shared SetFormat implementation calls SwitchFormat only when the requested format differs from the current one. API Effect SetFormat(current_format) No conversion; values remain unchanged SetFormat(other_format) Runs the forward or inverse transform SwitchFormat() Always toggles format and transforms values NativePoly::SetValues(values, format) Assigns the values and label; does not transform them NativePoly::OverrideFormat(format) Changes only the label DCRTPoly::OverrideFormat(format) Changes only the outer label; does not retag or transform its NativePoly children The last two methods are not substitutes for conversion. A DCRT object with outer EVALUATION and coefficient-format children is inconsistent. Keep the outer and child formats aligned when importing GPU results. To import an already-transformed array, create an evaluation-format NativePoly for each tower and assign its values with the evaluation label. Inverting it again during import would undo the GPU computation. D7 tests this import path using a separate DCRT object. 5. Roots, prime widths, and the transform cache ¶ A valid primitive root is not necessarily the same root. Replacing ψ by \\(\\psi^3\\) still gives a primitive 2N-th root but changes the evaluation addresses. Do not regenerate roots using this repository's independent parameter finder when comparing or importing an OpenFHE context. Read each tower's root. The pinned RootOfUnity implementation chooses the smallest primitive root by enumerating primitive powers. The D7 fixtures use this function with LastPrime(bits, 2N) for exact 50-, 55-, and 60-bit primes. The test independently enumerates odd root powers and checks the minimum. FirstPrime(bits, m) searches above \\(2^{\\mathrm{bits}}\\) ; its argument is not a guarantee of a prime with exactly that many bits. LastPrime searches below that bound and checks the resulting bit length. This is why the D7 matrix uses LastPrime. Cache constraint: the pinned FTT table cache looks up by modulus and checks table length; it does not compare the requested root on a cache hit. Use a consistent root for each modulus/ring-size pair. The gate tests the normal OpenFHE-generated root choice. Arbitrary custom-root contexts sharing a modulus are not covered by this result. Do not call a global cache reset inside an integration adapter to work around this constraint. OpenFHE's bit-reversed power tables and preconditioned multiplication constants are internal implementation data. They are not interchangeable with this repository's \\([\\psi^0,\\ldots,\\psi^N]\\) table. Share parameter values and polynomial arrays; construct backend-specific twiddle tables separately. 6. DCRT tower order and flat GPU packing ¶ DCRTPoly owns a vector of NativePoly objects. Each tower has its own modulus, root, format, and value allocation. The ILDCRTParams constructor retains the provided modulus/root order and forms the composite modulus Q as their product. The polynomial's composite Q can exceed 64 bits. It is not a native tower modulus and must not be truncated into the CUDA modulus array. For N=65536, each row below is copied from its own NativePoly allocation: OpenFHE tower Parameters Flat GPU element range Byte offset 0 \\((q_0,\\psi_0)\\) 0…65535 0 1 \\((q_1,\\psi_1)\\) 65536…131071 524288 2 \\((q_2,\\psi_2)\\) 131072…196607 1048576 \\[ \\mathrm{address}(\\ell,i)=65536\\ell+i,\\qquad Q=q_0q_1q_2. \\] Read the active tower list from the object, not a hard-coded sorted list or an assumed CKKS level. The D7 test deliberately uses bit widths in the order 55, 50, 60 so sorting or swapping towers cannot silently pass. Packing coefficient data requires \\(a_i\\bmod q_\\ell\\) at each address. For an integer coefficient -1, this is \\(q_\\ell-1\\) in every tower. The test starts from a BigInteger polynomial containing \\(Q-1\\) and \\(2^{64}+17\\) , imports it as a DCRTPoly, and checks all residues and coefficient-domain CRT reconstruction. No interpolation is needed to run the per-tower NTT itself. CRTInterpolate() requires coefficient format and returns a BigInteger Poly with the placeholder root 1. That root is not a valid primitive 2N-th root; do not use the interpolated object's placeholder to configure an NTT backend. OpenFHE's separate tower allocations are not one contiguous host buffer. Copy values tower by tower into the flat buffer; do not reinterpret-cast the DCRT object or assume its NativeInteger objects have the GPU's scalar ABI. The D5 dataflow map describes the current host-vector API and the ownership/stream design subsequently specified in D9 and accepted by D10 . 7. Pointwise products and active levels ¶ With matching q, ψ, format, and slot order, pointwise evaluation multiplication followed by inverse gives the negacyclic ring product. D7 multiplies a full three-tower DCRT polynomial by x and checks the independent coefficient rule: \\[ \\mathrm{out}_i= \\begin{cases} -\\mathrm{in}_{N-1}\\pmod q,&i=0,\\\\ \\mathrm{in}_{i-1},&1\\le i<N. \\end{cases} \\] Both the wrap term and large-input coefficient residues are checked. DropLastElement() removes the last tower and its parameter. It retains the remaining ordered prefix without scaling or transforming values. It is not CKKS rescaling, ModUp, or ModDown. D7 checks this prefix behavior, including that dropping a copied object's last tower does not change the original's parameter list. The D8 graph records the actual Hybrid basis and reduction operations. 8. Adapter checklist and remaining scope ¶ Before handing an object to the backend: Check N and cyclotomic order, then obtain the actual ordered active towers. Retain each tower's q and ψ; validate the supported modulus/root contract. Establish the domain and ordering, with consistent outer/child formats. Pack canonical values at limb*N+index , without a twist or permutation. Run the explicitly requested operation. Inverse includes normalization. Import results with matching parameters and truthful format labels. This freezes the power-of-two NTT and DCRT array boundary. It does not freeze Hybrid digit order, Q/P concatenation, rounding, key layout, device ownership, stream/event lifetime, or CPU fallback. The D8 graph now specifies Hybrid digit order, Q/P layout, and the pinned CKKS reduction sequence. D9's device ownership/API design is now accepted by D10's ADR , closing P1's architecture gate. Native runtime implementation and its exactness/failure tests remain later work. 9. Run the checks ¶ After installing the pinned OpenFHE release with scripts/openfhe_setup.sh : cmake -S . -B build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -R 'openfhe_exact_compat|openfhe_representation_contract' --output-on-failure The original gate retains random and boundary coefficient cases plus an independent inverse input. The new representation gate adds full-size monomial and inverse-basis formulas, format API behavior, root selection, three-tower packing/import, BigInteger coefficients, pointwise multiplication, and tower dropping. It links only OpenFHE core. See the daily entry for observed build and publication results. Pinned source references ¶ All links below identify the exact OpenFHE commit inspected for this contract. The linked entry points lead to the statements used above. Format enum and SetFormat . NativePoly SwitchFormat and SetValues . FTT dispatch and cached tables . RootOfUnity and prime selection . DCRT format conversion and tower parameters . DCRT interpolation and dropping a tower ."}, {"title": "Hybrid operation graph", "summary": "D8: exact digit extension, Q/P tower addressing, key MAC, ModDown, delayed operations, and full-size OpenFHE checks.", "group": "Learn", "url": "openfhe-hybrid-operation-graph.html", "text": "OpenFHE Hybrid key switching: the exact operation graph ¶ D8 traces OpenFHE 1.6.1 , commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , with N=65536 and the 64-bit NativeInteger backend. It fixes the sequence of representation changes, CRT representatives, key addressing, accumulation, and reduction that a future backend must preserve. Read the D7 representation contract for the NTT's exact array order. The executable evidence is openfhe_hybrid_tests.cpp . It calls the actual OpenFHE PKE library and compares complete arrays with an independent unreduced-CRT formula and scalar arithmetic. These are CPU results. 1. Read the complete path ¶ The ordinary path starts at KeySwitchCore(c,key) . Its output is two contributions in the active Q basis, ordered B first, A second . The ciphertext wrapper decides how to combine them with existing components. Split the active Q towers Contiguous groups of up to α towers form the digits. Keep their original evaluation arrays. Convert each digit to coefficients Inverse NTT only the towers in that digit. Extend to its complement Approximate CRT produces coefficients in every other active Q tower and every P tower. Return the complement to evaluations Forward NTT those new towers, then stitch them around the saved original digit towers. Multiply and accumulate in QlP All digits contribute to two evaluation arrays, using the B and A key vectors. Reduce each accumulated result Inverse NTT its P towers, approximate-switch P to active Q, forward NTT the correction, subtract, and multiply by the inverse of P. Update the ciphertext Add the first contribution to c0; add or replace c1 according to the caller. The implementation has no general coefficient-domain round trip of all QlP towers. It preserves the original digit evaluations during extension and leaves Q evaluations intact during ModDown. 2. Names, levels, and tower order ¶ Let M be the full context's Q-tower count, L the current ciphertext's active Q-tower count, K the P-tower count, and D the configured large-digit count. The active basis is an ordered prefix of Q: \\[ Q_\\ell=\\prod_{i=0}^{L-1}q_i,\\qquad P=\\prod_{j=0}^{K-1}p_j,\\qquad \\alpha=\\left\\lceil\\frac{M}{D}\\right\\rceil,\\qquad \\beta_\\ell=\\left\\lceil\\frac{L}{\\alpha}\\right\\rceil. \\] The source also caps the active digit count at the configured partition count. For valid active prefixes this does not alter the formula above. α belongs to the full context; do not recompute it from L. The last active digit may be shorter than α, including when only part of the first digit remains. Object Tower order Representation Input component q₀ … qₗ₋₁ Evaluation, bit-reversed within each tower Digit d's source q at indices dα … min((d+1)α,L)−1 Evaluation, then coefficient Digit d's complement Other active Q towers in order, then p₀ … pₖ₋₁ Coefficient, then evaluation Assembled digit Active Q prefix, then all P Evaluation Stored key pair for digit d Full Q, then all P Evaluation Each MAC output Active Q prefix, then all P Evaluation Each ModDown output Active Q prefix Evaluation Here L is a tower count , not the ciphertext's public GetLevel() value. Derive it from the actual element parameters; the D9 proposal keeps these metadata consistent. Every tower retains its exact OpenFHE modulus and root. 3. Why approximate CRT must keep the unreduced sum ¶ For a digit's actual source basis \\(B=\\prod_i b_i\\) , define \\(\\widehat B_i=B/b_i\\) . At coefficient index r, the implementation computes: \\[ t_i=[x_i\\widehat B_i^{-1}]_{b_i},\\qquad S=\\sum_i t_i\\widehat B_i,\\qquad \\widetilde x_m=[S]_m. \\] Brackets mean the canonical residue in the indicated modulus. The final modulus m is a complement tower. There is no reduction of S modulo B before reducing it modulo m. In general: \\[ S=x+\\kappa B,\\qquad 0\\le x<B,\\qquad [S]_m\\ne[x]_m. \\] ApproxSwitchCRTBasis implements this sum using the precomputed hat inverses, hat residues, and modular multiplication/accumulation. Our test independently forms the full unreduced sum with arbitrary-precision BigInteger arithmetic; it does not read those conversion tables to compute its expected answer. The digit is converted to coefficient format before this operation. Its complement is then transformed using each destination tower's own root. The digit's original evaluation towers are copied directly into their slots. Applying the CRT formula to evaluation coordinates would change the result. This contract uses WITH_REDUCED_NOISE=OFF . The centered-conversion branch selected by that build option is different and is outside this fixture matrix. 4. A reduced level changes key addresses ¶ A key keeps all M Q towers followed by K P towers. A ciphertext digit contains only L Q towers followed by those same K P towers. For active-array index i: \\[ \\operatorname{keyIndex}(i)= \\begin{cases} i,&i<L,\\\\ i+(M-L),&i\\ge L. \\end{cases} \\] For the fixture M=5, α=3, K=3 , reducing to L=4 gives: Array Tower positions Active digit q₀, q₁, q₂, q₃, p₀, p₁, p₂ Full key indices used 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 Full key index skipped 4, the dropped q₄ Index 4 in the active digit is p₀, while index 4 in the key is q₄. Using the same flat index for both silently reads the wrong key tower when unchecked multiplication is used. The diagnostic key gives every digit, tower, and evaluation position a distinct deterministic value to expose this. Explore the active level Choose the number of remaining Q towers in the executable fixture. 5 Q towers 4 Q towers 3 Q towers 2 Q towers 1 Q tower 5. Two MACs, then two ModDowns ¶ For digit evaluation array d and key arrays A and B, each active tower m and bit-reversed evaluation coordinate k compute: \\[ Z_{0,m,k}=\\left[\\sum_d D_{d,m,k}B_{d,\\operatorname{keyIndex}(m),k}\\right]_m, \\] \\[ Z_{1,m,k}=\\left[\\sum_d D_{d,m,k}A_{d,\\operatorname{keyIndex}(m),k}\\right]_m. \\] EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt returns these two QlP arrays. The multiplication is pointwise; there is no NTT inside the MAC loop. All products and sums remain canonical residues. The evaluation key already supplies the gadget scaling: in private-to-private generation, its old-secret term is P times the old secret on that digit's Q towers, and zero on its other towers. Do not add an extra global twist or a guessed CRT/gadget multiplier to the digits. For each accumulated array Z, extract and inverse-transform its P towers: \\[ u_j=\\operatorname{INTT}_{p_j}(Z_{p_j}),\\qquad \\widehat P_j=P/p_j, \\] \\[ R_i=\\left[\\sum_j [u_j\\widehat P_j^{-1}]_{p_j}\\widehat P_j\\right]_{q_i}, \\] \\[ Y_{q_i}=\\left[(Z_{q_i}-\\operatorname{NTT}_{q_i}(R_i)) [P^{-1}]_{q_i}\\right]_{q_i}. \\] This is the exact implementation order for the pinned CKKS path. Its noise scale is 1, so the source passes t=0 and skips the BGV-specific t factors. There is no floating-point rounding operation in these steps. Calling this “division by P” does not authorize replacing its approximate representative with a canonical or centered CRT representative, or with a generic rounding rule. Accumulate before ModDown. Moving ModDown inside the digit sum changes carry/correction terms. A simple scalar illustration with q=97 and P=17 is \\(\\operatorname{Down}(16)=0\\) , \\(\\operatorname{Down}(1)=0\\) , but \\(\\operatorname{Down}(17)=1\\) . This is a modular-arithmetic example, not a small polynomial fixture. The full-size test also explicitly checks that the wrong per-digit reduction order produces different arrays. 6. Which wrapper calls which graph? ¶ Entry point Result and caller obligation EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore β active digit arrays in QlP; reusable for hoisted operations with compatible parameters EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt Two QlP MAC arrays; reduction is deferred EvalFastKeySwitchCore Extended MAC followed by ModDown of both arrays KeySwitchCore Precompute followed by the fast core; two Q contributions KeySwitchInPlace with two components Add B contribution to c₀; replace c₁ with the A contribution KeySwitchInPlace with three components Add B to c₀ and A to c₁; discard c₂ Relinearization Switch each component from index 2 using the corresponding evaluation key, accumulate into c₀/c₁, then resize to two For the in-place wrapper: \\[ c'_0=c_0+Y_0,\\qquad c'_1=\\begin{cases}Y_1,&\\text{two-component input},\\\\c_1+Y_1,&\\text{three-component input}.\\end{cases} \\] D8 executes the two- and three-component wrapper cases. The source trace also records the general relinearization loop; higher ciphertext degrees and their full key families remain a later integration test. Delayed QP operations are distinct from digit extension ¶ KeySwitchExt(ciphertext,addFirst) multiplies the retained Q evaluations by P and appends zero P towers . It does not approximate-lift the original ciphertext into P. If addFirst=false , its entire first component is zero. KeySwitchDown reduces both accumulated QP components; KeySwitchDownFirstElement reduces only the first. The test checks both addFirst settings and delayed addition of a QP MAC result before reduction. The pinned CKKS EvalFastRotationExt has another ordering requirement: it computes the extended MAC, optionally adds P times c₀ on the Q towers, then applies the automorphism to both QP components . Reduction is deferred. The automorphism map and full rotation API are source-traced here; their exact end-to-end arrays remain P6/P7 work. Do not move the permutation across the selected rotation key without a separate equivalence proof. 7. Full-size fixture and evidence ¶ The deterministic fixture uses FIXEDMANUAL CKKS with multiplicative depth 4, first modulus size 60, scaling modulus size 50, two large digits, and the fixed ring dimension. It produces five Q towers and three P towers . Tower Modulus Primitive root q₀ 1152921504606584833 18043022392882 q₁ 1125899902124033 15995550378 q₂ 1125899911168001 21670117260 q₃ 1125899903827969 938640682 q₄ 1125899908022273 6511788946 p₀ 1152921504598720513 800790938143 p₁ 1152921504597016577 17749908910371 p₂ 1152921504595968001 11469071954203 The configuration requests 50 scaling bits, but q₂ and q₄ are 51-bit primes just above the power-of-two boundary; q₁ and q₃ are 50-bit. All P towers and q₀ are 60-bit. Use the exact table rather than inferring widths from the knob. These exact parameters are asserted in the executable. Pattern generation and diagnostic key generation use fixed integer formulas in that source file. The diagnostic key is not a cryptographic evaluation key . A separate pair of actual generated secret keys supplies a real key-switching key; its fixed arrays are shared by all comparisons in that run. Random key generation is not compared across separate runs. Cases Coverage L=5,4,3,1 × four input patterns Full level, partial last digit, exact digit boundary, partial first digit Zero, all q−1, monomial x¹⁷, deterministic dense residues Zero path, bounds, closed-form coefficient structure, tower-distinct data Actual generated key at L=5 and L=4 Same exact graph with real cryptographic key arrays Intermediate states Digit INTT, complement CRT and NTT, digit assembly, both MACs, P INTT, correction CRT and NTT, final scale Composition and wrappers Fast/full core equality, input immutability, two/three-component update, delayed QP path Negative alternatives Canonical-lift substitution, missing P-key offset, per-digit ModDown Observed on Linux x86_64 with GCC 14.2, Release, NativeInteger 64, and OpenMP off: 18 cases and 117,571,584 residue comparisons passed . All 15 CTests in the OpenFHE-enabled build passed. The deterministic reduced-level fixture found 524,288 residues that distinguish the wrong per-digit reduction order. Every comparison checks all 65536 entries of every involved tower, plus modulus/root order and outer/child format. Intermediate conversion states are observed by calling the same public primitive separately; the tests do not instrument or modify OpenFHE's implementation. Full-core equality checks the composition against the independent formulas. Run the opt-in gate after installing pinned OpenFHE : cmake -S . -B build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -R 'openfhe_' --output-on-failure The Hybrid gate links OpenFHE PKE in addition to core. The standalone build still has no OpenFHE dependency. The setup script already builds all modules. 8. Work counts and the next boundary ¶ For one core call, counting transforms per tower rather than per launch: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Digit INTTs}&=L,\\\\ \\text{Complement NTTs}&=\\beta_\\ell(L+K)-L,\\\\ \\text{ModDown INTTs}&=2K,\\\\ \\text{ModDown NTTs}&=2L. \\end{aligned} \\] At M=L=5, K=3, α=3, β=2, this is 11 inverse and 21 forward NTTs . At L=4 it is 10 inverse and 18 forward. These are logical counts from the source graph, not GPU launch counts or measured performance. With 8-byte residues and N=65536, the assembled digits request \\(8N\\beta_\\ell(L+K)\\) bytes and the two QP accumulators request \\(16N(L+K)\\) bytes. Each is 8 MiB at full fixture level. Keys, temporary coefficient buffers, parameters, allocator overhead, and simultaneous lifetime choices are additional. D9 specifies the proposed lifetimes ; an implementation and allocation measurements are still needed before claiming a peak-memory bound. D8 freezes this source graph and the exercised array boundary. D10's ADR accepts it together with the D9 device/buffer/stream/cache design , closing P1's architecture gate while leaving runtime implementation and native validation open. P4/P6 must broaden base-conversion and cryptographic fixtures across the production parameter/level/key matrix. This is not a GPU implementation, performance result, or complete CKKS encryption/decryption integration gate. Pinned source references ¶ Hybrid key generation and ordinary/delayed core . ApproxSwitchCRTBasis and ApproxModDown . Partition construction and level-specific conversion tables . Relinearization and ciphertext component accumulation . CKKS extended fast rotation ordering ."}, {"title": "Device backend contract", "summary": "D9: checked buffer views, stream dependencies, resource leases, cache identities, and the proposed OpenFHE adapter.", "group": "Learn", "url": "device-backend-contract.html", "text": "Device buffers and the backend boundary ¶ D9 · API proposal with executable metadata checks · N=65536 A polynomial on the GPU needs more than a pointer and a length. Its ordered primes, roots, active level, representation, producing operation, and lifetime must travel together. D9 defines that boundary and proposes an opt-in OpenFHE format-conversion adapter as the first integration seam. Read D7's representation contract for the array convention and D8's Hybrid graph for Q/P transitions. The D5 buffer map describes the existing synchronous implementation. 1. What D9 delivers ¶ Artifact State after D9 Immutable parameter snapshot, layouts, views, cache identities and dependency checks Implemented as a portable host metadata prototype Backend, immutable polynomial, stream and completion interfaces Compile-checked abstract proposal; no runtime factory OpenFHE metadata bridge Checked against actual D8 Hybrid outputs and evaluation-key parameters CUDA allocations, streams, events, asynchronous copies and cache eviction Proposed lifecycle; not implemented by these headers OpenFHE adapter and device dispatch Proposed first seam; implementation follows acceptance and native GPU gates The prototype lives in backend_contract.hpp , with interfaces in backend_api.hpp and tests in backend_contract_tests.cpp . The ntt::proposal namespace makes its status explicit. These headers introduce no CUDA or OpenFHE dependency into the standalone build. The current CUDA wrapper and its algorithms were unchanged by D9. Later offline preparation adds baseline ownership and preflight fixes without implementing this proposed resident backend. D10 accepted this design in ADR 0001 ; P1 is complete. The article records the D9 proposal and its implementation limits. The ADR adds fixed initial budgets, admission rules and partial-submission cleanup, with an implementation backlog . A metadata check cannot prove a physical allocation, an event wait, or native GPU correctness. 2. Carry the complete polynomial identity ¶ The immutable Parameters object owns a deep copy of: ring dimension N; full, ordered Q and P lists, each containing the exact modulus and primitive 2N-th root; full-context digit width α, which must not be recomputed after dropping Q towers. Its factory rejects invalid dimensions, composite or duplicate moduli, invalid roots, and invalid digit geometry. For these power-of-two rings, the root test is ψᴺ = −1 mod q; together with prime q and q ≡ 1 mod 2N, it establishes order 2N. The semantic bound is q < 2⁶³. The initial device shape is narrower: N=65536 and canonical 50–60-bit primes. A valid shape still needs an available backend and an operation verified on the selected GPU. Alternative primitive roots can be mathematically valid metadata. The first OpenFHE adapter must import the exact existing roots and respect D7's shared FTT-cache constraint; support for conflicting custom-root contexts is not implied. Each PolynomialLayout adds the active Q count L, an explicit ordered list of TowerRef {Q or P, index} , domain, order, and residue encoding. M is the full Q count; K is the P count. The full parameter snapshot remains attached at reduced levels. Field Initial supported contract Coefficient domain Natural coefficient order Evaluation domain OpenFHE-compatible bit-reversed slot order Residues Canonical uint64_t , 0 ≤ x < q for each tower Packing Tower-major; each selected tower has exactly N consecutive residues Device identity Device ordinal plus backend-session identity Allocation identity Registry ID plus generation; never an OpenFHE object address Montgomery and lazy residues are explicit enum values but rejected by this prototype. They cannot enter the canonical kernels under a different label. The host-value validator checks the exact element count and every residue. An adapter must verify the outer DCRT format and each tower's format, dimension, modulus and root before constructing this layout; relabeling data is not conversion. Forward requires coefficient input and returns evaluation layout. Inverse requires evaluation input and returns coefficient layout, including the existing N-inverse normalization. Pointwise multiplication requires identical evaluation layouts. Parameter equality compares complete values, not hash equality alone. 3. Make views explicit and bounded ¶ A future private registry owns allocation records. A public view carries an allocation ID/generation, first tower, tower count, and active Q count. It exposes no raw device pointer. Validation checks a live matching generation, device and session, allocation capacity, legal whole-tower extent, and layout consistency. Byte counts use checked multiplication. \\[ \\operatorname{offset}(t,i)=8(tN+i),\\qquad \\operatorname{bytes}(T)=8NT. \\] At N=65536, one tower is 524,288 bytes, or 0.5 MiB . Whole-tower subviews are immutable aliases that retain the allocation and its readiness completion. Arbitrary coefficient strides and partial-tower views are deferred. A reduced QP basis is a particularly important boundary: Basis Logical order Indices in the full five-Q/three-P key Full QP, L=5 q₀ q₁ q₂ q₃ q₄ p₀ p₁ p₂ 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Active QP, L=4 q₀ q₁ q₂ q₃ p₀ p₁ p₂ 0 1 2 3 5 6 7 Shortening the full allocation to seven towers produces q₀…q₄,p₀,p₁, which is wrong. The validator rejects that attempted L=4 view. Materialize active QP in a fresh contiguous allocation using the explicit tower map. A Q prefix alone can use a zero-copy prefix view; the full key's P suffix can also be viewed without copying. For initial Hybrid execution, gathering the active key into a packed immutable snapshot is the simple proposed option; strided key access requires a separately accepted kernel contract. The proposal returns fresh output allocations for forward, inverse and pointwise operations. Inputs are immutable, and output aliasing is rejected. Existing kernels may still mutate a private output or private scratch buffer. An initial implementation may therefore need an extra device-to-device copy; that cost must appear in benchmarks. Public in-place operations wait for a separate alias and concurrency design. 4. Submit work with an explicit completion ¶ Backend proposes create_stream , upload , forward , inverse , pointwise , subview , download , and close . Submit carries an owned stream lease and owned dependency handles. PendingPolynomial couples an immutable result handle with its completion. This is the intended usage, not a runnable backend: Submit queue{backend.create_stream(), {}}; auto resident = backend.upload(coefficient_layout, host_values, queue); auto transformed = backend.forward(resident.value, queue); auto copy = backend.download(transformed.value, queue); auto evaluation_values = copy->wait_values(); The upload must validate and copy the borrowed host span into owned staging before returning. Download owns its host destination and exposes values only after successful completion. The same stream orders this example; a consumer on another stream must explicitly depend on the producer's completion. Producer state Consumer rule Pending, same stream FIFO stream order establishes the dependency Pending, different stream Queue a wait for the exact producer event and generation Succeeded Consumption is allowed after actual event success is established Failed Reject the consumer and propagate the producer error Different device or backend session Reject; peer transfer is not part of this API Every submitted operation receives a monotonically increasing ID in its backend session. Only earlier submitted completions can be dependencies; callers cannot manufacture future events or introduce cyclic waits. Each completion owns one immutable event instance. Re-recording an event behind an existing completion handle is forbidden. The host prototype checks metadata supplied as trusted registry records. The runtime must resolve every handle through its private registry, refresh event status, and issue actual cudaStreamWaitEvent calls. Passing the prototype a fabricated record does not create a CUDA event or prove synchronization. 5. Keep resources alive through completion ¶ Each submitted operation pins its input allocations, output allocation, stream, plans, keys, host staging, and scratch leases. The backend retains these pins even when the caller drops the returned completion. Dropping a handle is not cancellation. Private scratch is reusable only after every operation that can touch it has completed successfully or device quiescence is proven. The proposed lifecycle is: Validate all metadata, capability and dependency handles before submission. Reserve output/scratch and cache leases under the appropriate locks. Submit work and record a completion event; retain all pins in an operation record. On success, publish readiness and release operation pins. On failure, mark the result failed and quarantine affected resources until the stream/device is drained or torn down safely. A failed event is not evidence that the device has stopped using memory. The can_retire prototype permits retirement only with zero external leases, zero in-flight pins, and either successful readiness or proven quiescence. Runtime pin counts are registry facts, not guesses from shared_ptr::use_count . A budget exhausted by pinned entries produces backpressure or a reported allocation failure; it never permits eviction of a live entry. The backend state should use unique_ptr<Impl> , move-only allocation owners, and a device guard on every runtime call. Constructors must unwind partial allocation safely. close() stops submission, drains work, reports errors and invalidates further use; destructors are nonthrowing. Outstanding leases retain backend state until safe release. An unrecoverable context failure needs a quarantine/teardown policy rather than immediate pool reuse. D5 identified a raw- Impl* constructor-failure leak in the existing wrapper. This proposal specifies the replacement discipline. Subsequent offline preparation fixes baseline ownership with unique_ptr and device guards; native allocation-failure injection and teardown tests remain in the accepted implementation backlog. 6. Cache semantic identities, then pin leases ¶ Caches belong to one backend session and device. A content hash can accelerate lookup, but a hit must compare the complete semantic key. Object pointers, ring size alone, or OpenFHE key tags alone are insufficient. Cache Required identity NTT plans/tables Device/session, N, exact ordered q/ψ pairs, encoding, table convention, arithmetic revision Digit conversion plans Full parameter context including α, L, digit index, ordered source/complement bases, conversion-rule revision Evaluation-key material Device/session, full context, immutable material ID/generation, key tag, key kind and index, packing revision The NTT plan bundles forward/inverse powers and normalization constants. Its current table convention is natural ψ powers including the N endpoint. It cannot reuse an OpenFHE bit-reversed FTT table as if the two layouts were equal. A forward/inverse plan may share identity because both directions are included; the operand's domain still controls which operation is valid. For digit d, the conversion-plan validator derives its contiguous Q partition using the full α, then verifies the exact ordered active-Q/P complement. Its rule revision includes D8's uncentered, unreduced approximate CRT sum . A canonical-CRT plan is a different operation. ModDown needs a distinct plan kind for its P→active-Q conversion and P-inverse scale; the prototype's ConversionPlanKey covers digit extension only. Evaluation keys are deep immutable A/B snapshots; secret keys are not uploaded. A material ID/generation is assigned to that snapshot. Mutating the original OpenFHE key requires a new snapshot and identity. A rotation index or relinearization component is part of the identity; equal tags do not establish equal key data. Packing validates digit count, full QP order and every residue. On a cache miss, a future implementation publishes one pending entry under a mutex and shares its readiness event with concurrent requests. This single upload must be waited on across streams. Readers pin the entry through their own completion. A budgeted LRU can evict only unpinned, quiescent entries. The proposal contains equality/validation rules and retirement checks, not an implemented cache, uploader, hash table or LRU policy. The abstract API exposes cache_stats() , set_cache_budget(bytes) , and trim_cache() . Statistics count allocated cache bytes including pending uploads, with pinned bytes counted once per entry; operand/scratch pools are separate. Budget is reserved before allocation. Lowering the budget evicts eligible entries first and fails if live pins prevent the requested bound. Trimming returns only bytes actually freed. ADR 0001 selects the initial budget and admission policy; their runtime implementation is pending. Hoisted digits are explicit immutable results, not an implicit global cache. Their provenance includes the complete parameter snapshot, source allocation ID/generation, complete active-Q view, domain/order and rule revision. The prototype rejects stale or incompatible provenance. Reuse across rotation keys is permitted only when that input/context is unchanged and each key matches the same Q/P/α context; the digits do not depend on a particular key's A/B data. A private registry must also validate that the referenced source exists and has the claimed layout. 7. Budget the Hybrid lifetimes ¶ D8 fixes the graph; D9 assigns the ownership boundaries. Digit coefficient scratch lives through its complement conversion. Each assembled evaluation digit lives through every consuming B/A MAC. QP accumulators live through ModDown or an explicitly delayed QP consumer. P coefficient scratch and Q correction buffers live through the final correction transforms and subtraction. Keys and tables remain pinned by all readers. For the full D8 fixture M=L=5, K=3, α=3, β=2 at N=65536: Storage Logical size Lifetime Input Q polynomial 2.5 MiB Upload through last decomposition reader Two assembled QP digits 8 MiB Hoist result through last MAC reader Two QP accumulators 8 MiB MAC through last ModDown/delayed reader Two final Q outputs 5 MiB Returned immutable outputs Full two-digit A/B key 16 MiB Cached snapshot plus in-flight reader pins The sizes follow 8NT bytes per T-tower polynomial. They are logical payloads, not a measured or proven peak allocation bound. Streaming digits can release scratch earlier; preserving a hoist for several rotations keeps all digits alive. Allocator alignment, twiddle tables, host staging, concurrent streams, conversion scratch and out-of-place copies add memory. D10 accepts explicit budgets and fail-fast admission in ADR 0001; P2/P3 must measure actual live bytes. 8. Integrate OpenFHE through a transactional adapter ¶ The proposed first seam is an explicit opt-in DCRT format-conversion adapter with SetFormat-equivalent behavior. It does not replace OpenFHE's global SwitchFormat or patch every NativePoly call. This small boundary tests exact tower packing, transform ordering, ownership and failure handling before Hybrid GPU integration adds conversion and key-material lifetimes. The host operation is synchronous even if its internal backend is asynchronous: Snapshot the DCRT context and validate all tower metadata, labels and values. If the requested format already matches, return without conversion. During preflight, select the device route or an explicitly permitted CPU route. Pack each tower using ConvertToInt() into canonical tower-major storage. Do not reinterpret OpenFHE native integer objects as a flat uint64_t* . Upload, transform, download and wait. Import into a private candidate object, set each tower's actual format and the DCRT format, and verify metadata. Replace the caller's object only after the entire candidate succeeds. The caller must serialize access to the same host object during the operation. The candidate preserves strong exception safety: a failed upload, launch, completion, download or import leaves the original host object unchanged. This is a proposed guarantee; it requires adapter and fault-injection tests. Situation RequireDevice AllowCpuBeforeSubmit Invalid metadata, residues or format labels Error Error Already in the requested format, valid input No-op No-op Available, supported and verified device operation Device Device Device unavailable, valid unsupported shape, or unverified operation Error CPU before submission Any failure after submission Propagate error Propagate error CPU fallback uses a private host candidate too. No automatic CPU retry occurs after device submission. A caller may explicitly retry the unchanged original after handling the backend error. An unverified CUDA operation cannot advertise production support merely because a kernel compiles; a diagnostic harness can run the native exactness gates to establish that capability. 9. Verification and D10 acceptance ¶ backend_contract_check runs in the normal CPU build. It checks valid and invalid roots/parameters, immutable snapshots, domain transitions, canonical host values, byte overflow, reduced-QP mistakes, view ranges, stale generations, aliasing, stream dependencies, failed-resource retirement, cache identities, hoist provenance, and preflight-only fallback. The opt-in openfhe_hybrid_graph additionally imports real pinned OpenFHE parameters at L=5,4,3,1 and checks the proposed active QP layout and key indices against actual Hybrid outputs and full-context key towers. It retains D8's 18 cases and exact residue-array comparisons. .venv/bin/cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j2 .venv/bin/ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build --output-on-failure -j2 D10's accepted ADR records these decisions: the immutable, out-of-place initial API and its device-to-device copy cost; the DCRT format adapter as the first seam and its preflight-only fallback; private-registry provenance, resource pins and error quarantine; cache upload coordination, memory budget and backpressure; the ordered implementation backlog: RAII/failure cleanup, native exactness, resident NTT backend, adapter tests, then conversion/Hybrid integration. Required runtime tests include partial-constructor failure, wrong device, concurrent cache misses, missing event waits, completion abandonment, cache pressure with live readers, asynchronous failure and drain, and host rollback. None is replaced by metadata simulation. Real CUDA execution, complete Hybrid GPU support, CKKS application integration and performance remain later gates."}, {"title": "Run & debug", "summary": "Run the N=8 example and step through each butterfly in VS Code.", "group": "Learn", "url": "vscode-debugging.html", "text": "Run and debug the NTT in VS Code ¶ Run the N=8 CPU example on macOS, then step through its butterflies in VS Code. CUDA hardware is not required. One-time setup ¶ Open the repository folder in VS Code: code . If VS Code asks, install the recommended extensions: - CMake Tools - C/C++ - LLDB DAP Wait for CMake Tools to finish configuring build/debug . The workspace uses Xcode's lldb-dap ; check that Xcode command-line tools are installed. Run the small example ¶ Open the command palette with Cmd+Shift+P , choose Tasks: Run Task , then choose Run: NTT debug example . The expected output is: natural-order coefficients: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 bit-reversed evaluation values: 5 0 13 8 9 11 5 8 recovered coefficients: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 roundtrip passed Debug it step by step ¶ Open examples/step_by_step.cpp . Click left of the transform.forward(values) line to create a breakpoint. Open Run and Debug with Cmd+Shift+D . Select Debug: NTT example (step by step) . Press F5 . The program first stops at main because stopOnEntry is on. Press F5 again to reach the breakpoint. Press F11 to step into NegacyclicNtt::forward in include/ntt/ntt.hpp . Use F10 to advance one source line at a time. Useful values to add to the Watch panel: stage_pair_distance group_start_index pair_offset upper_index lower_index twiddle_power values For N=8 , the forward transform has three stages with pair distances 4 , 2 , and 1 . Expand values after each butterfly to see the array change in place. Other shared commands ¶ Cmd+Shift+B : configure and build the debug example. Test: All CPU checks : build and run the complete CPU test suite. Debug: CPU unit tests : generate the N=1024 fixture and debug the tests. The debugger deliberately targets the CPU reference. CUDA kernel debugging will be added on the RTX machine using cuda-gdb or Nsight after the native CUDA build is available. CodeLLDB crash on current macOS/Xcode ¶ The recorded CodeLLDB 1.11.4 / LLDB 19.1.7 setup crashed with SIGBUS on AppleClang symbols. Xcode lldb-dap stopped correctly at main . If an old lldb configuration appears, run Developer: Reload Window and select the lldb-dap configuration."}, {"title": "GPU NTT implementations", "summary": "Primary-source survey, performance limits, and techniques to evaluate.", "group": "Research", "url": "gpu-ntt-survey.html", "text": "GPU NTT implementations ¶ Date: 2026-11-05 Project target: CKKS, OpenFHE 1.6.1, Hybrid RNS key switching, NVIDIA RTX 5090 ( sm_120 ), ring dimension 65536 Current repository revision inspected: 363f972 Evidence policy: implementation facts below come only from author papers, official repositories/source code, or NVIDIA documentation. Recommendations and inferences are explicitly labelled. Executive conclusions ¶ Keep exact OpenFHE array compatibility while replacing three bottlenecks: modular division, strided column accesses, and repeated host transfers. Arithmetic: Shoup for fixed twiddles; Barrett/Montgomery for variable products. Layout: compare the baseline with a coalesced transpose/Four-Step path. Thread work: test 4/8 register-held values and fewer block barriers. Integration: keep buffers, keys, and the Hybrid Q → QP → Q pipeline on GPU. Evidence: use FIDESlib as the closest CKKS/OpenFHE comparison; measure complete operations as well as individual NTTs. These are recommendations from the sources below, not measured speedups for this repository. 1. Current implementation ¶ 1.1 Sourced facts from this repository ¶ The CUDA backend uses a negacyclic transform with folded twisting and the following schedule: Forward: DIF column pass followed by a DIF contiguous-tile pass. Inverse: DIT contiguous-tile pass followed by a DIT column pass and a separate scale kernel. Fixed split width and block size of 256. Limb-major storage [limb][coefficient] ; blockIdx.y selects the limb. One full table of powers of psi and psi^-1 , each [limb][N+1] . Two launches per forward transform for N > 256 ; three for inverse. Host upload and download on every public forward , inverse , and pointwise_multiply call; multiply at least keeps its two forward transforms, pointwise product, and inverse together on device. Device multiplication is currently (__uint128_t(a) * b) % q . CPU simulation shares the exact stage bodies with CUDA, and CUDA tests compare exact arrays to a CPU reference and Python big-integer oracle. Primary code: ntt_kernels.cu , dev_core.hpp , mod_arith.hpp , and cuda_ntt.hpp . 1.2 Strengths worth retaining ¶ Recommendation/inference: keep these properties through optimization: The exact mathematical contract: natural-order input, bit-reversed evaluation output, exact inverse, and exact equality to the CPU oracle. The shared simulator/device stage definitions where practical. Optimized arithmetic can sit behind a second policy while the reference policy remains unchanged. Limb-major RNS storage as the baseline. It maps naturally to one modulus per independent transform, pointwise CKKS arithmetic, and OpenFHE's DCRT-tower model. Precomputation at context construction rather than in a hot operation. Named-hardware benchmark provenance and automatic correctness gates. 1.3 Immediate limitations ¶ Observed source behavior Recommended experiment mul_mod uses 128-bit remainder Division-free Shoup and Barrett policies Column threads load a[r + 256*t] : 2,048-byte spacing Coalesced transpose/Four-Step; measure sectors per requested byte Full power table and a barrier after every radix-2 stage Packed twiddles, Shoup quotients, radix-4/8 register work Fixed 256-thread split Tune launch shape for N=65536 and real tower/batch counts Host vectors and synchronous copies Add device views, stream ownership, and reusable scratch Keep the reference path as an oracle. Four logical array movements alone do not establish efficient DRAM access. 2. Libraries and techniques ¶ Reference Technique to evaluate FIDESlib Exact OpenFHE interoperability, device state, transform-boundary fusion GPU-NTT / HEonGPU Merge versus Four-Step schedules, batches, streams, memory pools CKKS-GPU-CORE Eight values per thread and padded shared memory PhantomFHE Radix-8, lazy Shoup ranges, key-switch fusion TensorFHE Operation batching; tensor arithmetic is a separate research path ICICLE Explicit ordering, device domains, and asynchronous APIs cuFHE / cuHE Resident keys and stream-aware operations in a different FHE scheme Theodosian RTX 5090 cache/write-bandwidth limits and fusion tradeoffs Read the implementation evidence and source links 2.1 FIDESlib: the closest match to the project target ¶ FIDESlib describes itself as a server-side CKKS GPU library interoperable with OpenFHE. Its current release states support for OpenFHE 1.6.1, Hybrid key switching, CUDA 12/13, and sm_70 through sm_120 ; it also states that GPU operations are compared bit-for-bit to a patched OpenFHE CPU reference. These are precisely this project's scheme, integration, key-switch, and GPU targets. See the official README and its bit-compatibility design record . Its current 1D NTT uses CT butterflies for forward and GS butterflies for inverse, shared-memory staging, warp-level synchronization below a warp, vectorized 16-byte loads/stores, and Shoup/Barrett arithmetic selected by templates. More importantly, the NTT mode is templated to fuse downstream work including rescale, plaintext multiplication, ModDown, and key-switch dot/accumulate operations. See NTT_1D.cu , INTT_1D.cu , NTTfusions.cuh , and ModMult.cuh . FIDESlib's Hybrid pipeline has dedicated decomposition/ModUp and ModDown kernels, per-device limb partitions, CUDA streams/events, and graph capture in its multi-GPU/scheduling layer. Its high-level RNS object tracks whether a polynomial is in the extended basis and exposes fused key-switch operations. See Conv.cu , LimbPartition.cu , and RNSPoly.cpp . What to absorb: Make exact OpenFHE ciphertext compatibility—not only decrypt-level CKKS tolerance—the integration acceptance target where the same algorithmic formulation is used. Model transform state explicitly: coefficient/evaluation order, normal/extended RNS basis, level, and owning device/stream. Create fusion points at transform boundaries. A generic forward() that always stores a complete intermediate is insufficient for high-performance key switching. Use Shoup constants beside twiddle and key constants. Keep generic Barrett reduction for operands whose multiplier is not precomputed. Test the complete Hybrid formulations. FIDESlib's compatibility record shows that two mathematically valid rotation/key-switch formulations need not produce bit-identical ciphertexts because rounding and noise enter in different orders. Preserve full QP towers during export/import. FIDESlib documents a real compatibility bug caused by truncating extended-basis ciphertexts during materialization. Inference: FIDESlib is both a design reference and a competitor baseline. Before designing a new OpenFHE patch layer, evaluate whether this project's intended contribution is (a) independent kernels integrated directly into stock OpenFHE, (b) an alternative backend with a stable ABI, or (c) improvements that can be contributed to FIDESlib/OpenFHE. Duplicating its entire compatibility layer without a differentiator is high effort. 2.2 GPU-NTT / HEonGPU: Merge NTT and Four-Step NTT ¶ Özcan and Savaş present two GPU algorithms: an iterative “Merge NTT” and a Four-Step NTT. Their target ranges include 2^12..2^16 for HE and larger sizes for proving. They optimize kernel count and block shape for coalesced global access, and report both single-transform latency and concurrent-transform throughput. The paper reports that the iterative form can outperform Four-Step for HE throughput despite Four-Step's locality. See the author paper, Two Algorithms for Fast GPU Implementation of NTT , especially its design and evaluation sections. The current official GPU-NTT repository supports 32- and 64-bit Barrett arithmetic, rejecting moduli above 30 and 61 bits respectively. It specializes ring sizes at compile time, uses per-size kernel configuration tables, supports RNS transforms and batches, accepts CUDA streams, and has different low-ring and multi-stage paths. See the official repository , ntt_launch_impl.cuh , and modular_arith.cuh . HEonGPU uses GPU-NTT inside a GPU-resident BFV/CKKS library. Its paper describes a GPU memory pool, pinned host pool, and multi-stream execution in addition to the NTT itself. See HEonGPU and the official repository . What to absorb: Compare at least two schedules: an improved two-pass/Merge path and a Four-Step or explicit transpose path. Do not assume the current fixed split is optimal. Build a launch table for N=65536 across tower and batch counts; tune it on the 5090. Treat batches as part of the NTT API, not a benchmark wrapper. CKKS naturally supplies many modulus towers and often multiple ciphertext components. Report throughput as well as latency; the best shape changes when enough independent transforms exist to saturate the GPU. Add an allocator and stream contract at the library boundary. 2.3 CKKS-GPU-CORE: memory-centric CKKS and 8-point-per-thread NTT ¶ Jung et al.'s memory-centric CKKS work keeps up to eight NTT values per thread in registers, exchanges through shared memory between local subtransforms, and pads shared memory to reduce bank conflicts. Its basis conversion launches across output limbs and coefficients and accumulates input-limb products before Barrett reduction. See Over 100x Faster Bootstrapping in Fully Homomorphic Encryption through Memory-Centric Optimization with GPUs and the official CKKS-GPU-CORE repository . The source implements two NTT phases, register arrays of eight coefficients per thread, Shoup butterflies, padded shared memory, batched ModUp, and fused ModDown/NTT epilogues. See NttImple.cu and Context.cu . What to absorb: Evaluate radix-8-per-thread as a direct evolution of this repository's two-pass structure: keep eight coefficients in registers, use shared memory only for cross-thread permutations, and reduce full-block barriers. Add padding and profile bank conflicts rather than assuming the [256] shared tile is conflict-free for every access pattern. Co-design basis conversion and NTT buffers. The same extended-basis layout should flow directly into forward NTT and evaluation-key multiplication. Fuse inverse-transform epilogues and forward-transform prologues where the Hybrid formula permits. 2.4 PhantomFHE: radix-8 NWT, lazy Shoup butterflies, and scheme-level fusion ¶ Phantom implements BFV, BGV, and CKKS on CUDA. Its 1D path handles small transforms in one block; its larger 2D path performs radix-8 work with eight register-held values per thread, padded shared-memory exchanges, and two phases. Forward CT butterflies use Shoup multiplication and lazy [0, 2q) / [0, 4q) ranges; inverse GS butterflies defer final reduction/scaling. See the official repository , ntt_1d.cu , fntt_2d.cu , and butterfly.cuh . The Phantom paper describes intra-kernel fusion that reuses register temporaries and inter-kernel fusion across operations such as NWT plus scaling and ModDown plus correction. Its key-switch path connects ModUp, evaluation-key inner product, and ModDown on a supplied stream. See Phantom: A CUDA-Accelerated Word-Wise Homomorphic Encryption Library and eval_key_switch.cu . What to absorb: Use CT forward and GS inverse so compatible orderings can eliminate explicit permutations between NTT, pointwise multiplication, and INTT. Define and prove lazy ranges per butterfly. Reductions are removed only when the next consumer's bound remains safe. Prefer register reuse and boundary fusion over simply increasing shared-memory tile size. Expose the CUDA stream all the way through key switching. 2.5 TensorFHE: tensor-core NTT and operation-level batching ¶ TensorFHE maps a decomposed 32-bit NTT to tensor-core matrix multiplications and emphasizes throughput rather than single-operation latency. It batches CKKS operations and changes layout from (batch, limb, N) to (limb, batch, N) so equal-limb data from different operations is contiguous. It also assigns independent matrix multiplications to separate streams. See the author paper, TensorFHE , especially Sections IV-C and IV-D. What to absorb: Operation-level batching and a layout selectable between [limb][batch][N] and [batch][limb][N] are worth measuring for inference serving. Throughput-oriented inference can benefit from batching several ciphertexts even if one ciphertext already batches all RNS towers. Inference/caution: do not make tensor-core NTT the first implementation path. TensorFHE's arithmetic decomposition and throughput objective differ from OpenFHE's usual 50–60-bit RNS towers and this project's immediate exact-compatibility goal. Establish a fast integer-core Shoup/Barrett backend first; treat tensor cores as a separate experimental backend. 2.6 ICICLE: useful API ideas, not a direct CKKS backend ¶ ICICLE's official NTT API exposes stream, batch size, row/column batch layout, input/output ordering, device-resident flags, async execution, reusable per-device domains, fast-twiddle mode, and an explicit mixed-radix selection. See the official NTT API . What to absorb: Separate an algorithm-independent NTT contract from backend-specific tuning extensions. Make ordering explicit in the type/configuration rather than relying on comments. Initialize and cache a maximum-size domain once, then reuse it for smaller transforms where mathematically compatible. Allow both row-batched and column-batched transforms and device-resident async operation. Inference/caution: ICICLE is primarily a proof-system field library, not an OpenFHE CKKS/Hybrid implementation. Its API concepts transfer; its field choices and transform semantics must not be assumed compatible. Its CUDA backend also has source-availability/licensing conditions that should be checked before any reuse. 2.7 cuFHE/cuHE: historically relevant but not the integration model ¶ cuFHE implements TFHE gates and credits cuHE's GPU NTT; its API supports CUDA streams and keeps a public bootstrapping key initialized on the GPU. See the official cuFHE repository and official cuHE repository . What to absorb: Keep large evaluation/bootstrapping keys resident and permit concurrent independent operations through streams. Inference/caution: TFHE gate bootstrapping has different polynomial sizes, dataflow, and key-switch structure from CKKS Hybrid RNS. cuFHE is not a suitable algorithmic baseline for the OpenFHE integration; it is mainly historical evidence for GPU-resident keys and stream-level concurrency. 2.8 Theodosian: RTX 5090 changes the optimization priority ¶ Theodosian profiles CKKS on RTX 5090 and reports that optimized NTT and basis-conversion kernels are L2-bandwidth-bound. Its NTT uses two kernels; on-the-fly twiddle generation trades arithmetic for fewer twiddle loads. It finds small limb counts underutilize the GPU, uses L2-aware batching until the working set approaches cache capacity, applies multi-polynomial caching and complementary pipelining to Hybrid key-switch stages, and uses CUDA Graphs to reduce launch overhead for static FHE graphs. See Theodosian . The paper reports that batching can hurt after the footprint exceeds the 5090's effective L2 capacity, and that key switching benefits more from cache-aware multi-polynomial execution and graph scheduling than from additional local kernel fusion once the baseline is already heavily fused. What to absorb: Profile L2 read/write throughput and SM-to-L2 traffic on the actual 5090, not only DRAM bandwidth and occupancy. Generate high-volume twiddles on the fly when the arithmetic cost is cheaper than their cache traffic; retain a precomputed mode for low-volume stages. Batch according to measured working-set size. “More batches” is not monotonic. Capture stable key-switch/rotation/inference sequences in CUDA Graphs. Pipeline stages with complementary DRAM/L2 behavior only after dependencies and cache interference are measured. 3. Algorithms and arithmetic ¶ 3.1 CT/GS versus Stockham and Four-Step ¶ Sourced observation: Phantom and FIDESlib use CT forward plus GS inverse; GPU-NTT compares iterative Merge and Four-Step schedules; ICICLE exposes mixed-radix and ordering choices. Recommendation: keep CT/DIF forward and GS/DIT inverse as the first optimized path because the current repository already has the equivalent ordering contract and no bit-reversal pass. Add a Four-Step/transpose prototype only as a benchmarked alternative for the strided column pass. A Stockham autosort path may offer regular accesses and natural ordering, but it normally ping-pongs global buffers; for the target sizes, its extra full-array traffic must beat the cost of the current uncoalesced gather before adoption. 3.2 Mixed radix and register staging ¶ Sourced observation: CKKS-GPU-CORE and Phantom use up to eight values per thread and radix-8 local transforms; GPU-NTT notes higher-radix butterflies as a further optimization. Recommendation: implement radix-4 first, then radix-8: Load 4/8 values per thread using coalesced vectorized transactions. Execute the local stages in registers. Store into padded shared memory for the permutation between local groups. Use warp shuffles where partner lanes remain within a warp. Store once at the end of the phase. This directly reduces table loads, barriers, and shared-memory operations relative to one butterfly per thread per stage. 3.3 Modular multiplication ¶ Sourced observation: GPU-NTT uses Barrett with explicit modulus-bit bounds; Phantom and CKKS-GPU-CORE use Shoup values for twiddles and lazy butterflies; FIDESlib provides Shoup and Barrett paths. Recommendation: use three explicit primitives rather than one universal mul_mod : mul_const_shoup(x, w, w_shoup, q) for twiddles, N^-1 , basis constants, and evaluation-key constants when precomputation is useful. mul_var_barrett(a, b, q, mu) or a benchmarked Montgomery alternative for pointwise variable products. reduce_wide(acc, q, mu) for basis-conversion/key-switch accumulators with a proved accumulator bound. Retain the current __uint128_t % q only as host/device test oracle. Test each optimized primitive exhaustively around 0 , q-1 , lazy bounds, and the maximum supported OpenFHE prime size. 3.4 Twiddle representation ¶ Recommendation/inference: replace [psi^0, ..., psi^N] as the only device representation with: stage/phase-major twiddles in the order consumed by warps; a parallel Shoup quotient table; optional compact “seed” twiddles for on-the-fly expansion in the phase with O(N) table traffic; a metadata record containing transform convention, direction, modulus, ordering, and supported lazy range. The CPU reference can continue to build a simple power table; a packing step should produce the device format and be independently cross-checked. 4. RNS layout and Hybrid state ¶ 4.1 Baseline layout ¶ Recommendation: keep coefficient data limb-major: poly[component][digit-or-batch][limb][coefficient] with coefficient contiguous. This gives coalesced coefficient loads for one limb and makes modulus-specific constants uniform across a block. Provide views/strides so operation-level batching can switch the middle dimensions without copying. 4.2 Extended-basis state ¶ Recommendation: make these states explicit and non-interchangeable: Q, coefficient Q, evaluation QP, coefficient QP, evaluation Each buffer view should carry active Q tower count, P tower count, level, ordering, device, and stream/event ownership. This prevents accidental truncation or redundant transforms at the OpenFHE boundary. 4.3 Target Hybrid pipeline ¶ Recommendation/inference: design the first end-to-end kernel graph around OpenFHE's actual Hybrid formulation, approximately: input c2 in Q/evaluation -> digit partition / inverse NTT as required -> Q-digit to QP basis extension -> forward NTT of extended digits -> evaluation-key multiply-accumulate into two QP outputs -> approximate ModDown QP -> Q -> add/fold c0 as required by the exact OpenFHE operation The exact transform placement and rounding order must be derived from the pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1 source and frozen in compatibility tests. Do not substitute an algebraically equivalent formulation if bit equality is the goal. 4.4 Fusion candidates ¶ High-value, low-ambiguity experiments: INTT final scaling + decomposition pre-scale. Basis-extension output + first forward-NTT load/prologue. Forward-NTT final store + evaluation-key dot product when register and occupancy costs permit. INTT or NTT boundary + ModDown correction. Pointwise accumulation across Hybrid digits, deferring reduction only under a proved bound. Caution: fusion can lower occupancy by increasing registers/shared memory and can prevent cache-friendly scheduling. Theodosian shows additional fusion is not automatically beneficial on RTX 5090. Require profiler evidence for every fused variant. 5. Streams and memory ¶ NVIDIA documents that asynchronous host/device copies require pinned host memory and can overlap with kernels in other streams. It also documents global-to-shared asynchronous copies and pipelines, and states that CUDA Graphs reduce repeated launch setup cost for fixed workflows. See the CUDA Best Practices Guide , asynchronous copy documentation , and CUDA Graphs documentation . Recommended API split: NttContext : immutable moduli, roots, packed twiddles, reduction constants, launch table, and allocator handle. DeviceRnsView : pointer(s), dimensions/strides, basis/domain/order metadata, device, and readiness event. enqueue_forward/inverse(view, stream) : no allocation, no synchronization, no host copy. convenience host methods: pinned staging plus explicit or documented synchronization. reusable scratch arena sized for maximum supported level/digit count. captured CUDA graphs for fixed ring dimension, level, digit count, and operation sequence. Recommendation/inference: try cuda::memcpy_async /pipeline only after the coalesced phase layout is fixed. The current column gather has no useful sequential “next tile” per block to hide without first changing data ownership; async copy cannot repair uncoalesced addresses. 6. Correctness and measurement ¶ 6.1 Correctness ladder ¶ Arithmetic primitive versus __uint128_t % q , including adversarial bounds and every OpenFHE modulus. Butterfly and single-stage exact tests for CT/DIF and GS/DIT, including lazy-range invariants. Complete forward/inverse exact equality against the existing CPU and Python oracle for every N in {65536} . RNS batches across realistic CKKS tower counts, not only three limbs. Negacyclic polynomial multiplication exact equality. Basis conversion exact equality to the pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1 implementation. Hybrid relinearization and rotation: exact ciphertext comparison where formulation and code generation permit; decrypt-error comparison as a secondary semantic test. Long operation sequences and inference circuits, including level/scale/noise metadata and repeated rotations. 6.2 Required performance views ¶ For each ring dimension, modulus bit-width, and tower count: kernel-only forward and inverse latency using CUDA events; end-to-end latency with pinned and pageable host transfer reported separately; transforms/second for batches of towers, ciphertext components, and independent ciphertexts; full polynomial multiply latency; Hybrid key switch, relinearization, and rotation latency; complete inference latency and peak device memory; bytes read/written, achieved DRAM bandwidth, L2 read/write throughput, global load/store efficiency, bank conflicts, register count, occupancy, and launch count from Nsight Compute/Systems; cold setup, warm steady state, and CUDA Graph replay as distinct measurements. 6.3 Baselines ¶ This repository's current pre-Barrett kernel, labelled clearly as a correctness baseline. Optimized kernel with identical transfers. GPU-NTT or another standalone GPU NTT with matched modulus/order/batch where license and semantics permit. FIDESlib 2.2/OpenFHE 1.6.1 for NTT microbenchmarks and, more importantly, full Hybrid key switch/rotation. Stock OpenFHE 1.6.1 CPU, with build flags and thread count recorded. Do not compare one implementation's kernel-only time with another's transfer-inclusive time, nor one-limb latency with an entire RNS polynomial. 7. Priorities ¶ Priority Deliverable P0 Real-GPU exact checks, arithmetic policies, device-buffer/stream contract P1 Shoup/Barrett kernels, register staging, coalesced layout, measured dispatch P2 Explicit Q/QP state, exact basis conversion, resident keys, safe fusion P3 Hoisting, operation batching, graph replay, whole-inference profiling The integration plan tracks completion; the benchmark plan defines the workload and acceptance record. The priorities here do not mark a roadmap gate complete. 8. Licensing and provenance ¶ This section is informational, not legal advice. FIDESlib: MIT license in its official repository. Its OpenFHE interoperability currently depends on patches, which should be reviewed separately for upstream/license provenance. GPU-NTT: Apache-2.0. HEonGPU: check the repository's current license before copying code; concepts described by the paper can be independently implemented. PhantomFHE: GPLv3. Copying its source into a differently licensed project can impose copyleft obligations. Use the paper and behavior as design evidence unless the project intentionally adopts compatible licensing. CKKS-GPU-CORE: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 in the repository. The non-commercial/share-alike terms make direct code reuse unsuitable for many projects. Independently implement paper-level ideas and preserve clean-room provenance. ICICLE CUDA backend: source availability and the backend license have changed across releases. Verify the exact version's terms before any reuse. cuFHE/cuHE: their official repositories state their licenses; verify individual files before reuse. Recommendation: continue the repository's clean-room policy. Cite papers and inspect behavior/interfaces, but write original kernels. If code is adapted from MIT/Apache sources, preserve required notices and record the exact file/commit. Do not copy GPL or CC BY-NC-SA kernel code unless the project's license decision explicitly permits it. 9. Next implementation steps ¶ The survey supports the following immediate implementation sequence: Introduce arithmetic policies and implement ShoupTwiddle plus BarrettVariable , with exact tests. Add a device-buffer/stream API without removing the current host-vector facade. Instrument and benchmark the existing two-pass kernel on RTX 5090 using CUDA events and Nsight. Implement one radix-8, padded-shared-memory variant and one coalesced transpose/Four-Step variant. Select per-size/per-batch winners through a dispatch table. Build the Q/QP state/layout and OpenFHE 1.6.1 compatibility harness before implementing base conversion. Optimize and report the full Hybrid key-switch graph, with FIDESlib as the central GPU baseline. Benchmarking and autotuning across GPU parameters and implementation variants are a permanent project workstream, not a one-time comparison. The concrete matrix, required counters, reproducibility record, dispatch rules, and acceptance gates are defined in benchmark-tuning-plan.md . The HTML explainer should then be updated to show both the current correctness schedule and the selected production schedule. It should no longer imply that low nominal global byte count alone means efficient VRAM access; it should visualize coalescing, register-held radix-8 work, padded shared memory, and the complete Q -> QP -> Q Hybrid dataflow. Primary sources ¶ Papers, repositories, and NVIDIA documentation Özcan and Savaş, Two Algorithms for Fast GPU Implementation of NTT ; GPU-NTT source . Özcan and Savaş, HEonGPU ; HEonGPU source . Yang et al., Phantom ; PhantomFHE source . Jung et al., Over 100x Faster Bootstrapping through Memory-Centric Optimization with GPUs ; CKKS-GPU-CORE source . Fan et al., TensorFHE . Agulló-Domingo et al., FIDESlib source and documentation . Choi et al., Theodosian . Ingonyama, ICICLE NTT API . Vernam Lab, cuFHE and cuHE . NVIDIA, CUDA C++ Programming Guide , CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide , and Nsight Compute documentation ."}, {"title": "Choosing the GPU", "summary": "Compare hardware for exact u32/u64 modular arithmetic and CKKS workloads.", "group": "Research", "url": "gpu-selection-survey.html", "text": "Choosing a GPU for CKKS ¶ Hardware assessment recorded on 2026-11-06. The RTX 5090 choice is provisional; production selection requires the benchmark gate below. Specifications and historical prices are reference values, not current quotes or kernel timings. Decision ¶ Role GPU Reason Primary development RTX 5090 32 GB, 1.792 TB/s bandwidth, Blackwell sm_120 Compatibility control RTX 4090 Ada sm_89 ; useful when owned or rented More capacity / enterprise support RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96 GB ECC; evaluate when the working set exceeds 32 GB Later production comparison H200 / B200 Larger HBM and bandwidth; benchmark the complete workload Use the 5090 for development. Select a production GPU only after measuring resident memory and end-to-end Hybrid key switching. Why FP8 and FP16 marketing does not answer our question ¶ These kernels perform exact modular u32 / u64 arithmetic and move RNS data. Tensor TOPS do not measure that workload. NVIDIA's Tensor Core type table does not list arbitrary u32 / u64 modular operations. Relevant primitives include mul.hi , mul.lo , and mul.wide and __umul64hi . The native instruction table does not publish a directly comparable 64-bit multiply throughput. Measure Shoup/Barrett instruction chains rather than deriving an “INT64 TOPS” ranking. Official specification comparison ¶ Bandwidth is a peak specification. INT32 is published non-Tensor throughput, not modular multiplication performance. “—” means no comparable value was published in the cited product material. GPU Memory Peak bandwidth L2 CC INT32 TOPS Power RTX 5090 32 GB GDDR7 1.792 TB/s 96 MB 12.0 104.8 575 W RTX 4090 24 GB GDDR6X 1.008 TB/s 72 MB 8.9 41.3 450 W RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96 GB ECC GDDR7 1.792 TB/s 128 MB 12.0 126 600 W L40S 48 GB ECC GDDR6 864 GB/s Not stated 8.9 — 350 W A100 80 GB SXM 80 GB HBM2e 2.039 TB/s 40 MB 8.0 19.5 400 W standard H100 SXM 80 GB HBM3 3.35 TB/s 50 MB 9.0 30 Up to 700 W H200 SXM 141 GB HBM3e 4.8 TB/s 50 MB 9.0 — Up to 700 W B200 SXM 180 GB HBM3e Up to 8 TB/s 126 MB 10.0 — Up to 1,000 W CC 12.0/10.0 compilation requires CUDA 12.8+. RTX 5090 memory correction is not the same enterprise reliability/support offering as RTX PRO or data-center ECC. L40S L2 is not stated on its product page; the full AD102/L40 figure must not be silently substituted. Primary sources for the table ¶ Open NVIDIA specification sources and qualifications NVIDIA's CUDA GPU compute-capability list maps RTX 5090 and RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell to CC 12.0, B200 to CC 10.0, H100/H200 to CC 9.0, RTX 4090/L40S to CC 8.9, and A100 to CC 8.0. NVIDIA's CUDA architecture/toolkit matrix lists first toolkit support and ongoing support; CUDA 12.8 added sm_100 , sm_101 , and sm_120 compiler support. The RTX Blackwell architecture whitepaper provides the RTX 5090's 170 SMs, 21,760 CUDA cores, 32 GB GDDR7, 1.792 TB/s, 96 MB L2, 575 W, 104.8 INT32 TOPS, and built-in GDDR7 single-bit correction. It also provides RTX 4090 comparison figures. The RTX PRO Blackwell architecture whitepaper provides 96 GB ECC GDDR7, 1.792 TB/s, 128 MB L2, 600 W, and 126 INT32 TOPS. The shorter product datasheet confirms the main board specifications. The official L40S product page specifies 48 GB GDDR6 with ECC, 864 GB/s, 18,176 CUDA cores, and 350 W. The Ada architecture whitepaper documents the 96 MB L2 full-AD102/L40 configuration; the current L40S page does not separately state L2. The A100 product page specifies 80 GB HBM2e, 2,039 GB/s for SXM, and 400 W. NVIDIA's Ampere architecture analysis documents 40 MB L2 and SECDED protection of HBM, L2, L1, and register files. The H100 datasheet specifies 80 GB, 3.35 TB/s, and up to 700 W. NVIDIA's Hopper architecture analysis documents 50 MB L2 and its architecture comparison. The H200 datasheet specifies 141 GB HBM3e, 4.8 TB/s, and up to 700 W. NVIDIA's HGX component table specifies H100/H200/B200 capacity and bandwidth and a configurable limit up to 1 kW per GPU. The Blackwell tuning guide documents B200/GB200's 180 GB memory support and 126 MB L2. NVIDIA's GPU memory-error management guide documents HBM ECC correction and error-management behavior for Ampere, Hopper, and Blackwell data-center families. Cost and availability snapshot ¶ Prices in the original assessment were regional, time-sensitive reference points. Compare the complete workstation or VM cost and measured work per hour. Read the historical pricing evidence Prices are volatile, regional, and not directly comparable between a bare card and a complete cloud VM. The following are official reference points, not a purchasing quote: NVIDIA lists the RTX 5090 from $1,999 . NVIDIA launched the RTX 4090 from $1,599 , but its current official product page no longer provides a meaningful live purchase price. A used or already-owned 4090 can still be economical; a new project should not assume launch-price availability. NVIDIA Marketplace listed a partner RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition at $14,079.99 when this report was checked. That is about seven times the RTX 5090 list price for three times the VRAM, professional ECC positioning, MIG, enterprise support, and about 20% more published non-Tensor INT32 peak—not seven times the kernel performance. See the official NVIDIA Marketplace listing . Google Cloud lists an RTX PRO 6000 96 GB virtual-workstation GPU at $1.09565 per GPU-hour in the first displayed region, before the VM, storage, network, and any licensing charges. The page states that price and availability vary by region. See Google Cloud GPU pricing . AWS Capacity Blocks list examples of about $5.97 per H100-hour and $14.21–$14.80 per B200-hour , sold as eight-GPU blocks in the shown regions. These are suitable for bounded cross-GPU experiments, not evidence that either card is cost-effective for daily development. See AWS Capacity Blocks pricing . Inference: local RTX 5090 ownership is the best development economics when the card remains well utilized. A cloud RTX PRO 6000 is an attractive way to validate the 96 GB path without buying a workstation card. H200/B200 rentals should be delayed until optimized kernels and a fixed benchmark harness exist, otherwise expensive time will mostly measure unfinished code. Interpretation for this project ¶ Comparison Published difference What still needs measurement 5090 versus 4090 1.78× bandwidth, 33% more VRAM/L2, about 2.54× INT32 peak Actual NTT and key-switch speedup PRO 6000 versus 5090 Same bandwidth, 3× VRAM, 33% more L2, about 20% more INT32 peak Benefit from capacity, reliability, and batching H200/B200 versus RTX Larger HBM and higher bandwidth Modular arithmetic, cache behavior, and end-to-end cost At N=65536, a 40-tower polynomial uses 65536 × 40 × 8 = 20 MiB . Evaluation keys, QP temporaries, twiddles, and concurrent ciphertexts can consume much more. Record peak memory with all keys resident before choosing capacity. Recommended acquisition strategy ¶ Develop and tune on RTX 5090; check workstation power and cooling. Retain or rent RTX 4090 for cross-architecture correctness and timing. Evaluate RTX PRO 6000 if measured residency exceeds 28 GB or enterprise reliability/support is required. Rent H200/B200 after the optimized kernels and fixed harness exist. Mandatory cross-GPU benchmark gate ¶ Run the same commit, parameters, and timing boundaries. Record toolkit, clocks, power policy, median, p95, and peak memory. Layer Required cases Arithmetic u32/u64 add/subtract, high/low products, Shoup, Barrett, reference remainder; registers and spills Memory / NTT Coalesced copy, strided gather, L2/DRAM cases, forward/inverse at N=65536, realistic tower counts Hybrid ModUp, ModDown, decomposition, key MAC, relinearization, rotation; one request and batches Correctness Exact pinned-OpenFHE arrays at every intermediate boundary See the benchmark plan for counters and result records. Keep kernel-only and transfer-inclusive timing separate. Selection rule ¶ Keep RTX 5090 as primary if the complete resident set fits and its end-to-end latency is within 15% of the fastest tested card. If only capacity blocks it, compare RTX PRO 6000 with H200 rental and deployment costs. Use profiling to separate memory-layout limits from modular-multiply limits before redesigning arithmetic or selecting hardware. Bottom line ¶ RTX 5090 is the development choice. Production hardware remains an open, measured decision; FP8/FP16 Tensor figures do not settle it."}, {"title": "Integration roadmap", "summary": "The tracked 95-working-day plan through exact OpenFHE Hybrid integration.", "group": "Plans", "url": "openfhe-integration-plan.html", "text": "CKKS GPU-to-OpenFHE integration plan ¶ Target: OpenFHE 1.6.1 CKKS with Hybrid key switching Primary arithmetic: 50–60-bit RNS primes stored in uint64_t , with uint32_t indexing/control paths where safe Ring dimension: 65536 Estimate: 95 planned working days + 15 risk-reserve days Last updated: 2026-11-12 (Asia/Shanghai) Follow the live tracker, then open the phase you need from the section sidebar. The sequence is NTT → base conversion → Hybrid key switching → OpenFHE. Update this tracker only when a deliverable passes its gate. Live tracker ¶ Field Current value Working day D11 Active phase P2 — first RTX GPU baseline (offline preparation complete) Active task Validate and record the real GPU environment; D11 hardware gate remains open Next gate P2: native CUDA/OpenFHE exactness and named-hardware baseline (D11–D15) Overall state In progress; offline preparation complete, hardware verification pending Hardware: RTX 5090 primary, RTX 4090 control. Use larger-memory hardware only when measurements justify it; see the GPU comparison . Status vocabulary: DONE , IN PROGRESS , NOT STARTED , BLOCKED . Phase Working days Status Exit artifact P0 — pre-plan correctness baseline Before D1 DONE CPU oracle, schedule simulator, CUDA source, exact OpenFHE NTT gate P1 — contracts and architecture D1–D10 DONE Accepted ADR , frozen operation graph and implementation backlog P2 — first RTX GPU baseline D11–D15 IN PROGRESS Offline compilation/preflight ready; native exact CUDA results and named-hardware baseline pending P3 — production GPU NTT D16–D40 NOT STARTED Tuned, device-resident NTT dispatch P4 — exact base-conversion model D41–D49 NOT STARTED OpenFHE-exact CPU Q↔QP oracle and fixtures P5 — GPU base conversion D50–D61 NOT STARTED Exact and tuned ModUp/ModDown kernels P6 — Hybrid key-switch contract D62–D68 NOT STARTED Frozen decomposition/QP/MAC/ModDown graph P7 — GPU Hybrid key switching D69–D85 NOT STARTED Exact relinearization and rotation backend P8 — OpenFHE integration D86–D95 NOT STARTED Integrated backend with fallback and full tests P9 — risk reserve D96–D110 NOT STARTED Used only for integration and correctness risks Definition of exact compatibility ¶ Given the same parameters, input arrays, ciphertext components, and evaluation keys, the GPU path must produce the same per-tower arrays as the pinned OpenFHE implementation. Approximate CKKS decryption agreement is a secondary test, not the compatibility criterion. Key generation randomness is outside this rule; the fixed generated key is part of the test input. Every production kernel must pass, in order: arithmetic primitive versus the __uint128_t oracle; operation versus this repository's CPU reference; operation versus pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1; full pipeline versus OpenFHE using fixed inputs and keys; benchmark/profiler gate on named hardware. P0 — pre-plan baseline (DONE) ¶ Negacyclic CPU NTT and Python big-integer oracle. Shared CPU/CUDA stage bodies and portable schedule simulator. Correctness-first two-pass CUDA source. Exact public OpenFHE 1.6.1 transform compatibility for all target N . RTX 4090/5090 build targets and rented-GPU setup script. Benchmark and tuning methodology. Execution of CUDA gates on real hardware; this belongs to P2. P1 — contract and architecture freeze (D1–D10) ¶ Status: DONE. ADR 0001 accepts the contracts and implementation backlog; native runtime gates remain in later phases. Day Work Required output D1 Read forward column kernel and launch geometry Index and memory-access map for N=65536 D2 Derive forward column butterfly indices Worked p,g,j,u,v,r,d example and twiddle proof D3 Read inverse column kernel and DIT arithmetic Forward/inverse correspondence D4 Read contiguous tile kernels Proof that every global stage appears exactly once D5 Read constructor, buffers, and public API Host/device dataflow and allocation map D6 Read modular arithmetic and exact test gates Arithmetic bounds and adversarial-test list D7 Trace OpenFHE NTT/DCRT representation Natural/bit-reversed and coefficient/evaluation contract D8 Trace OpenFHE Hybrid implementation Exact Q -> QP -> MAC -> Q operation graph D9 Design buffer views and backend seam Device buffer/stream/cache API proposal D10 Architecture review Accepted ADR and implementation backlog D1 complete: the interactive work-distribution map covers N=65536, gather/scatter and shared-memory indices, launch geometry, active thread ranges, and requested memory-access patterns. This is a source analysis and CPU-schedule check, not evidence of real-GPU correctness or speed. D2 complete: the same page's column proof derives CPU group/offset equivalence, both global exponents, coverage, bounds, and three worked examples. column_mapping_check runs the production stage body with two tagged-table probes for every column butterfly at N=65536; the separate schedule gate checks real-root arrays. Real-GPU validation is still a P2 deliverable. D3 complete: the inverse proof derives the factor of 2 per butterfly and final N-inverse normalization, documents row→column→scale ordering, and provides worked examples. inverse_column_check probes every inverse column pair and inactive thread at N=65536, then checks real-root stage composition and full normalization for two 46-bit primes. These are CPU-side checks of shared helpers, not actual GPU execution. D4 complete: the row-kernel and coverage proof derives tile-local/global group equivalence and global twiddle exponents, proves the far/near stage partition and legal block-local stage fusion, and provides worked examples. tile_mapping_check probes every near-stage pair in both directions at N=65536 and checks stage/endpoint coverage for N=65536 plus N=2,64,256,512. Hardware races and CUDA launch behavior remain P2 work. D5 complete: CUDA buffers and dataflow maps the constructor, all six device allocations, normal destruction, per-method copies, launch composition, representation ownership and missing integration contracts. Static source checks pin this map and byte-count arithmetic; they are not CUDA-runtime tests. D5 identified a raw-Impl constructor exception gap. Later offline preparation changes it to unique ownership, adds device guards and checks host inputs, and compiles the wrapper. Native failure-path verification and the production resident runtime remain open. D6 complete: Arithmetic bounds and tests records canonical-range induction, integer-width bounds, parameter preconditions, the exactness ladder and deferred adversarial cases. Two CPU gates compare 14,956 primitive results against Python big integers and exercise six patterns in both transform directions plus a closed-form negacyclic product at N=65536 with 50/55/60-bit primes. The multiplication-count comment is corrected; arithmetic algorithms are unchanged. Native GPU and invalid-input hardening remain explicit later gates, not evidence claimed by this review. D7 complete: the OpenFHE representation contract traces pinned NativePoly/DCRTPoly format conversion, exact odd-root evaluation indices, minimum-root selection and its cache constraint, canonical residues, ordered tower packing/import, and active-prefix behavior. A new opt-in core-only gate checks full-size formulas and three-tower operations with 50/55/60-bit primes against OpenFHE 1.6.1. The OpenFHE-enabled Release suite passes 14 tests on this Linux CPU environment. No CUDA execution or Hybrid rounding graph is claimed by that D7 result. D8 and D10's later architecture acceptance are documented below. D8 complete: the Hybrid operation graph traces digit splitting, partial partitions, coefficient/evaluation transitions, noncanonical approximate CRT, full-key P offsets at reduced levels, B/A MAC ordering, exact CKKS ModDown, wrapper updates, and delayed QP operations. openfhe_hybrid_graph checks 18 full-size cases and 117,571,584 residue comparisons against independent unreduced-CRT and scalar formulas, including real generated keys and negative alternatives. The opt-in Release suite now has 15 CPU tests. Rotation automorphisms and higher-degree relinearization are source-traced but remain later end-to-end gates; production matrix coverage, GPU execution and performance are not claimed. D9 complete: the device backend contract proposes immutable parameter snapshots, checked whole-tower views, device/session and generation identities, explicit stream/event dependencies, pinned resource lifetimes, cache identities, and an opt-in transactional DCRT format adapter. The portable metadata prototype is checked by backend_contract_check and a bridge to actual D8 OpenFHE output/key parameters. The opt-in Release suite has 16 CPU tests. Abstract interfaces compile without a CUDA dependency; CUDA resource management, event execution, caches and the adapter are not implemented. D10's acceptance is recorded below. D10 complete: ADR 0001 accepts the D7–D9 behavioral contracts and the dependency-ordered implementation backlog . It freezes the explicit DCRT adapter, validated no-op and preflight-only fallback, immutable outputs, registry/lease ownership, partial-submission quarantine, separate configurable memory budgets and fail-fast admission. The review accepts P1's architecture; it does not promote the abstract API to a runtime backend. The existing CUDA constructor gap, native build, exactness, profiling and all later implementation gates remain open backlog items. D11 is next; P2 has not started because a native GPU environment has not been provisioned here. P1 exit gate: NTT/DCRT (D7) and Hybrid (D8) representation, ordering, and active-tower transitions are explicit. The exact pinned CKKS approximate-CRT and reduction order is identified (D8); broader production fixtures remain P4/P6 work. Buffer views record ring size, Q/P towers, level, representation, ordering, device, stream, and event ownership (D9 proposal and host checks; runtime implementation pending). The first integration seam and CPU fallback behavior are frozen by ADR 0001 . Architecture review accepted and implementation work has dependencies, observable acceptance criteria and explicit remaining runtime gates. P2 — first real GPU baseline (D11–D15) ¶ Offline preparation is complete : stock nvcc builds sm_89/sm_120 binaries, 19 host/OpenFHE CTests pass with GPU tests excluded, and the native fixture/collection path is prepared. This starts P2 preparation but does not complete D11's actual GPU environment record or any later native gate. Day Work Required output D11 Provision the selected GPU and CUDA toolkit Reproducible environment record D12 Build native architecture, resolve baseline safety/build blockers and run smoke tests Native build, constructor/failure cleanup and validated smoke tests (B02) D13 Run exact gates over all N and modulus widths Correctness report D14 Profile with CUDA events and Nsight Kernel, L2, DRAM, occupancy, and register data D15 Analyze and publish baseline Raw samples, summary table, first bottleneck decision Follow backlog B01–B04 . Constructor RAII, device ownership and input-validation blockers must be fixed and fault-tested before the native baseline is accepted. The updated collector requires CUDA/OpenFHE coverage and propagates stage failures. Its 46-bit transfer-inclusive benchmark still does not replace 50–60-bit performance, CUDA-event timing or Nsight collection. Minimum cases: zero, one, q-1 , alternating bounds, deterministic random, forward, inverse, round-trip, evaluation-domain inverse, and polynomial multiplication. Record 50-, 55-, and 59/60-bit OpenFHE-compatible primes. P2 exit gate: OpenFHE == CPU reference == CUDA for every target size. Kernel-only and transfer-inclusive timings are separated. Actual sectors/requested byte and L2/DRAM traffic are recorded for the strided pass. P3 — production GPU NTT (D16–D40) ¶ D16–D25 — arithmetic and device API ¶ Days Work D16–D17 Introduce an arithmetic-policy interface while retaining the oracle path D18–D20 Implement Shoup multiplication and parallel twiddle quotient tables D21 Add adversarial Shoup tests at maximum supported modulus width D22–D23 Implement and test Barrett variable multiplication D24 Add device-buffer, asynchronous stream, and event API D25 Run complete exact regression and benchmark D26–D40 — layout, radix, and selection ¶ Days Work D26–D29 Prototype coalesced transpose/Four-Step replacement for the column pass D30–D32 Implement radix-4 register staging D33–D35 Implement radix-8 register staging and padded shared memory D36 Compare warp shuffle and shared-memory exchange for small stages D37 Add compile-time specialization for N=65536 D38 Run latency/throughput autotuning over real tower and batch counts D39 Freeze the per-device/per-size dispatch table D40 Production NTT gate and documentation update P3 exit gate: Exact transform for all target sizes and moduli. No optimized kernel silently uses device-side integer remainder. Device-resident forward, inverse, and pointwise operations compose without host synchronization. A selected latency and throughput configuration exists for every target size on the primary GPU. P4 — exact base-conversion model (D41–D49) ¶ Day Work D41 Locate and trace OpenFHE ModUp ( Q -> QP ) D42 Locate and trace OpenFHE ApproxModDown ( QP -> Q ) D43 Record precomputed constants, reductions, rounding, and table layouts D44 Extract fixed OpenFHE intermediate fixtures D45 Implement standalone CPU Q -> QP oracle D46 Implement standalone CPU QP -> Q oracle D47 Test every target level, tower count, and adversarial coefficient D48 Design base-conversion buffer and batched APIs D49 Review and freeze the exact contract P4 exit gate: the standalone CPU base-conversion arrays equal pinned OpenFHE at every intermediate boundary, not only after a complete key switch. P5 — GPU base conversion (D50–D61) ¶ Days Work D50–D52 Implement batched GPU Q-to-P basis extension D53 Exact Q-to-P tests and profiler pass D54–D56 Implement GPU QP-to-Q reduction D57 Exact QP-to-Q tests and profiler pass D58 Add dynamic CKKS level and tower-count support D59 Tune coefficient tile, output-tower mapping, accumulation, and reduction D60 Run the complete size/tower/batch benchmark matrix D61 Production base-conversion gate P5 exit gate: Exact Q-to-QP and QP-to-Q arrays. Dynamic active Q/P tower counts. NTT and base conversion share device buffers without conversion copies. Selected latency and throughput configurations are recorded. P6 — Hybrid key-switch contract (D62–D68) ¶ Day Work D62 Trace evaluation-key generation and storage D63 Trace digit decomposition and transform placement D64 Trace ModUp and evaluation-key multiplication D65 Trace ModDown and final ciphertext folding D66 Extract fixed intermediate fixtures from OpenFHE D67 Design GPU evaluation-key layout, cache, and lifetime rules D68 Freeze the full Hybrid operation graph P6 exit gate: all decomposition, basis, ordering, scaling, rounding, and final accumulation boundaries have exact OpenFHE fixtures. P7 — GPU Hybrid key switching (D69–D85) ¶ Days Work D69–D71 Implement device-resident digit decomposition D72–D73 Connect inverse NTT and Q-to-QP ModUp D74–D76 Implement evaluation-key multiply-accumulate in QP D77–D78 Connect QP-to-Q ModDown D79 Implement final c0/c1 accumulation D80 Validate relinearization D81 Validate automorphism/rotation D82 Exercise all target CKKS levels and digit counts D83 Run exact intermediate and final-array tests D84 Profile key cache, L2 behavior, memory footprint, and launch gaps D85 Production Hybrid key-switch gate P7 exit gate: Fixed inputs and evaluation keys produce exact OpenFHE tower arrays. Relinearization and rotations pass exact and decryption tests. Evaluation keys and constants upload once and remain cached. Full key-switch latency and batched throughput are reported. P8 — OpenFHE integration (D86–D95) ¶ Day Work D86 Implement the OpenFHE GPU backend adapter D87 Add CMake CUDA discovery and opt-in configuration D88 Map DCRTPoly towers to versioned GPU buffer views D89 Add parameter, twiddle, basis-table, and scratch caches D90 Add evaluation-key cache D91 Dispatch NTT, base conversion, and Hybrid key switching D92 Implement explicit unsupported-case CPU fallback D93 Finish stream/event lifetime, synchronization, and error handling D94 Run OpenFHE CKKS examples and relevant upstream tests D95 Integration release gate, documentation, and benchmark report Dispatch policy: invalid input or inconsistent format -> error valid same-format request -> validated no-op available + supported + verified -> CUDA backend unavailable/unsupported/unverified -> CPU only if allowed before submission busy/budget/allocation failure -> visible resource error submission attempted, then any failure -> visible error; no automatic CPU retry The opt-in adapter defaults to RequireDevice . See ADR 0001 for the accepted transaction, ownership, cache-budget and admission rules. Production integration remains a P8 gate. P8 exit gate: OpenFHE CKKS runs with Hybrid key switching through the GPU backend. Original CPU tests and fallback remain green. NTT, base conversion, relinearization, and rotation meet exact gates. Constants and evaluation keys remain resident across operations. All results identify hardware, clocks/power mode, driver, toolkit, compiler, commit, parameters, timing boundary, and correctness status. P9 — risk reserve (D96–D110) ¶ Use only for work that blocks a preceding exit gate: OpenFHE rounding/reduction-order differences; natural/bit-reversed ordering mistakes; CKKS level/tower corner cases; register spilling, shared-memory pressure, or poor occupancy; evaluation-key memory pressure and cache eviction; CUDA stream/event lifetime defects; primary-GPU compiler or architecture problems; cross-GPU dispatch regressions. Do not schedule new features in the reserve. Cipher inference begins only after P8 is green; it is a separate plan covering model operators, rescaling, rotations, graph scheduling, accuracy, and end-to-end latency. Progress log ¶ Date Day Change Evidence/commit 2026-11-06 D1 Created tracked integration plan; began line-by-line CUDA forward-column review; completed primary-source GPU selection survey dbf06cd 2026-11-07 D1 Added the N=8 VS Code debug example and native LLDB DAP setup; clarified CPU butterfly variable names; expanded the direct-sum and negacyclic derivations with locally bundled math rendering. CPU learning prerequisites are covered; the D1 CUDA index/memory map remains the next deliverable. Debug example and CTest gates; 57 formulas verified in the browser 2026-11-08 D1 Organized the documentation into an HTML engineering notebook with a daily journal, shared navigation, search, and a Pages workflow. Historical entries cite commits and are marked reconstructed. Implementation gates remain unchanged. Daily entry ; documentation build and regression checks 2026-11-09 D1 → D2 Completed both endpoint index/memory maps (4096 and 65536), with an interactive size comparison and portable schedule regression at N=65536. Next: column butterfly indices and twiddle proof. P1 exit gate remains open. Work-distribution map ; daily entry ; CPU schedule and explorer checks 2026-11-09 D2 → D3 Completed the column-pair/twiddle proof and six worked examples; exhaustive CPU execution of the real stage helper checked 270,336 pairs using 540,672 probes. Next: inverse-column correspondence. P1 exit gate remains open. Column proof ; column_mapping_check ; device_schedule_check 2026-11-10 D3 → D4 Completed inverse-column correspondence, factor-2/factor-N checks and normalization, plus inverse-mode explorer and worked examples. Next: contiguous kernels and exact stage coverage. P1 exit gate remains open. Inverse proof ; inverse_column_check ; daily entry 2026-11-10 D4 → D5 Completed row-kernel pair/twiddle equivalence and exact stage coverage. Added exhaustive CPU execution of both row-stage helpers plus all-size stage-list and endpoint checks. Next: constructor/buffer/API dataflow. P1 exit gate remains open. Row proof ; tile_mapping_check ; all 11 CTests passed 2026-11-10 D5 → D6 Completed constructor/allocation/transfer/API map and six static source-contract checks. Recorded constructor exception cleanup and device/stream ownership gaps. Next: modular arithmetic bounds and adversarial tests. P1 exit gate remains open. D5 map ; tests/docs/test_cuda_dataflow.py ; daily entry 2026-11-10 D6 → D7 Completed arithmetic bounds, canonical-range proof and adversarial-test inventory; added independent bigint and full-size 50/55/60-bit CPU gates. Next: OpenFHE representation/tower contract. P1 remains open. D6 contract ; arithmetic_bigint_check ; arithmetic_transform_check ; daily entry 2026-11-11 D7 → D8 Frozen the pinned OpenFHE NTT/DCRT array contract; full-size formula, format API, root, and three-tower checks pass. P1 remains open. D7 contract ; daily entry ; openfhe_representation_contract 2026-11-11 D8 → D9 Completed the pinned Hybrid graph and full-size intermediate-array gate; P1 remained open for ownership and architecture. D8 graph ; daily entry 2026-11-12 D9 → D10 Completed checked metadata and the backend API proposal; runtime implementation remained pending. D9 contract ; 78 host checks and 16 CPU/OpenFHE CTests 2026-11-12 D10 → D11 Accepted ADR 0001 and the implementation backlog; P1 is DONE. P2 awaits native GPU provisioning. ADR ; backlog ; daily review 2026-11-12 D11 preparation Completed stock CUDA sm_89/sm_120 compilation, baseline ownership/preflight fixes, native fixture compilation and tested failure-aware session collection. P2 preparation is in progress; D11 hardware acceptance and native gates remain open. Preparation guide ; 19 host/OpenFHE CTests; daily entry"}, {"title": "Architecture decision", "summary": "D10: accepted representation, backend ownership, adapter/fallback policy, resource budgets and P1 review.", "group": "Plans", "url": "architecture-decision.html", "text": "ADR 0001: accept the device backend architecture ¶ D10 · Accepted for implementation · 2026-11-12 (Asia/Shanghai) Use an explicit, device-resident backend with immutable polynomial handles, checked representation metadata, and owned completion dependencies. Integrate OpenFHE first through an opt-in DCRT format-conversion adapter with transactional host updates and preflight-only CPU fallback. This decision accepts the D7–D9 contracts and the implementation backlog . P1 is complete. D11 is next: provision the native GPU environment. Acceptance establishes the design; it does not establish CUDA correctness, runtime failure safety, or performance. 1. Review scope and evidence ¶ The reviewed source baseline is f349e72 . OpenFHE remains pinned to 1.6.1, commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , using NativeInteger 64. The production target is N=65536 with canonical 50–60-bit RNS primes. Small teaching examples and schedule boundary tests remain useful evidence; they do not expand production support. P1 question Accepted evidence Remaining runtime gate Does every butterfly/stage have the correct addresses and factors? D1–D4 proofs , exhaustive shared-helper probes and CPU schedule checks Native compilation, launch geometry, barriers and races Are arithmetic bounds and reductions explicit? D6 contract , independent bigint checks and full-size CPU patterns Native 50/55/60-bit adversarial matrix Are OpenFHE formats, roots and tower arrays exact? D7 contract and real NativePoly/DCRTPoly checks Same fixtures through CUDA and the adapter Is the Hybrid graph exact? D8 graph , 18 full-size cases and intermediate-array comparisons Broader parameter/key coverage and GPU composition Are buffer identity and ownership explicit? D9 contract , 78 metadata checks and a real OpenFHE metadata bridge Private registry, actual events, allocation and failure tests The existing CUDA wrapper remains a synchronous correctness baseline. The proposed interfaces in backend_api.hpp have no runtime implementation or factory. ntt::proposal stays in place until implementation tests justify promotion. This ADR freezes behavioral invariants, not a stable public C++ ABI. 2. Freeze the representation and operation boundary ¶ Accept these rules without reinterpretation: A deep immutable context carries N, the full ordered Q/P moduli and exact primitive roots, and the full-context digit width α. Each polynomial carries its ordered tower references, active Q count L, domain, order and encoding. Coefficients use natural order; evaluations use the D7 bit-reversed order. Residues are canonical uint64_t values. Forward, inverse and pointwise operations validate their input layouts. Inverse includes N-inverse normalization. Public inputs are immutable; results have fresh storage. Private scratch/output may use in-place kernels. Views alias only complete contiguous towers and retain allocation/readiness leases. A shortened full-QP span cannot stand in for reduced QlP. Reduced-level P key indices retain their full-context offsets. D8's digit/complement transform placement, unreduced approximate CRT, B-before-A output order, accumulation before ModDown, and delayed-QP scaling remain exact requirements. A decryption result alone cannot replace them. Semantic validity and device capability remain separate. Mathematical validity of an alternative primitive root does not establish OpenFHE cache compatibility. The first adapter accepts only the pinned context's canonical minimum roots and rejects known conflicting registrations before using the shared FTT cache. The process must not populate that upstream cache with conflicting custom roots; its entries do not retain enough root identity to detect such external usage after the fact. Broader custom-root support requires a new reviewed contract and fixtures. Out-of-place results may require an additional device-to-device copy when using the current kernels. Record that copy in memory and timing results. Public in-place writes, partial-tower strides, peer transfers and cross-device handles are excluded from the initial API. 3. Accept the first OpenFHE seam and fallback policy ¶ The first adapter is an explicit opt-in DCRT operation with SetFormat-equivalent behavior. It packs towers through ConvertToInt() , performs a backend transform, imports a private candidate, and commits the candidate only after complete success. It does not globally replace SwitchFormat or patch NativePoly storage. The first adapter transaction is synchronous to its caller. Validate the DCRT label, each tower's label, dimension, ordered q/ψ pairs, length and canonical values, including on a same-format request. Verify both tower labels and the outer DCRT label when importing; metadata overrides alone do not convert data. Access to the same host object must be serialized by its caller. Condition Accepted behavior Valid object already in the requested format Return without conversion Available, supported, verified device operation Use the device route Valid but unsupported layout, unavailable GPU, or unverified operation CPU only under AllowCpuBeforeSubmit ; otherwise error Invalid object/metadata/residues Error under either policy Admission busy, budget exhausted or allocation failure Report the resource error; do not silently change routes First enqueue-capable call attempted, then any failure Propagate failure; no automatic CPU retry RequireDevice is the explicit default for a caller opting into this adapter. Applications choosing fallback must select AllowCpuBeforeSubmit . The existing OpenFHE CPU APIs keep their existing behavior. Commit the completed candidate through the pinned DCRT move assignment, which is declared noexcept in the reviewed source . All validation and import work precedes that commit. Recoverable failures leave the original host object unchanged. This promise does not cover process termination: some upstream constructors/copy operations are declared noexcept while allocating. Adapter fault tests must distinguish recoverable injected failures from upstream fatal allocation paths; no universal out-of-memory rollback claim is accepted. 4. Close the partial-submission lifetime gap ¶ D9 establishes leases through completion. D10 additionally requires those leases to exist before the first enqueue-capable call , including when no completion handle is ever returned. Stage Required ownership and error behavior Preflight Validate handles, choose the route, reserve checked resource credits and create an operation record Submitting Mark the transaction as submitted before attempting the first async copy/kernel/wait; the operation record already owns all leases Event recorded Publish an immutable completion tied to that event/generation; retain all input/output/plan/staging/scratch pins Submission or event-recording failure Keep the record privately, mark it failed, drain the owning stream; do not release memory merely because no handle was returned Completed successfully Make readiness visible and release operation pins; external handles retain their own leases Failed, quiescence not established Poison the backend session, reject further submission and quarantine affected resources until safe teardown A producer on the same stream uses FIFO order. A pending producer on another stream needs the exact event wait. Only earlier submitted operation IDs are valid dependencies; IDs/generations must never wrap into reusable identities. A failed producer fails its consumers. Event failure is not proof of device quiescence, and dropping a completion is not cancellation. The registry validates all allocation, stream and event identities against its own session; user-supplied structs are not authority. Constructor unwinding uses RAII, each runtime call uses the owning device guard, and close() drains and reports errors. Destructors do not throw. The D5 raw-Impl cleanup gap is a release blocker tracked as B02, not a fix delivered by this ADR. Subsequent offline preparation repairs baseline ownership in source and compiles it; B02's native fault verification stays open. Do not automatically call cudaDeviceReset to recover a session in a shared process: it can invalidate allocations owned by other users of the device. An unrecoverable session reports failure and retains quarantine until safe owner-controlled teardown, potentially at the worker-process boundary. 5. Choose initial cache and admission limits ¶ Use these conservative, configurable defaults for the first backend/adapter implementation. They are engineering limits, not measured optimal settings. MiB means 2²⁰ bytes. Setting Initial decision Device cache budget 256 MiB per backend session Device operand/output/scratch budget Separate 256 MiB per backend session Owned host staging budget Separate 128 MiB per backend session Initial executor One explicit nonblocking CUDA stream per backend session Initial adapter concurrency One host adapter transaction at a time per session Admission when limits cannot be met Fail immediately with a specific error; no hidden waiting or automatic budget growth These are ceilings, not eager allocations. A second initial stream or concurrent adapter transaction reports a resource-limit/busy error. The accepted dependency model supports later multi-stream execution, but that capability remains disabled until its race/failure tests pass. Pure metadata helpers remain portable. Charge the allocator's requested allocation extents, including explicit padding, before allocating. These budgets bound accounted allocations, not CUDA's internal physical-memory reservation or driver overhead; measure those separately. Pending cache uploads count against the cache budget; an entry's pinned bytes are counted once. Operand/output/scratch allocations remain charged while any operation or external polynomial handle owns them. Host staging includes owned upload snapshots and retained download results. Register table-build/host-object memory separately in measurements; it is not covered by the staging cap. The initial allocator uses individually owned allocations, so trim_cache() reports actual released device bytes. A later pooling allocator must distinguish reusable suballocations from memory returned to CUDA and revise accounting before claiming the same statistic. Before the adapter's first submission, reserve credits for its complete checked transaction plan. Individual asynchronous backend operations reserve their own requirements before enqueue; a later operation can still fail after an earlier operation has submitted, which forbids automatic fallback for that transaction. Preserve the original host object in either case. Cache lookup uses D9's complete semantic keys. One mutex-protected pending entry coordinates each upload. Evict least-recently-used eligible entries only after external leases and operation pins are zero and quiescence is proven. If eviction cannot admit a request, return CacheBudgetExceeded ; if output/staging credits are unavailable, report the corresponding resource limit. Budget reduction is atomic: if the new limit cannot be met, retain the previous limit. The D8 full-context key payload is 16 MiB and one eight-tower forward/inverse plan has approximately 8 MiB of payload. Those explain why a 256 MiB cache is a reasonable initial ceiling; they do not bound a production key set. No peak memory or throughput claim follows from these defaults. 6. Alternatives reviewed ¶ Alternative Review result Raw pointer plus length Rejected: omits basis, format, generation and producer ownership Add global format hooks immediately Deferred: broadens the first seam before packing and rollback are tested Expose public in-place writes first Deferred: adds aliasing and concurrent-reader hazards before a native baseline Keep the current host-vector wrapper as the production backend Rejected: forces transfers and hides stream/device ownership Retry CPU after a CUDA failure Rejected: submission can be partial and dependencies may still be live Unlimited caching or automatic cache growth Rejected: defeats predictable admission and live-reader safety Replace approximate CRT with canonical CRT Rejected: changes pinned OpenFHE intermediate arrays Tune layouts/arithmetic before measuring the baseline Deferred: native correctness and named-hardware evidence come first The existing wrapper is retained as the baseline comparison during migration. No algorithm rewrite or runtime adapter is introduced by D10. 7. Accept P1 and track the implementation ¶ P1 exit requirement D10 disposition NTT/DCRT and Hybrid representation, ordering and active towers are explicit Accepted: D7/D8 source and CPU exactness gates Pinned approximate-CRT and reduction order are identified Accepted: D8 independent intermediate-array checks Buffer/device/stream/event ownership is specified Accepted: D9 metadata model plus D10 submission-failure rules First integration seam and CPU fallback are frozen Accepted: explicit DCRT adapter and the policy in section 3 The backlog gives each follow-up a phase, dependency and observable acceptance criterion. Native build/safety and exactness block benchmark claims. Runtime lifecycle and cache gates block promotion from ntt::proposal . The transactional adapter and application tests block production OpenFHE integration. D11–D95 retain their phase order; completing D10 does not mark any later implementation gate complete. Review validation reruns the 16-test CPU/OpenFHE Release suite, documentation unit tests, interactive mathematics, route/fragment checks, and desktop/mobile browser checks. Exact observed results are appended to the daily journal . At D10 acceptance, no CUDA compiler or GPU was available in the review environment. A compiler-only toolkit was installed during later offline preparation ; real GPU evidence is still pending. Any later change to residue encoding, root conventions, public aliasing, submission/fallback boundaries, ownership, or resource-accounting semantics requires a superseding ADR and exact regression evidence. Budget values may be explicitly configured within the accepted admission rules; benchmark-based default changes must record the hardware and workload that justify them."}, {"title": "Implementation backlog", "summary": "D10: dependency-ordered native GPU, resident runtime, cache, conversion, Hybrid and adapter acceptance gates.", "group": "Plans", "url": "implementation-backlog.html", "text": "Implementation backlog after D10 ¶ Accepted by ADR 0001 · 2026-11-12 · N=65536 P1's contracts are complete. The next working day is D11 : provision and record the native GPU environment. B01–B04 now have completed offline preparation ; their native acceptance gates remain open. B05–B12 are NOT STARTED . Design acceptance, compiler-only builds and portable CPU checks do not satisfy CUDA/runtime gates. The 95-working-day roadmap retains its phase boundaries. Each item lists a deliverable, dependencies and acceptance criteria. Update its state with reproducible evidence. 1. Deliverables and dependencies ¶ Select a task ID for its acceptance criteria. The comparison scrolls horizontally. ID Planned phase/days Deliverable Depends on B01 P2 / D11 Named GPU environment and reproducibility record Accepted ADR B02 P2 / D12 Native build, constructor safety and validated baseline B01 B03 P2 / D13 Exact native CUDA/OpenFHE baseline matrix B02 B04 P2 / D14–D15 Reproducible timing, profiler evidence and first bottleneck B03 B05 P3 / D16–D23 Checked arithmetic policies and optimized reductions B03; B04 informs selection B06 P3 / D24 Resident buffers, explicit stream and owned completions B02, B03 B07 P3 / D24–D25 Plan cache, budget admission and lifecycle regression B06 B08 P3 / D26–D40 Measured NTT layout/radix selection and production NTT gate B04–B07 B09 P4–P5 / D41–D61 Broader exact basis-conversion model and GPU implementation B08; D8 graph B10 P6–P7 / D62–D85 Production Hybrid fixtures, keys, hoisting and GPU graph B09; D8/D9 contracts B11 P8 / D86–D93 Transactional OpenFHE adapter and integrated dispatch B08–B10 B12 P8 / D94–D95 Application, fallback and integration release evidence B11 B02's safety fixes are prerequisites for a trustworthy native baseline. They are scheduled explicitly inside P2 rather than silently assuming the current wrapper is failure-safe. B06/B07 implement lifecycle rules before production resident execution. B11 is the first OpenFHE host adapter; library tests against OpenFHE in earlier phases do not themselves integrate that adapter. 2. P2: establish the native baseline ¶ B01 — environment record ¶ Record the actual GPU model, memory and compute capability; driver; CUDA compiler/runtime; host compiler; OS; CMake flags; source commit; OpenFHE commit, NativeInteger size and build options. Record clocks/power mode and profiler versions before performance measurements. Start with RTX 5090; use RTX 4090 as the control. If only a control GPU is available, label that run and keep the primary-device gate open. Use native architecture compilation on the selected device; sm_120 requires an appropriate toolkit (the current configured target starts at CUDA 12.8). Acceptance: checked-in or attached environment record plus successful device, compiler and driver queries. A CPU machine, toolkit version string alone, or PTX fallback without a native build does not complete B01/B02. This task does not provision or rent hardware as part of D10. Offline status: the compiler/toolchain record and collection scripts are ready. Actual GPU/driver discovery and native device execution are still pending. B02 — native build and baseline safety ¶ Work in cuda_ntt.hpp and ntt_kernels.cu . Replace raw constructor ownership with exception-safe RAII, retain the owning device, use a device guard, and validate checked extents and supported canonical inputs before enqueue. Resolve compiler/architecture blockers exposed by the first native build. Keep the baseline arithmetic and transform convention intact. Offline status: unique ownership, device guards and checked host preflight are implemented; sm_89/sm_120 compilation and driverless invalid-constructor checks pass. Native allocation/upload fault injection and context-error cleanup remain required before B02 is complete. Acceptance: native build and smoke run; injected failures at each allocation and constant upload unwind all prior allocations; invalid inputs submit no work; normal/destructor cleanup uses the owning device and does not throw. Update the D5 source-contract tests and buffer map to match the actual fix. Static source checks alone cannot complete this item. B03 — exact CUDA matrix ¶ Offline status: cuda_openfhe_exact is compiled and registered with real 50/55/60-bit OpenFHE and D8 level/basis fixtures. All native cases remain unrun. Extend the native harness to import the exact OpenFHE fixture moduli, roots, input arrays and expected arrays. At N=65536 cover 50/55/60-bit primes, active Q levels and representative tower counts; include D7's deliberately unsorted 50/55/60-bit packing case and D8's actual context parameters where applicable. Exercise zero, one, q−1, alternating bounds, monomials, deterministic dense residues, independent evaluation input, forward, inverse, round-trip, pointwise and negacyclic product. Compare complete arrays against the CPU oracle and pinned OpenFHE using the same fixtures. Run CUDA Compute Sanitizer memory, race, initialization and synchronization checks on the affected kernels. Acceptance: every selected case passes exactly with a recorded native build and no relevant sanitizer errors. The current CLI's generated baseline uses 46-bit parameters; its success alone does not cover this matrix. Keep the existing CPU/OpenFHE and small schedule-boundary gates green. B04 — timing and bottleneck report ¶ Separate construction/table upload, device-resident CUDA-event timings, transfer-inclusive wall time, and explicit synchronization. Add raw samples, warm-up, median/p5/p95, workload metadata and ptxas resource reports. Profile the strided column pass's sectors/requested byte, L2/DRAM traffic, bank conflicts, occupancy, registers/spills and launch gaps. The session collector , called by the AutoDL helper , now requires CUDA/OpenFHE coverage, retains logs and propagates failed stages, including benchmarks. Eleven host control-flow tests exercise those failure classifications. Actual timings, CUDA events, raw samples and profiler collection are still pending; its basic 46-bit wall-clock benchmark does not complete B04. Acceptance: reproducible raw results, exact-gate status and one evidence-backed first bottleneck decision on named hardware. Follow the benchmark plan . Do not label the current wrapper's transfer-inclusive wall time as kernel-only time. 3. P3: implement resident NTT safely ¶ B05 — arithmetic policies ¶ Retain the exact oracle path while adding Shoup twiddle multiplication and Barrett variable multiplication, with their exact range proofs and tables. Run independent bigint, maximum-width adversarial, complete-transform and native OpenFHE comparisons before selecting optimized dispatch. Acceptance: no hidden integer-remainder path in selected optimized kernels; range/overflow tests and hardware-specific exactness evidence accompany each policy. Alternative encodings need a superseding contract, not a changed label. B06 — resident runtime and registry ¶ Implement the accepted metadata through a private registry, move-only allocation owners, one explicit initial stream, immutable views/results, owned upload and download staging, and completions. Pin all resources before attempting submission. Retain failed operation records even when event recording or submission throws before a completion is returned. Poison/drain or quarantine a failed session. Acceptance: test wrong device/session, stale generations, alias rejection, input immutability, chained resident forward/pointwise/inverse, completion abandonment, close-with-live-handles, constructor unwinding, submission failure, event-recording failure, and failure before quiescence. Confirm a host download cannot expose data before success. Multi-stream capability stays disabled until exact cross-stream waits, event-generation handling and race tests pass. B07 — cache and memory admission ¶ Implement complete semantic plan keys, a single pending upload per key, readiness waits, external leases, in-flight pins and eligible-entry LRU eviction. Apply ADR 0001's separate 256 MiB cache, 256 MiB device workspace and 128 MiB host staging defaults, with checked allocation-size charging and configurable limits. The initial allocator owns individual allocations. Acceptance: tests cover concurrent same-key lookup, changed roots/basis/device, live-reader eviction attempts, failed uploads, pending entries, budget exhaustion, actual released bytes, all-or-nothing budget reduction and retained output/host result credits. Busy/budget failures are explicit, bounded and cannot deadlock waiting for the caller's own lease. Do not enable multi-stream dispatch merely because the cache lookup mutex is tested. B08 — production NTT selection ¶ Evaluate coalesced transpose/Four-Step, radix-4/8, shared-memory padding and warp exchange only after the baseline is measured. Preserve exact ordering, roots, normalization and the required out-of-place copy cost. Acceptance: device-resident composition without intermediate host waits; exact gates for every supported workload; recorded launch/resource parameters; latency and throughput selections for N=65536 on the primary GPU, with labeled control-device evidence. P3 closes only with the complete regression and dispatch record, not one faster microbenchmark. 4. P4–P7: preserve exact conversion and Hybrid behavior ¶ B09 — base conversion ¶ Broaden D8's model into independent Q→QP and QP→Q CPU fixtures across the intended levels, tower counts and boundary coefficients. Freeze exact constants, integer representatives, reduction order, and separate digit-extension/ModDown plan kinds. Then implement batched GPU conversion over the resident views. Acceptance: every intermediate array matches pinned OpenFHE; shortened digits and dynamic active prefixes work; NTT and conversion compose without host copies. Canonical CRT substitution must remain a detectable negative case. Record native sanitizer and performance evidence before the P5 production gate. B10 — Hybrid runtime, keys and hoists ¶ Broaden the production parameter, digit, level and key-family fixtures before GPU composition. Snapshot immutable A/B evaluation-key material with versioned identity; cache full-context metadata; preserve reduced-level P offsets, B/A accumulation, delayed QP scaling and one final ModDown per accumulated result. Add an explicit immutable hoist result tied to its source allocation/version. Acceptance: complete intermediate/final arrays match for fixed input/key material; altered key snapshots cannot reuse stale cache entries; stale hoists are rejected. Actual rotation automorphisms and higher-degree relinearization need full exact and CKKS decryption tests; D8's source trace alone is insufficient. Record key upload reuse, peak live memory and full-operation timing. 5. P8: integrate and release ¶ B11 — transactional adapter ¶ Implement the explicit opt-in SetFormat-equivalent DCRT seam before integrating broader operations. Validate every tower and outer format, use per-element packing/import, reserve the complete transaction's resource credits, and commit through a verified nonthrowing move only after successful download/import. Use RequireDevice by default; permit CPU only before submission under the explicit fallback policy. Add later conversion/Hybrid dispatch behind their gates. Acceptance: same-format requests are validated no-ops; CPU-only builds keep working; unsupported/unavailable/unverified cases obey policy. Recoverable packing, allocation, upload, launch, event, download and import failures leave the original host object unchanged. No post-submission automatic fallback occurs. Test partial submission without a returned completion, concurrent host-object access rules and upstream noexcept limitations explicitly. Application input, keys and CKKS metadata must survive both device and permitted CPU paths. B12 — integration release ¶ Run relevant upstream CKKS examples/tests and the complete exact pipeline, including all target levels, relinearization, rotations and explicit fallback. Keep constants/keys resident across repeated operations and publish cold/warm, end-to-end and kernel-only measurements with raw evidence and source revisions. Acceptance: all P8 exit gates are green; runtime failure and teardown tests pass; exact arrays and secondary decryption checks agree; per-device dispatch, limits, configuration and reproducibility instructions are published. Production promotion removes the proposal label only after these relevant implementation gates, with a reviewed API migration. 6. D11 handoff and evidence record ¶ D11 begins on the provided GPU machine. Record its environment before installing or changing the selected toolkit. Configure a separate native build directory; reuse the pinned OpenFHE installation produced by scripts/openfhe_setup.sh . For example, after setting the install prefix for that machine: cmake -S . -B build/native-gpu -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\ -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=native \\ -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX\" cmake --build build/native-gpu -j2 ctest --test-dir build/native-gpu --output-on-failure CMake auto-detection can produce a CPU-only build. B02/B03 require an actual CUDA compiler, the CUDA targets and their registered CTests, a real GPU, and successful native execution. The commands above are a handoff, not an observed D10 GPU run or proof that the current source already compiles with nvcc. For each completed item, retain the source commit, parameter/fixture identifiers, hardware/toolchain record, exact commands, raw logs, failing-case diagnosis where relevant, and the final gate result. Update this backlog, the roadmap and that calendar day's journal together. Leave unexecuted items visibly open."}, {"title": "Before renting a GPU", "summary": "Completed compiler-only preparation, baseline safety fixes, tested session reporting and the first real-GPU handoff.", "group": "Plans", "url": "offline-gpu-preparation.html", "text": "Before renting a GPU ¶ D11 preparation complete · hardware verification still pending · N=65536 The compiler, host checks and first-session collection path can be prepared without paying for a GPU. That preparation is now complete on this Linux workspace. D11 itself remains open because its final deliverable includes an actual GPU/driver environment record. Rent or borrow a GPU when ready to verify that environment and run the prepared native checks. The roadmap working-day numbers are engineering milestones, not continuous rental days. 1. What is ready ¶ Work Observed result CUDA build Stock CUDA 13.0.88 compiler builds and links sm_89 and sm_120 targets without a driver Host tests 19 host CTests pass; four registered GPU tests are explicitly excluded CPU build All 15 CTests pass with CUDA disabled Reports Eleven control-flow tests cover false success, missing coverage, stage failures and record preservation GPU tests Compiled against real OpenFHE, with full-size 50/55/60-bit and D8 level/tower fixtures; not executed on a GPU GPU probe Correctly refuses this driverless environment; no GPU arithmetic result is claimed The local preparation report is bench-results-offline-final/report.json , with status OFFLINE_READY and GPU execution NOT_RUN . Generated toolchains, binaries and reports stay in ignored directories; source and instructions are published. CI also compiles CUDA without a GPU and runs only the host gates. 2. Reproduce the offline preparation ¶ Use Linux x86_64, Python 3.12+, CMake 3.24+ and a compatible C++20 host compiler. The pinned installer downloads NVIDIA's compiler/runtime/header components, verifies each SHA-256, retains component notices and installs under build/ . It installs no driver and changes no system packages or shell configuration. From the repository root, with cmake / ctest on PATH: bash scripts/openfhe_setup.sh python3 scripts/install_cuda_toolkit.py python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode offline \\ --cuda-root build/toolchains/cuda-13.0.2 \\ --openfhe-prefix build/openfhe-compat/install The OpenFHE helper pins commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , NativeInteger 64 and reduced-noise off, and deliberately builds CPU reference tests with CUDA disabled. Its parallelism defaults to two jobs; set NTT_BUILD_JOBS if the machine supports more. The session runner selects project-local CMake/CTest from .venv/bin when present. The toolkit lock records exact upstream components from NVIDIA's CUDA 13.0.2 redistributable manifest. The compiler's component version is 13.0.88. Existing matching installs are reused; conflicting or incomplete destinations and checksum mismatches fail visibly. On this host, CUDA 12.8.93 failed its compiler-identification build because its math headers conflict with glibc 2.41's sinpi / cospi declarations. Stock CUDA 13.0 compiles successfully here; no vendor-header patch was applied. A rental image may use a different supported OS/toolkit combination. Match its driver, CUDA toolkit and host compiler rather than assuming that this compiler-only installation establishes driver compatibility. OpenFHE libraries and build directories are not universally portable between machines. Reuse binaries only with compatible OS, glibc, architecture and compiler settings; otherwise build the pinned dependency on a matching CPU machine/image before the paid session, or rebuild it on the GPU machine. 3. Fixes made before rental ¶ The first real nvcc compilation exposed a missing closing namespace in the CUDA source. The launch helpers also named a private nested implementation type from free functions; they now deduce that internal type without exposing it publicly. The shared schedule and modular arithmetic algorithms are unchanged. The synchronous wrapper now: owns Impl through unique_ptr , so constructor exceptions destroy partial state; captures the owning device and selects/restores it around operations and cleanup; checks prime moduli below 2⁶³, canonical primitive roots, checked buffer sizes, nonempty matching tower lists and power-of-two N in [2,65536] before CUDA calls; rejects wrong-length or noncanonical operands before upload; rejects unsafe benchmark exponents/counts before shifting or allocating; reports device information through ntt_cuda_cli info when a usable GPU exists. The D5 map reflects the updated ownership. Host tests exercise validation, including three invalid constructors linked against the real CUDA-compiled wrapper. They do not allocate device memory. Device selection, partial allocation/upload failures, launch errors and cleanup under a failed context still need native fault-injection tests. Cleanup selection/free errors cannot be reported by the nonthrowing destructor; this remains a production lifecycle limitation, not an accepted asynchronous backend implementation. The compiler-only checks do not implement the D9 backend , its caches, or the transactional OpenFHE host adapter. 4. Prepared native exactness matrix ¶ cuda_openfhe_exact compares the CUDA wrapper with the independent CPU reference and actual OpenFHE NativePoly transforms/products at N=65536. Its prepared cases include: deliberately unsorted 55/50/60-bit primes with actual OpenFHE minimum roots; the exact D8 Q/P moduli and roots, with Q and QlP bases at L=5,4,3,1; zero, one, q−1, alternating bounds, x¹⁷, xᴺ⁻¹ and deterministic dense residues; forward, inverse from independent evaluation arrays, round trip, pointwise multiplication followed by inverse, and the complete negacyclic product; exact complete-array comparisons and unchanged host inputs. The --smoke option limits that executable to the three-width context with zero and dense patterns for the four Compute Sanitizer tools. This is a bounded smoke scope, not the complete production sanitizer/parameter matrix. All GPU cases are compiled but unrun in this workspace. The existing cuda_oracle_crosscheck , cuda_matches_ref and cuda_verify gates remain. All four native tests carry the gpu CTest label. The wrapper-constructor preflight is a separate host test and is safe without a driver. 5. First session on real hardware ¶ Choose an RTX 5090 image for the primary target. An RTX 4090 can establish the control run; label it accordingly and leave primary-device evidence open. Use an image with a matching NVIDIA driver, CUDA compiler, Compute Sanitizer, and Nsight tools for later profiling. Native architecture mode needs CMake 3.24+. Use the intended clean source checkout and the pinned OpenFHE install. First check the image, then run the collector: nvidia-smi nvcc --version compute-sanitizer --version NTT_CUDA_ROOT=/usr/local/cuda \\ NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" \\ bash scripts/autodl_setup.sh The helper no longer performs an implicit git pull or package installation. It records the selected commit, hardware/driver/clocks/power data and toolchain, requires a native CUDA build plus all OpenFHE/GPU tests, probes the actual device, runs the exact suite and four sanitizer smoke tools, then collects the existing 46-bit transfer-inclusive baseline benchmark. GPU mode rejects dirty source unless explicitly marked exploratory with --allow-dirty . For full control, call the Python collector directly: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode gpu \\ --cuda-root /usr/local/cuda \\ --openfhe-prefix \"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" \\ --architectures native --benchmark --skip-sanitizers is available for an explicitly limited diagnostic run; its report says EXPLICITLY_SKIPPED . Such a run cannot satisfy sanitizer gates. Missing tools, missing GPU/OpenFHE tests, build/test/sanitizer/benchmark failures all produce a nonzero exit and a failed report. An existing report directory is never overwritten. Open report.json and the named stage logs after every run. The basic benchmark still uses 46-bit primes and wall time around public calls, including transfers, host validation and device guards. It is not a 50–60-bit production performance report, CUDA-event timing, or a completed D14 profile. 6. When to stop the rental ¶ Use the first GPU session to establish D11 hardware facts, execute D12 smoke and B02 failure-path work, run D13 exactness, and collect the D14 measurements that are ready. Export all raw records before stopping the machine. Analyze and publish results offline for D15. The collector returning GPU_CHECKS_PASSED still leaves native failure injection, full production coverage, kernel-only raw timing and Nsight profiling as explicit remaining gates. Completing this offline checklist does not automatically close D11, B02, B03, D14 or P2. The backlog controls those acceptance criteria."}, {"title": "Benchmarks & tuning", "summary": "Workload matrix, profiling evidence, and per-device kernel selection.", "group": "Plans", "url": "benchmark-tuning-plan.html", "text": "GPU NTT benchmark and tuning plan ¶ Primary target: NVIDIA RTX 5090 ( sm_120 ) Cross-architecture control: NVIDIA RTX 4090 ( sm_89 ) Workload target: OpenFHE 1.6.1 CKKS with Hybrid key switching Ring dimensions: 65536 Choose kernels using exact checks and measured latency or throughput at N=65536. Record the GPU, CKKS parameters, batch shape, and transfer boundary. 1. Implementations to compare ¶ Each implementation must preserve the same transform convention and pass the same exact correctness gates. Current radix-2 two-pass implementation using reference modular arithmetic. Two-pass implementation with Shoup twiddle multiplication and Barrett or Montgomery variable multiplication. Radix-4 register-staged implementation. Radix-8 register-staged implementation with padded shared memory. Coalesced Four-Step/transpose implementation. Fused variants used by base conversion and Hybrid key switching. Compare GPU-NTT and FIDESlib only when transform semantics, modulus limits, RNS workload, and timing boundaries match. 2. Workload matrix ¶ The minimum matrix is: Axis Values Ring dimension 2^16 Modulus width OpenFHE-compatible 50, 55, 59/60-bit primes Active RNS towers representative shallow, medium, and deep CKKS levels Polynomial count one component, full ciphertext, key-switch digits Batch size 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , then L2-capacity-limited sweep Direction forward, inverse, forward–pointwise–inverse Objective single-operation latency and steady-state throughput Transfer policy device-resident, pinned end-to-end, pageable end-to-end GPU RTX 5090 primary; RTX 4090 control Take tower and Hybrid digit counts from the pinned OpenFHE parameter sets. Three-limb synthetic fixtures alone are insufficient. 3. GPU and kernel parameters to tune ¶ Tune or record all of the following: threads per block and blocks per transform; values and butterflies per thread; radix and number of stages fused per kernel; tile/split dimensions and transpose shape; register count per thread and register spilling; static/dynamic shared memory per block and bank conflicts; active blocks and warps per SM; warp-shuffle versus shared-memory exchange boundaries; twiddle layout, Shoup table layout, and on-the-fly twiddle generation; lazy-reduction range and reduction frequency; RNS limb, ciphertext, and operation batch layout; CUDA stream count, asynchronous copies, and CUDA Graph replay; scratch-buffer reuse and allocator behavior. Retain ptxas -v reports and Nsight occupancy. Select by end-to-end performance, not register count alone. 4. Measurements ¶ Timing ¶ CUDA-event kernel-only latency for every kernel and complete transform; transfer-inclusive latency, separating pinned and pageable host memory; transforms per second and coefficients/towers processed per second; full polynomial multiplication latency; Hybrid key switch, relinearization, rotation, and hoisted-rotation latency; complete encrypted-inference latency and peak device memory; cold setup, warm steady state, and CUDA Graph replay separately. Report median, p5, and p95 after warm-up. Store raw samples so later summaries can be regenerated without rerunning the GPU. Hardware counters ¶ achieved DRAM and L2 bandwidth; global load/store transaction efficiency; L2 hit rate and read/write sectors; shared-memory bank conflicts; registers per thread, spills, occupancy, and eligible warps; SM and memory-pipeline utilization; kernel-launch count and synchronization gaps. The current column pass must specifically record memory sectors per requested byte because its neighboring-thread coefficient addresses are 2,048 bytes apart. 5. Reproducibility record ¶ Every result row must include: GPU name, compute capability, clocks, power mode, and memory size; driver, CUDA toolkit/runtime, compiler, CMake flags, and git commit; implementation/variant ID and all tuned launch parameters; N , modulus bits, actual moduli or parameter-set ID, tower count, digit count, batch shape, transform ordering, and transfer policy; correctness-gate result and profiler command/version; whether setup, allocation, transfers, and synchronization are included. Results without this header are exploratory and must not appear in published comparison tables. 6. Tuning procedure ¶ Run exact arithmetic and transform gates for the candidate variant. Reject variants that spill unexpectedly, exceed resource limits, or violate lazy-reduction bounds. Sweep launch and staging parameters at N=65536. Profile the best latency and throughput candidates with Nsight Compute. Repeat using realistic RNS tower and Hybrid digit batches. Validate finalists on RTX 4090 to identify architecture-specific choices. Record the winner in a dispatch table keyed by GPU capability, ring dimension, tower/batch range, and latency/throughput mode. Re-run the complete Hybrid key-switch and inference benchmarks. A locally faster NTT variant is accepted only if it improves the target pipeline or supplies a clearly justified fallback. 7. Acceptance gates ¶ Exact forward/inverse agreement for every supported N and OpenFHE modulus. No silent fallback to device-side integer remainder in optimized kernels. No benchmark mixes kernel-only and end-to-end timing boundaries. A selected latency and throughput implementation exists for N=65536 on RTX 5090. RTX 4090 results either validate the same dispatch or document a separate one. Full Hybrid key-switch and inference results accompany NTT microbenchmarks. The HTML explainer identifies which schedule is the correctness baseline and which implementation the tuner selected for each workload class."}, {"title": "Writing & publishing", "summary": "Preview the site, add a daily journal entry, and publish the HTML documentation.", "group": "Contribute", "url": "contributing-docs.html", "text": "Writing and publishing the project notebook ¶ Edit Markdown and the three interactive HTML lessons in docs/ . Build the site locally, then export it to the personal website. Generated HTML is not source. Preview locally ¶ python3 -m venv .venv .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r docs/requirements.txt .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory build/docs-site Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ . Rebuild and refresh after editing. The interactive source HTML also works independently. Read the site ¶ The left sidebar switches pages. On this page lists the current page's sections and highlights your reading position. On smaller screens, use Menu for pages and Sections for headings. Search opens from the top bar or Ctrl+K / Cmd+K . Learn pages explain the mathematics and code; Research records evidence; Plans track gates; the Daily journal records dated results. Calendar dates and roadmap working days are separate: publishing a note does not complete a gate. Add a daily entry ¶ python3 scripts/new_journal.py --date 2026-11-10 --title \"Describe the outcome\" The helper creates a draft without overwriting an existing entry. Append to an existing day. Keep the title , date , summary , phase , and status metadata. Fill all six required sections: Section Record Outcome What changed Reasoning Evidence behind the decision Changes Links to code and documents Verification Observed checks, hardware, and limits Open questions What remains unresolved Next step The next deliverable Use status: published when complete; drafts stay off the site. Label reconstructed history and cite commits. Update the integration plan when a gate changes. Its tracker supplies the homepage checkpoint. Add or edit an article ¶ Register new documents in docs/site.json with source, URL, title, group, and summary. Use relative links to documents and code; the builder converts them to HTML routes or revision-pinned GitHub links. Use ## headings for main sections and ### for subsections. Main headings populate the section sidebar automatically, including headings in HTML lessons. Existing anchors are preserved. Tables scroll inside the article. Write inline math as \\(...\\) and display math as \\[...\\] . KaTeX is bundled locally. Put long reference material in <details markdown=\"1\"> with a clear <summary> ; keep the main conclusion visible. Search includes collapsed text. Validate before publishing ¶ .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests/docs -v .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check node --test tests/docs/*.test.cjs The build validates catalog coverage, journal metadata, and local links, assets, and heading fragments. The site supports project subpaths. The Node checks run the pages' actual inline scripts. The work-distribution checks cover N=65536 in both directions, stage controls, boundary selections, and complete per-stage index coverage. The handwritten-derivation checks compare DIT and negacyclic DIF butterfly results with independent direct NTT sums and check full-size twiddle/index boundaries. Node.js 22+ is required; no packages are needed. These checks do not replace browser inspection or CUDA execution. The separate C++ column proof executes the production stage helper on CPU: cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build --target ntt_column_mapping_tests ntt_device_sim ctest --test-dir build -R 'column_mapping_check|inverse_column_check|device_schedule_check' --output-on-failure The column check executes the real shared stage helper with tagged power tables at N=65536. It probes the input pair, ladder multiplier, and twiddle selection for every column butterfly. This is CPU verification, not a native CUDA build or a GPU race check. The inverse gate additionally checks reciprocal-table selection, the factor of two from one forward/inverse stage pair, and full N-inverse normalization with valid roots for two primes at N=65536. For D4, run: cmake --build build --target ntt_tile_mapping_tests ctest --test-dir build -R tile_mapping_check --output-on-failure It probes every row butterfly at N=65536 in both directions and checks the two-pass stage lists and global endpoint coverage at N=65536 and small-size boundaries. Run a full build and ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure to include the D3 and D4 gates together. The Python documentation suite also includes D5 source-contract checks for the allocation/copy/API map. These inspect C++ source text and pin byte-count arithmetic; they do not compile CUDA, inject failures, or validate runtime resource cleanup. See CUDA buffers and dataflow . The D6 arithmetic contract has two CPU gates: cmake --build build --target ntt_arithmetic_contract_tests ctest --test-dir build -R 'arithmetic_' --output-on-failure They check compiled primitives against Python big integers and run full-size 50/55/60-bit transform patterns. They are not native CUDA tests. OpenFHE representation gate ¶ The optional OpenFHE build registers both openfhe_exact_compat and openfhe_representation_contract . The D7 contract explains the pinned source, exact array convention, and test command. These are CPU tests against OpenFHE 1.6.1; GPU execution remains a separate gate. Device backend contract gate ¶ The normal build registers backend_contract_check . Read the D9 proposal for buffer layouts, stream dependencies, cache identities and fallback. These are host metadata checks, with abstract interfaces compiled by the test. The optional openfhe_hybrid_graph also checks the proposed active QP metadata and key offsets against actual OpenFHE objects. Neither gate implements a CUDA resource manager or OpenFHE device adapter. D10's ADR accepts the design and its limits. The backlog records dependencies and runtime acceptance criteria; update it alongside the roadmap and journal as work passes. The offline preparation guide covers compiler-only CUDA builds, host checks and native session collection. GPU tests use the gpu label: ctest --test-dir build/cuda-offline13 -LE gpu --output-on-failure excludes them. Such a passing host suite is not a GPU correctness result. Publish with GitHub Pages ¶ Add the notebook to the personal website ¶ From this repository: .venv/bin/python scripts/export_website.py --website-root /path/to/minnow54426.github.io Review, commit, and push the generated systems/ntt-gpu/ files in the website repository. Its Pages workflow publishes the site. Open the notebook . The exporter refreshes managed files only and adds a return link to the personal website. Navigation, search, and math also work in local previews. Standalone project Pages deployment ¶ Ordinary pushes to ntt-gpu build a preview artifact; they do not publish the personal website. For an optional standalone site, set repository Settings → Pages → Source → GitHub Actions , then manually run the Documentation workflow with deploy_standalone enabled. Its URL is minnow54426.github.io/ntt-gpu ."}, {"title": "Define and accept the device backend architecture", "summary": "D9 and D10 close P1; compiler-only CUDA preparation is ready while D11 hardware verification remains open.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-12.html", "text": "2026-11-12 P1 complete / P2 D11 preparation ← All entries Define and accept the device backend architecture ¶ D9 and D10 close P1; compiler-only CUDA preparation is ready while D11 hardware verification remains open. Outcome ¶ Completed the D9 backend proposal . The roadmap advances to D10's architecture decision and implementation backlog. P1 remains in progress until that review accepts the proposal. This change implements host metadata checks and compile-checked abstract interfaces; a CUDA runtime backend and OpenFHE adapter remain proposed. Reasoning ¶ D7 fixes representation and D8 fixes the exact Hybrid graph. A pointer/length API would still lose the active Q prefix, full-key P offsets, exact roots, producer completion and ownership of scratch/key material. The design keeps those facts together in immutable snapshots and checked whole-tower views. The first integration seam is an explicit opt-in DCRT format-conversion adapter. It uses a private host candidate and commits only after complete success. CPU fallback is allowed only during preflight; invalid data and runtime failures are errors. Immutable public inputs and fresh outputs simplify initial alias and concurrency rules, with possible device-to-device copies counted as a cost. Changes ¶ Added backend_contract.hpp : immutable parameters, canonical layouts, checked extents, allocation generations, operation layouts, dependencies, cache identities, retirement and hoist rules. Added backend_api.hpp : proposed stream, completion, immutable polynomial, owned download, cache-budget and backend APIs. There is no runtime implementation or factory. Added backend_contract_tests.cpp as the normal-build backend_contract_check CTest. Extended the Hybrid gate to import real OpenFHE context parameters and compare active QP metadata/key offsets with actual Hybrid output and key towers at L=5,4,3,1. Documented staging ownership, event waits, single-upload cache entries, resource pins, failure quarantine, out-of-place costs and Hybrid lifetimes. The existing raw-Impl CUDA constructor cleanup gap is still open. Registered the English article, linked D5/D8 to it, updated build guidance, and prepared the website export with the existing readable theme and sidebar. Verification ¶ The portable contract executable passes 78 checks on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2. The same test passes AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer with no reported issues. Tests include invalid roots, canonical values, overflow, incorrect reduced-QP spans, stale generations, overlap, missing cross-stream waits, failed-resource retirement, changed cache identities and forbidden post-submission CPU fallback. Final local verification: All 16 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed in 11.31 seconds, including the 78-check metadata gate and the existing 117,571,584-residue Hybrid gate. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The documentation build generated 25 HTML pages, including eight journal entries, and validated local links, fragments, assets and catalog coverage. Chromium passed 100 page/width checks across all 25 notebook pages at 1440/1024/375/320px: English content, rendered formulas, no page-wide overflow, and no script or failed HTTP responses. D9's section links and search worked; its article, this entry, the roadmap and both introductory lessons passed computed text-contrast checks. The tracker displays D10. Desktop and phone screenshots of the new article were inspected. Publication exports the source article and journal to the existing personal website, preserving section navigation and the shared design. Deployment and live verification follow the website push. These are CPU/metadata and browser gates; no CUDA compiler or real GPU is available in this environment, and no GPU correctness or performance result is claimed. Open questions ¶ D10 must accept the first seam, immutable API, copy cost, registry provenance, cache budget/backpressure, failure quarantine and ordered implementation backlog. Runtime tests still need allocation/upload fault injection, cache races, asynchronous error/drain handling, completion abandonment and host rollback. Production Hybrid conversion/key-family coverage remains later work. Next step ¶ Produce the D10 architecture decision record and actionable backlog, accepting or amending the D7–D9 contracts. Keep P1 open until that review is complete; then proceed to P2's named-hardware native CUDA exactness and profiling gates. D10: accept architecture and close P1 ¶ Outcome ¶ Accepted ADR 0001 and the implementation backlog . The roadmap now marks P1 DONE and points to D11. P2 remains NOT STARTED pending native GPU provisioning. This acceptance freezes behavioral contracts and implementation priorities; it does not implement or validate the CUDA runtime backend or OpenFHE adapter. Reasoning ¶ The D1–D9 evidence establishes stage/index arithmetic, exact representation, the pinned Hybrid graph and checked metadata. Review identified two design gaps that needed explicit decisions: resource admission limits, and resource ownership when work is partially submitted but event recording or handle publication fails. The ADR retains pins before the first enqueue-capable call, preserves failed operation records without returned handles, and requires drain/quarantine rather than immediate reuse. It chooses separate configurable cache/device-workspace/ host-staging ceilings of 256/256/128 MiB, one initial stream and one adapter transaction per session, with fail-fast admission. These are conservative design limits rather than measured optimal settings. The DCRT adapter remains the first integration seam. RequireDevice is its explicit default; invalid data and resource errors cannot silently fall back. Only valid unsupported/unavailable/unverified operations may use an explicitly permitted CPU route before submission. Candidate import precedes the pinned nonthrowing DCRT move commit. Upstream allocating noexcept operations and the shared FTT cache's missing root identity remain explicit limitations. Changes ¶ Added the accepted ADR with decisions, source/evidence traceability, alternatives, partial-submission/error semantics and P1 exit dispositions. Added twelve dependency-ordered backlog items, B01–B12, each with phase, deliverable and observable acceptance criteria through P8. Made constructor RAII/device/input validation a P2 prerequisite, and recorded the setup helper's missing benchmark-error propagation and default-build coverage as baseline-reporting work. No CUDA fix is claimed in D10. Updated D5/D8/D9 cross-links, build guidance, catalog and roadmap. Historical D9 results above remain intact; the new ADR supplies the current acceptance. Prepared both new English pages for the existing website, section sidebar, search and shared readable theme. Runtime code and kernels are unchanged. Verification ¶ Observed on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2: All 16 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed in 11.41 seconds, retaining the 78 metadata checks and 117,571,584 Hybrid residue-array comparisons. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The site builds 27 HTML pages with eight journal entries and validates routes, fragments, assets and catalog coverage. Chromium passed 108 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px: English text, rendered formulas, no page-wide overflow and no script/HTTP errors. ADR and backlog section links/search worked. Focused contrast checks covered the new pages, current journal, roadmap and both introductory lessons. The homepage and tracker show D11, P1 DONE and P2 NOT STARTED. Visual review found the four-column task table cramped on phones. A contained, keyboard-scrollable table preserves column widths; twelve task IDs link to their acceptance criteria. Four focused width checks passed after the change, including keyboard scrolling and task navigation. Desktop and phone screenshots of the ADR/backlog were inspected. Both repositories are prepared for publication from this source revision. Deployment and public-file verification follow the website push. The review environment has no usable native CUDA/GPU execution path, so none of the later native implementation gates is marked done. Open questions ¶ B01 must establish an actual primary/control GPU environment. B02/B03 must resolve native compilation and lifecycle failures before correctness or benchmark claims. Runtime budget defaults need measured memory-pressure evidence; broad parameter/key coverage, multi-stream capability, adapter rollback and process failure handling remain implementation acceptance gates. Next step ¶ D11 / B01: record the provided GPU, driver, toolkit, compiler, source revisions, OpenFHE build and native architecture configuration. Then run B02's native build and baseline-safety work before B03 exactness and B04 profiling. P2 is ready to start, not completed by the D10 review. Offline preparation before GPU rental ¶ Outcome ¶ Completed the offline preparation for the first native session. Real nvcc builds and links both sm_89 and sm_120 targets, including a new direct CUDA/OpenFHE exactness program. The collector returns OFFLINE_READY after 19 host/OpenFHE tests and explicitly leaves four GPU tests unrun. D11 remains the current working day: its actual hardware/driver record cannot be completed in this driverless workspace. P2 preparation is now IN PROGRESS. Reasoning ¶ The user asked to finish work that can be done before renting. Compiler-only validation finds expensive setup failures without charging GPU time. CUDA 12.8.93 failed compiler identification on this host's glibc 2.41 because of math-header exception-specification conflicts. A stock CUDA 13.0.88 compiler from the 13.0.2 redistributable release works here; vendor headers were not patched. The first nvcc build exposed the existing CUDA file's missing namespace closure. Free launch helpers now deduce the private implementation type without exposing it publicly. The original raw-Impl construction path also needed unique ownership, while default CMake detection and the old benchmark pipeline could misclassify incomplete/failed work as success. The changes address those offline blockers without claiming native execution or performance. Changes ¶ Added a SHA-256-locked local NVIDIA compiler/runtime/header installer, preserving licenses and leaving drivers/system packages unchanged. Added explicit CMake CUDA enable/require controls, a shared CUDA backend library, GPU labels, host validation/constructor targets, and compiler-only CI. Changed the synchronous wrapper to unique Impl ownership and owning-device guards. Added checked dimensions/counts, prime/root bounds and canonical input rejection; preserved the six-buffer dataflow and arithmetic stage bodies. Added the N=65536 direct CUDA/CPU/OpenFHE comparison program: exact-width 55/50/60-bit primes, D8 Q/QlP bases at L=5,4,3,1, seven coefficient patterns, independent inverse input, round trip, pointwise/inverse and ring product. It is compiled but not run on a GPU. Replaced the AutoDL helper's implicit pull/install and fragile verdict with explicit session collection, immutable report directories and stage logs. Missing coverage or failed build/test/sanitizer/benchmark stages return nonzero. The pinned OpenFHE helper now explicitly selects its 64-bit/no-reduced-noise CPU build and bounded parallelism, so offline preparation cannot accidentally execute newly detected GPU tests. Updated the D5 map, roadmap/backlog, source guidance and website catalog. Verification ¶ Observed on Linux x86_64, glibc 2.41, GCC 14.2 and stock CUDA compiler 13.0.88: Both architecture targets and all CUDA executables compile and link. The offline collector passed 19 host/OpenFHE CTests. These include 17 standalone host-preflight assertions and 20 assertions in the CUDA-linked constructor executable; the latter rejects three invalid constructors before device access. The separate CUDA-disabled build passed all 15 CTests. Eleven mocked-process reporting tests passed, including benchmark/sanitizer failure propagation, missing GPU labels/tests and preservation of prior records. They test collection control flow, not GPU arithmetic or CUDA allocation failure. Actual ntt_cuda_cli info returned a visible insufficient-driver error here. An explicit CUDA-required/CUDA-disabled configuration also failed as intended. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The build generates 28 pages, including eight journal entries, with valid routes, fragments and assets. Chromium passed 112 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px: English content, rendered math, no page-wide overflow or script/HTTP errors. The preparation guide's section links/search and current P1/D11/P2 state passed; focused text contrast and desktop/phone screenshots were reviewed. Source CI and public deployment are checked after publication. Reports remain under ignored bench-results-* ; local development reports record that the tested source working tree was dirty before its publication commit. No GPU was rented, no driver was installed, and no native kernel, sanitizer, performance or peak-device-memory result is claimed. Open questions ¶ D11 still needs an actual GPU/driver environment record. B02 requires native allocation/upload fault injection, wrong-device and cleanup-failure evidence. The collector's sanitizer scope is a smoke subset; full production cases and kernel-only/raw/Nsight profiling remain later gates. CUDA 13's successful offline compilation does not establish compatibility with a rental machine's driver. Next step ¶ Use the prepared clean checkout on the chosen compatible GPU image, record the hardware, and run the native collection command. Export raw records before stopping rental; analyze them offline. Keep D11/B02/B03/P2 open until their specific native acceptance criteria pass."}, {"title": "Freeze the OpenFHE NTT and DCRT representation contract", "summary": "Completed D7 with full-size OpenFHE representation checks and prepared the accumulated D3–D7 notebook for publication.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-11.html", "text": "2026-11-11 P1 / D7 → D8 ← All entries Freeze the OpenFHE NTT and DCRT representation contract ¶ Completed D7 with full-size OpenFHE representation checks and prepared the accumulated D3–D7 notebook for publication. Outcome ¶ D7's NTT/DCRT boundary is documented against pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1. The tracker advances to D8, the Hybrid operation graph; P1's architecture gate stays open. The website export includes the previously unpublished D3–D6 work and this D7 contract, with the shared section navigation retained. Reasoning ¶ A round trip alone can conceal a matching forward/inverse permutation or scale error. D7 checks every evaluation address against closed-form monomials and independent inverse basis vectors. A real three-tower DCRT object verifies parameter order and packing without assuming a contiguous OpenFHE allocation. The pinned source distinguishes SetFormat (conditional conversion), SwitchFormat (always conversion), SetValues (data plus label), and OverrideFormat (label only). DCRT OverrideFormat changes only the outer label. RootOfUnity selects the minimum primitive root, but the FTT cache lookup does not compare roots on a cache hit; consistent imported roots are part of this contract. Changes ¶ Added the D7 representation contract , including exact formulas, parameter/root requirements, format APIs, ordered tower packing, active-level behavior, and pinned source references. Added openfhe_representation_tests.cpp and the opt-in openfhe_representation_contract CTest gate. At N=65536, checked four monomials and four independent inverse slots at each of three exact-width primes (50/55/60 bits), minimum roots, canonical values, and format API behavior against actual OpenFHE NativePoly operations. Checked a deliberately unsorted 55/50/60-bit tower list, BigInteger coefficients Q-1 and 2^64+17, CRT reconstruction, flat evaluation-array import, pointwise multiplication by x with negative wrap, and tower dropping. Clarified that FirstPrime(50, m) searches above 2^50: the original gate uses a 51-bit prime there. D7 uses LastPrime for exact-width fixtures. Corrected reference-header comments that conflated mathematical and stored evaluation order or described individual ladder twiddles as odd powers. The NTT algorithm is unchanged. Reused stage-coverage storage across stages in the tile test. This removes the observed GCC 14 Release vector-teardown build failure without suppressing warnings or removing coverage. Registered the article, updated build/test instructions, and advanced the roadmap to D8. Verification ¶ Built OpenFHE from commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f locally on Linux x86_64 with GCC 14.2, static libraries, NativeInteger 64, and OpenMP off. The initial OpenFHE-enabled Release build passed all 14 CTests, including the original exact compatibility gate and the new representation gate. The tile coverage target also compiles in the normal GCC Release configuration. Final verification: All 14 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed after the complete test changes. All 23 documentation unit tests and all 4 explorer tests passed. The build generated 22 pages with seven journal entries and validated links, fragments, assets, and catalog coverage. Chromium followed every section link on all 22 pages at 1440, 1280, 1024, and 375px: 88 page/width checks passed without JavaScript errors or page-wide horizontal overflow. Search, menu, sections, and the forward explorer worked. Focused desktop/mobile checks confirmed D7 math without KaTeX errors, the D7→D8 progress row inside its table, and inverse explorer stage order, reciprocal twiddles, and pass labels. Screenshots of the D5–D7 articles were inspected at 1440 and 375px. Publication exports all current source pages, including D3/D4 proofs, D5/D6 articles, and this D7 entry, to the existing personal website repository. Live verification follows the website deployment. These are CPU/OpenFHE and browser checks; no CUDA compiler or real GPU was available, and no GPU performance result is implied. Open questions ¶ D8 must trace the exact Hybrid decomposition, Q/QP transitions, key MAC, rounding, and ModDown sequence. D9 still needs explicit buffer/stream ownership, RAII constructor-failure cleanup, and the integration seam. Arbitrary custom-root contexts sharing a modulus are outside the D7 test matrix because of the pinned OpenFHE cache behavior. Next step ¶ Trace OpenFHE 1.6.1 Hybrid key switching and freeze the exact operation graph with intermediate-state fixtures. Preserve bit equality rather than replacing it with approximate CKKS decryption agreement. Manuscript supplement: recursion and stage twiddles ¶ The user's handwritten derivation was added to the NTT and butterfly lesson : Split the original sum into even and odd inputs and derive the shared multiplication used by the two outputs. Recurse into the a₄ᵣ and a₄ᵣ₊₂ subsequences and explain why roots square. Expand all four outputs and draw an accessible P/R/Q/S-to-b₀/b₂/b₁/b₃ SVG. Specify zero-based cyclic DIT stages, group lengths, and local offsets: w(s,j) = omega_N^(j*N/2^(s+1)) . Interpret 8; 4,12; 2,10,6,14 as one four-bit reversed root-table convention, rather than a universal list of cyclic DIT factors. Map this to the repository's reverse-stage negacyclic DIF, including c=psi^d and w=omega_N^(N*J/(2d))=psi^(N*J/d) . Add an N=4/8/16/65536 exponent calculator with indices, distances, both root conventions, and stage direction. Preserve the existing numerical examples. New Node tests execute the actual calculator script and compare cyclic DIT and negacyclic DIF with independent direct sums. They check target-size stage boundaries, indices, and control limits. Documentation CI runs both groups of interactive mathematical checks. This documentation supplement did not advance D8 or P1 and did not add a GPU result. At publication, 23 documentation tests and eight interactive tests passed; all 22 generated pages passed local link and fragment validation. Standalone and generated lessons passed browser checks at 1440/375/320px: no KaTeX errors or page overflow; the target-size calculator, section links, search, four-point verification, and N=8 controls worked. Desktop/mobile network and calculator screenshots were inspected. The original record included Chinese navigation, which is translated in the English review below. Teaching revisions from the second manuscript ¶ The page review addressed each handwritten item without advancing D8 or P1: Merge direct-evaluation cost and savings, retaining the slider, chart, and old s8 anchor. Distinguish multiplication counts from butterfly counts and correct the doubling ratio and log–log curve description. State the cyclic DIT stage lengths, distances, and dependencies. Derive the negacyclic odd-root split and prove the folded factors using the remaining local diagonal twist. Every stage has c=ψᵈ; the final stage still has c=ψ. Share one editable 16-point example between the NTT and CPU-to-CUDA pages: q=97, ψ=19, all four stages, 32 pairs, factors and intermediate values. Column 3's first two stages and row 3's last two stages read the full trace. Add a weighted-butterfly diagram and final-stage explanation to the 65536 schedule page; typeset the column, row, and inverse proofs. Use N=65536/K=3 in the D5 host/GPU flow diagram: 1.5 MiB operands, six buffers, constant uploads, eight launches, and 4.5 MiB operand transfers. Typeset D7's ring, evaluation positions, probes, root tables, tower packing, and negative wrap. Refine D6 ranges and invariants and fix narrow-screen table overflow in the standalone HTML. At publication, 23 Python tests and 12 Node tests passed. All 16 basis vectors, boundary and signed inputs matched independent odd-root evaluation. Both pair groupings matched the full transform; every intermediate stage satisfied the diagonal relation with pre-twisted cyclic DIF. All 22 pages built with valid links/fragments. Five revised pages passed 1440/375/320px interaction, math, and overflow checks; three standalone lessons also passed. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. Site-wide navigation and formulas were checked at 1440/1280/1024/375px; the checker was corrected to decode Chinese URL fragments before comparison. Production NTT algorithms were unchanged. No GPU execution result was claimed. English edition and website design review ¶ Applied the public frontend-design skill to the personal portfolio and notebook. The design uses porcelain, blue, and white surfaces, locally hosted Newsreader headings and Source Sans 3 body text, clear reading widths, and less decorative chrome. The portfolio leads with an existing photograph; the notebook keeps its page outline, search, and exact interactive examples. Translated the manuscript derivation, calculator, 16-point example, and prior journal additions into English while retaining their technical observations. The old Chinese fragment remains an invisible compatibility anchor. Photo collection titles and labels are also English. Published search text and accessible labels are checked for remaining Chinese characters. The review covers all 41 HTML pages: four portfolio hubs, 15 photo collections, and 22 notebook pages. The personal site now has visible phone navigation, a keyboard-accessible native photo viewer with arrow keys and focus restoration, and real painting disclosure buttons with inert collapsed content. Existing image and video files are retained. Local font files include their OFL licenses. Observed browser verification: 164 page/width checks passed at 1440/1024/375/320px, with no JavaScript errors, failed HTTP responses, formula errors, duplicate IDs, or page-wide overflow. The first pass found one 320px notebook-heading overflow; allowing the heading row to wrap resolved it. Local links, fragments, and referenced assets were validated across all 41 pages. Desktop and phone interaction checks passed for photo browsing, Escape and focus return, painting playback/pause/speed/folder controls, the 65536 calculator, 16-point input validation, original arithmetic widgets, and search. Screenshots of each page were captured; representative portfolio, collection, notebook, and phone layouts were inspected. Documentation and mathematical checks remain the separate CPU/browser gates; this design work does not advance D8 or imply a GPU result. Restore contrast in the two introductory lessons ¶ The user reported faint content in the NTT/butterfly and CPU-to-CUDA pages. The design refresh had made definition panels white while retaining pale foregrounds from the previous dark panels. Their equations reached only 1.04:1 contrast. Recap headings were dark on dark, and selected ghost buttons used dark text on a dark blue background. The shared standalone lesson theme now pairs light panels with dark text, readable accent colors, and explicit selected/hover button colors. Recaps use a light blue surface. Success badges define their own contrast-safe variables, so recalculation's inline variable references retain readable results. Small SVG labels and the 16-point network connections are darker; all network stages retain full-opacity lines, with thicker highlighted pairs. Color crossfades on stage controls and the standalone table of contents are removed to avoid low contrast during state changes. The source lessons and exported pages share these rules. Verification: 23 documentation unit tests and 12 interactive mathematical tests passed. Sixteen browser checks covered both lessons, standalone and generated, at 1440/1024/375/320px. With optional sections expanded, visible HTML text passed computed contrast checks (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text; excluding disabled controls and SVG). Checks included selected/hovered stage buttons, recalculated badges, arithmetic examples, math rendering, and page overflow. SVG labels and panels were inspected in screenshots; network lines retain full opacity and a stroke width of at least one pixel. The arithmetic and working-day roadmap are unchanged. D8: freeze the pinned Hybrid operation graph ¶ Completed the D8 graph against the clean OpenFHE 1.6.1 checkout at b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f . The tracker advances to D9. P1 remains open for buffer/device/stream/cache ownership and the final architecture decision. The source trace fixes contiguous digit partitions, the shortened last digit, coefficient/evaluation transitions, complement-only extension, full-key P offsets at reduced levels, B/A accumulation order, exact CKKS ModDown, and the two-/three- component wrappers. It also records extended ciphertext scaling, delayed QP accumulation, and the source ordering of extended rotation/relinearization. Approximate CRT keeps its unreduced integer sum; substituting canonical CRT or moving ModDown into the digit loop changes arrays. Added openfhe_hybrid_graph , an opt-in PKE-linked CTest using N=65536 throughout. The deterministic context has M=5, alpha=3, K=3 and two full-context digits. The executable pins all eight moduli/roots. Its scaling-size request is 50, but two selected Q primes have 51 bits; arithmetic uses the exact primes. Levels L=5,4,3,1 exercise full/partial last digits and a partial first digit. Four coefficient patterns (zero, q−1, x^17, deterministic dense residues) use repeatable diagnostic key arrays. Two further cases use an actual key generated from two real secret keys; that key is fixed within each comparison, not across separate random key-generation runs. An independent arbitrary-precision unreduced-CRT sum and scalar arithmetic check the complete digit INTT, complement conversion/NTT, assembled digit, two MAC outputs, P INTT, Q correction conversion/NTT, and final P-inverse scale. The integer oracle uses BigInteger arithmetic but no OpenFHE conversion tables to compute its expected values. Transform expectations use the already-checked repository NTT with the exact imported roots. Public-core composition, source immutability, in-place add/replace behavior, both extended addFirst settings, delayed reduction, and first-element-only reduction are checked separately. Observed results on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2, Release, NativeInteger 64, OpenMP off: All 15 OpenFHE-enabled CTests passed (11.44 seconds in this run). The new gate passed 18 cases and 117,571,584 complete-array residue comparisons. Fixtures detected canonical-CRT substitution and wrong reduced-level P offsets. For the diagnostic L=4 dense fixture, 524,288 residues distinguish the incorrect per-digit reduction schedule from accumulation followed by reduction. All 23 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The site builds 23 pages and validates local routes, fragments, and assets. D8's 18 math expressions, five-level explorer, all section links, search, English content, text contrast, and overflow checks passed at 1440/1024/375/320px. The roadmap shows D9, and both introductory lessons retain their contrast fix. Desktop and phone article/explorer screenshots were inspected. This is a CPU/source contract gate. It does not claim native CUDA results, end-to-end CKKS encryption/decryption correctness, complete rotation/key-family coverage, or production performance. P4/P6 broaden those fixtures; D9 next specifies the buffer/stream/cache contract and integration seam."}, {"title": "Prove inverse correspondence and complete stage coverage", "summary": "Completed D3–D6: inverse/stage proofs, CUDA dataflow, arithmetic bounds, and independent adversarial checks.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-10.html", "text": "2026-11-10 P1 / D3 → D7 ← All entries Prove inverse correspondence and complete stage coverage ¶ Completed D3–D6: inverse/stage proofs, CUDA dataflow, arithmetic bounds, and independent adversarial checks. Outcome ¶ D3 is complete. The tracker now points to D4: contiguous tile kernels and exact global-stage coverage. No GPU arithmetic or launch behavior changed; two source comments were clarified. The P1 architecture gate remains open. Follow-up: D4 is also complete. The tracker now points to D5 constructor, buffer and public-API dataflow. D3 and D4 are being committed together at the user's request; no push or website publication was requested this turn. D5 follow-up: completed the constructor/buffer/API map and advanced the tracker to D6. The user requested committing and pushing this follow-up; the earlier D3/D4 commit is 404e88a. Website export is a separate action. Reasoning ¶ The inverse uses the same column pairs and exponent indices as the forward transform, but reciprocal powers. Untwiddle the difference leg first, form sum/difference, then apply the inverse ladder to the lower result. This composition returns twice the original pair. Reversing all log2(N) stages returns N times the input, so one final modular N-inverse scale suffices. The inverse must execute near row stages first, far column stages second, and scaling last. This is three launches at both endpoint sizes, not two. The bit-reversed evaluation output of forward is the inverse's direct input; neither direction needs a separate bit-reversal pass. Changes ¶ Added the inverse proof and examples , including both endpoint sizes and a numeric N=8/q=17 butterfly. Extended the explorer with forward/inverse direction selection, ascending inverse distances, reciprocal twiddle display, pass order, and scale note. The panel DOM order follows execution order for keyboard navigation too. Extended column mapping checks with --inverse , registered as inverse_column_check in CTest. Tagged-table probes cover all 270,336 inverse column pairs (540,672 active probes), and inactive threads, at N=4096 and N=65536. Valid-root checks for two 46-bit primes per size verify each column stage composition, then the full inverse's unnormalized and normalized outputs against the original and independent CPU reference. Updated the roadmap . Calendar dates do not advance working days; completed deliverables do. D4: added the row-kernel and complete-stage proof . Since 2d divides the tile length, row-local groups are aligned CPU groups and J=j; E=(N/d)j still uses the global transform length. Far and near distances are disjoint and concatenate to the full CPU distance sequence. Added tile_mapping_tests.cpp and tile_mapping_check . Tagged-table probes execute the actual contiguous helpers in both directions, not just duplicated index formulas. Separate stage-list and global endpoint coverage checks exercise all five target sizes and N=2,64,256,512 boundaries. D5: added CUDA buffers and host/device dataflow , registered in the HTML notebook. Mapped six persistent buffers, constructor uploads, normal cleanup, host-vector API contracts, flat versus limb-grid launches, transfer payloads, and fused multiplication. Clarified two public header comments without changing runtime behavior. Added six static source-contract checks in test_cuda_dataflow.py . These pin allocation sizes/frees, constructor copies, public length guards and copies, fused launch ordering, getters/interface shape, and endpoint byte arithmetic. Recorded source-inspected constructor exception leaks and missing device/stream/representation ownership contracts. No RAII fix, failure injection, or native CUDA compile was performed as part of D5. Verification ¶ Configured CMake and built ntt_column_mapping_tests and ntt_device_sim . Targeted CTest run: column_mapping_check , inverse_column_check , device_schedule_check , and four fixture generators all passed (7/7). Four Node explorer tests passed, now covering both directions, sizes, all stages, boundary owners, twiddle exponents, and inverse panel order. All 15 documentation unit tests passed; the site build validated local links and fragments. Requirements were confirmed in the isolated .venv . These checks execute on CPU. No native CUDA compilation, actual GPU synchronization check, hardware timing, or GPU occupancy measurement is claimed. Full CMake build and all 10 CTests passed, including unit tests, the Python oracle crosscheck, forward/inverse column gates, and the device-schedule simulator. Desktop and 375px browser checks confirmed proof reflow and inverse controls. At N=65536, inverse stage 9/distance 256 shows column 3/thread 5, J=3, inverse ladder exponent 256, inverse twiddle exponent 768. D4: full CMake build and all 11 CTests passed, including the new tile gate. It checked 278,528 row pairs per direction (1,114,112 active probes across both directions), plus idle threads. Stage lists in both directions and complete per-stage endpoint coverage passed for all nine tested sizes. All four explorer tests, 15 documentation tests and the 18-page build/link check also passed. These are CPU-side checks, not real GPU evidence. D4 browser review confirmed readable proof/formula reflow at desktop and a 375px embedded viewport. The existing forward/inverse explorer remains intact; no GPU kernels were changed by D4. D5: all 21 documentation/source-contract tests, four explorer tests and 11 CTests passed. The documentation build passed local link and fragment validation. Allocation/transfer byte counts are derived from source, not measured VRAM use or transfer throughput. D5 browser review checked the generated article at desktop and 375px widths, including table-of-contents navigation and allocation tables. Open questions ¶ CUDA launch and barrier behavior still need real-device checks in P2. Performance impact of strided column loads is still unmeasured. Constructor exceptions can leak Impl and any completed device allocations; an RAII implementation and fault-injection tests remain required. Device, stream/event and representation ownership are not yet frozen. Next step ¶ D6: review modular-arithmetic preconditions and reduction bounds; inventory existing exact gates and specify missing adversarial tests. Parallel workload and navigation changes ¶ The following parallel work was recorded on origin/main before this merge. It is preserved as history; the current tracker is D6 and the current workload is N=65536. Earlier multi-size D3/D4 results above describe checks run before the N=65536-only changes were integrated. Integration result: merged origin/main's a578e7a and 8aa94c3 without discarding the D3–D5 work. The current lesson is ntt-65536-work-distribution.html , with no size selector; inverse direction and all proof sections remain. D3/D4 current gates now target N=65536 (small boundary coverage remains). The post-merge build and all 10 CTests, 4 explorer tests, 23 documentation/source tests, and the 19-page build/link check passed. The old 4096 fixture is not reintroduced. Current row probes total 1,048,576 across both directions; each column direction has 262,144 pairs and 524,288 active probes. Fresh desktop/mobile previews preserve the responsive navigation and D5 layout. Outcome ¶ The project workload is now N=65536. The work-distribution lesson has one fixed size, with 256 rows and columns, 16 stages, 524,288 butterflies, and 512 KiB per limb. Both passes launch 256 blocks per limb and use 2 KiB of shared memory per block. Requested value-array traffic is 2 MiB per forward. Reasoning ¶ The user requested removal of the 4096 polynomial and consistent 65536 examples across HTML and implementations. Small teaching examples and the N=64/1024 correctness fixtures remain: the latter exercise boundary schedules and keep the independent quadratic Python oracle and schoolbook product practical. The general transform APIs still accept power-of-two lengths. Historical notebook observations and cited research ranges remain historical. Changes ¶ Renamed the work-distribution lesson , updated links and navigation, removed the size selector and comparison column, and corrected the memory, launch, and column-proof counts. Aligned the butterfly and GPU explainers, site shell, current project scope, benchmark plan, and integration plan to N=65536. Removed the 4096 CTest fixture. CUDA comparisons and verification now use the 65536 fixture. The column mapping and optional OpenFHE compatibility gates target 65536. Vector generation defaults to 65536; CMake explicitly requests 1024 for the quadratic oracle. CUDA CLI and AutoDL benchmarks default to exponent 16 only. Explicit benchmark arguments can still explore other lengths. Verification ¶ Release C++ build with GCC 14.2 and warnings as errors: all 8 portable CTest checks passed, including the N=65536 schedule and column-mapping checks. Documentation: all 15 Python unit tests passed; build_docs.py --check built 18 pages and validated local links and fragments. Explorer: all 3 Node tests passed, covering all 16 stages, boundary selections, inactive threads, twiddle exponents, and exhaustive per-stage index coverage. Default vector generation produced N=65536, byte-identical to the explicitly sized deterministic fixture. AutoDL shell syntax validation passed. Chromium/Playwright checked all three interactive HTML lessons at 1440px and 375px. No horizontal page overflow or JavaScript errors occurred. The explorer selected 1283/34051 initially, 773/901 at distance 128, and 65534/65535 at the final row/stage. The cost widget initialized at 65,536 and 4,294,967,296 direct products. Desktop/mobile explorer screenshots were visually inspected. No CUDA compiler or OpenFHE installation was present. Those native gates were not run; all implementation execution reported above was on CPU. Open questions ¶ Native CUDA and OpenFHE checks depend on their respective toolkit and library. CPU simulation does not establish GPU launch or synchronization correctness. Next step ¶ Continue the inverse-column correspondence proof at N=65536; no roadmap gate advances solely because the workload scope changed. Notebook reading review ¶ Reviewed all 18 published pages individually. The site now has one section sidebar generated from each page's main headings, including interactive HTML. Desktop sections stay beside the article; smaller screens expose the same links through Sections in the top bar. The current section updates while scrolling, and keyboard users can open, close, and follow section links. Page-by-page changes ¶ Page Reading change Overview Removed repeated collections and recent-entry cards; retained one complete directory and a shorter implementation sequence Project & setup Cut repeated status/design prose; put build commands and exactness gates first NTT & butterflies Shorter introduction and recap; preserved derivations, formulas, and interactive controls CPU to CUDA Shorter vocabulary and SIMT explanation; corrected wave, occupancy, and memory-traffic claims 65536 work distribution Shorter ownership explanation; preserved the index proof and explorer Run & debug Removed assumptions about installed tools; compressed recorded debugger troubleshooting GPU NTT implementations Decision tables and shorter headings; library evidence and citations in expandable sections Choosing the GPU Compact specifications and decision tables; historical pricing and full citations remain expandable Integration roadmap Shorter introduction; preserved day tables and gates; corrected the stale five-size specialization task Benchmarks & tuning Shorter policy prose; corrected the stale five-dimension acceptance requirement to N=65536 Writing & publishing One concise editing, validation, and export workflow; documented page and section navigation Journal index Clearer date-versus-deliverable explanation and a single-column entry list Journal 2026-11-05 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved reconstructed evidence Journal 2026-11-06 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved historical roadmap state Journal 2026-11-07 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved recorded test results Journal 2026-11-08 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved publishing history Journal 2026-11-09 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved the original mapping work and follow-ups Journal 2026-11-10 Kept the workload change above and appended this review Article text, tables, code, and section links use larger type and consistent spacing. Wide tables scroll within the article. Existing heading IDs and bookmarked research-section aliases are retained. Search still includes collapsed references. No roadmap gate or CUDA implementation changed. Review verification ¶ All 17 documentation unit tests passed, including heading-ID collisions, inline-script preservation, and Markdown/math inside expandable references. The documentation build validated all 18 pages, local links, and fragments. All 3 explorer tests passed. Chromium checked every section link on every page at 1440, 1280, 1024, and 375px: 72 page/width checks passed with no JavaScript errors or page-wide horizontal overflow. Menu, section navigation, search, and Escape behavior were exercised at each width. Desktop and mobile screenshots were inspected. The review found and fixed Escape handling in search. No GPU execution or performance measurement is implied by these browser checks. Final narrow-screen checks passed on all 18 pages at both 320 and 375px. The 320px pass exposed an unwrapped GPU vocabulary table; shared scrolling wrappers now also cover lesson tables without nesting existing matrix wrappers. Focused browser checks confirmed sticky desktop sections, reading-position updates, final-section selection, both butterfly coefficient widgets, network step/reset, GPU pair-stage controls, expandable citations, and rendered math without KaTeX errors at desktop/mobile widths. Mobile section-menu screenshots were also inspected. D6 follow-up: arithmetic bounds and adversarial tests ¶ Outcome and reasoning ¶ D6 is complete; the live tracker advances to D7, not past the P1 architecture gate. The arithmetic contract records preconditions, temporary-width proofs, canonical-range induction, the exact test ladder and a concrete deferred adversarial checklist. No reduction algorithm, kernel geometry or public API changed. The implementation uses canonical residues after every operation, not lazy reduction. A 60-bit modulus still needs a product wider than uint64_t. The arithmetic-header comment incorrectly counted CPU butterfly multiplies: the CPU loop uses three including twiddle advance; shared device helpers use two with table lookup. Corrected the comment and made q>0 explicit. Changes ¶ Added a compiled arithmetic probe and full-size tests . Added Python arbitrary-precision comparisons for 14,956 primitive results, including boundary moduli/operands, full-width products, large exponents and valid/zero-input inverse behavior. Added N=65536 two-sided roundtrips and canonical-range checks for six patterns at 50/55/60-bit primes, plus the closed-form negative wrap product x^(N-1) * x = -1 . Registered both gates in CMake/CTest. Registered the D6 article in the generated HTML notebook and linked the dataflow article, validation instructions and roadmap to it. Verification ¶ All 12 standalone CTests passed , including both new arithmetic gates. Rebuilt the opt-in OpenFHE configuration and ran openfhe_exact_compat : passed against locally installed OpenFHE 1.6.1 at N=65536. Compiled the new C++ test with Clang AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; both the 14,956-result Python comparison and all full-size patterns/products passed with no sanitizer diagnostics. Invalid preconditions such as q=0 were deliberately not executed. All 23 documentation tests , 4 explorer tests , and the 20-page documentation build/link/fragment checks passed. No shared layout or interaction changed; this update adds article content. This is macOS host execution. No nvcc build, actual GPU run, race check or GPU performance measurement was performed or implied. Open questions and next step ¶ The constructor assumes primality, the public API assumes canonical residues, and parameter-search helpers are unsafe for some invalid dimensions/bit counts. The article records these as input-contract/hardening work, not tests that already pass. Native CUDA checks of the new 50/55/60-bit matrix remain P2/P3 work. D7 next traces OpenFHE coefficient/evaluation order, root selection and DCRT tower representation. Personal website export/publication is separate from this repository commit and was not requested for D6."}, {"title": "Make the 4096-point CUDA schedule tangible", "summary": "Added an interactive explanation of polynomial layout and block/thread ownership.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-09.html", "text": "2026-11-09 P1 / D1 → D3 ← All entries Make the 4096-point CUDA schedule tangible ¶ Added an interactive explanation of polynomial layout and block/thread ownership. Outcome ¶ Added a standalone HTML learning page explaining the current forward CUDA schedule using a concrete 4096-coefficient polynomial and one RNS limb. No CUDA implementation or roadmap gate changed. Later follow-up: completed the D1 index/memory map for both target extremes and advanced the working-day tracker to D2; the P1 architecture exit gate is still open. Subsequent D2 work completed the column index/twiddle proof and exhaustive stage-helper checks; the tracker now points to D3 inverse-column work. Reasoning ¶ Line-by-line reading obscures the overall work distribution. The explanation starts with data ownership: columns for the four far stages, then rows for the eight near stages. Interactive stage, block, and thread selection shows how butterfly pairs change, including inactive threads in the baseline schedule. Changes ¶ 4096-point NTT work distribution . Registered the learning page in the site catalog. Exported the existing interactive explanation with a standalone runtime and added context about synchronization, shared memory, and limb-major storage. Formatting follow-up: replaced the exported iframe/runtime with a small, self-contained lesson. Added consistent notebook typography, numbered sections, jump links, side-by-side pass descriptions, and an integrated light-themed explorer. The page now scrolls as one document; controls and pass descriptions stack at narrow widths. Mathematical content and thread mapping are retained. Added N=65536 to the same explorer, preserving the existing page URL and N=4096 example. Sparse row rendering includes selected operands and labels all omitted rows instead of drawing 256 rows. Documented the 256×256 layout, 8+8 stages, 512 KiB per-limb storage, 2 KiB shared memory per block, active thread ranges, stride-2048-byte column accesses, contiguous row accesses, and logical value-array traffic. Added a three-limb N=65536 fixture to the portable CTest schedule gate and dependency-free Node tests of the actual explorer script. D2: added the CPU-offset identity J = 256j + r , the pair-ownership bijection, both multiplier exponents, bit-operation equivalence, and table bounds to the HTML page, with six first/intermediate/last-stage examples. The explorer now displays CPU offset J, ladder exponent d, and difference exponent E for active threads, and no twiddle read for inactive threads. Added tests/column_mapping_tests.cpp and the column_mapping_check CTest target. Two basis-input probes execute the production helper using tagged power tables; all destinations and exact selected entries are checked independently of the helper's own index formulas. Verification ¶ Installed/confirmed docs/requirements.txt in the isolated .venv . .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests/docs -v : all 15 tests passed. .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check : built 17 pages and validated local links and fragments. Browser inspection at desktop and 375px embedded viewport widths: controls render and reflow; selecting distance 128 gives positions 773/901 for block 3, thread 5, and distance 1 gives 778/779. This documentation work does not constitute a real GPU run or benchmark. Formatting follow-up verification: all 15 documentation tests and the 17-page link/fragment build passed again. Browser inspection confirmed the desktop layout and 375px reflow, and the distance-128 control still displays global positions 773/901 for block 3, thread 5. N=65536 follow-up: node --test tests/docs/work_distribution.test.cjs passed 3 tests, including all-stage selected-pair checks and complete index coverage for both sizes. ctest --test-dir build -R '^device_schedule_check$' --output-on-failure passed the schedule gate plus its 4 fixture generators, including N=65536 with three primes. The simulator compares forward arrays, round trips, and multiplication against the CPU reference. These are CPU results, not real CUDA results. Browser checks of the extended page at desktop and a 375px viewport passed: N=65536 selects 16 stage controls and displays 1283/34051 for the first stage at block 3/thread 5. Comparison and explorer tables remain separate; selected operand rows stay visible in the sparse matrix. D2 checks: ctest --test-dir build -R 'column_mapping_check|device_schedule_check' --output-on-failure passed both gates and four fixture generators (6/6). The column gate checks 8,192 + 262,144 pairs, using 540,672 active-pair probes, plus every inactive thread at every column stage. Every global index and active thread owner is covered once per stage. All 3 explorer tests (now including displayed twiddle exponents), all 15 documentation tests, and the documentation build/link checks also passed. These remain CPU-only checks. D2 browser review: proof text and formula blocks reflow at desktop and 375px widths; the live N=65536 example displays J=1283, c=ψ^32768, and w=ψ^2566 for column 3/thread 5 in the first stage. Open questions ¶ The first pass uses few butterfly threads and strided global accesses. Changes to that baseline require correctness checks and measurements on real hardware. Next step ¶ D3: derive how the inverse-column DIT butterfly undoes each forward butterfly, including the final N-inverse normalization and reversed stage order."}, {"title": "Open the engineering notebook", "summary": "Unified learning pages, research, plans, and daily reasoning in a navigable HTML site.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-08.html", "text": "2026-11-08 P1 / D1 ← All entries Open the engineering notebook ¶ Unified learning pages, research, plans, and daily reasoning in a navigable HTML site. Outcome ¶ Documentation now builds into one HTML site with shared navigation, search, a current-checkpoint homepage, and a dated journal. Interactive explainers remain part of the reading path. Reasoning ¶ Developers need stable explanations and the history behind decisions. Research stays in reference articles; each journal entry records changes, evidence, decisions, and unresolved questions. Markdown remains editable source and is published as HTML, avoiding separately maintained copies. Existing interactive HTML retains its controls and math. Historical entries explicitly cite their reconstruction. Calendar dates and roadmap working days are separate. Changes ¶ Site catalog . Writing and publishing guide . Site builder . Daily-entry helper . Integration tracker . Follow-up: added a complete main-page directory with a direct link and visible URL for every article, plan, and published daily entry. Displayed URLs resolve to the current host and project subpath, so the same page works locally and after deployment. Website integration: added NTT-GPU · CKKS From Scratch beside the Rust blockchain topic in the personal website's Systems from Scratch section. The notebook is exported to /systems/ntt-gpu/ , with a return link to the website. A repeatable export helper keeps the website copy derived from these source documents. Corrected the integration to use the user's existing website checkout. The topic now opens the explicit HTML page systems/ntt-gpu/index.html rather than a directory URL. Bundled search data also supports opening HTML files directly, alongside local navigation and math assets. Publishing follow-up: directory labels and canonical URLs now use the public https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/ address, including in local previews. Relative navigation continues to support local testing. The source notebook and personal website are being published together. Verification ¶ The site builds 15 HTML pages with four published daily entries. All 11 documentation regression checks passed, including route rewriting, code links, math preservation, draft exclusion, and protection against overwriting entries. The build validates document coverage and local links, assets, and fragments. Browser checks confirmed full-text search from both root and nested journal pages, all 57 rendered formulas, and interactive NTT agreement after changing a coefficient. At a phone-sized viewport, the menu, journal, roadmap tables, and butterfly page fit without page-wide horizontal scrolling. Documentation changes do not add a GPU execution result. The main-page directory follow-up adds a coverage check for all 14 destination URLs, bringing the documentation suite to 12 passing tests. Desktop and phone-sized browser checks confirmed visible URLs and no page-wide overflow. Personal-website integration adds three export regression checks, bringing the suite to 15 passing tests. All 15 exported HTML pages returned HTTP 200 under /systems/ntt-gpu/ ; local assets, links, and fragments resolve. Browser checks confirmed the homepage topic, all 14 destination URLs, 57 rendered formulas, interactive butterfly agreement, and the nested-journal return link. The website and notebook fit a phone-sized viewport without horizontal overflow. Public release verification: source commit f91945b and website commit fd1a73e5 were pushed. Both website Pages workflows completed successfully; the deployment run is the release evidence. All 15 public HTML pages returned HTTP 200. Live browser checks confirmed the homepage topic, public directory URLs, search, all 57 rendered formulas, and interactive butterfly agreement. Open questions ¶ The notebook is now public at the personal website . Future entries should attach named-device measurements when they exist; real GPU execution and the D1 kernel mapping remain outstanding. The standalone project Pages workflow is optional. Next step ¶ Resume D1: map forward_strided_group_kernel blocks, threads, butterfly pairs, twiddles, and memory accesses for N=4096 and N=65536."}, {"title": "Make the butterfly implementation inspectable", "summary": "Added step debugging, clearer CPU variables, and rendered NTT derivations.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-07.html", "text": "2026-11-07 P1 / D1 ← All entries Make the butterfly implementation inspectable ¶ Added step debugging, clearer CPU variables, and rendered NTT derivations. Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from commit 751e723 and verification recorded during that work. Outcome ¶ Connected the direct NTT sum, butterflies, negacyclic twisting, and CPU loops. Added an N=8 VS Code example for source-level debugging. Reasoning ¶ Even/odd subtransforms share terms across opposite output indices; their sign difference produces the butterfly. Odd powers of a 2N-th root provide the negacyclic evaluation points. Rendering those formulas makes the derivation easier to read. Locally bundled KaTeX removes a network dependency for math. Xcode LLDB DAP replaces the observed crashing CodeLLDB adapter. Changes ¶ Implementation and explainer commit . Butterfly derivation . Debugging guide . N=8 example . Verification ¶ Recorded on 2026-11-07: 7/7 CPU CTest checks and 8/8 OpenFHE configuration checks passed. All 57 converted formulas passed strict parsing and browser rendering. The interactive example still matched the direct definition after changing a coefficient. These are not RTX CUDA runtime results. Open questions ¶ How do CUDA blocks and threads select butterfly indices and memory addresses? Next step ¶ Complete D1's forward-kernel index and memory-access map."}, {"title": "A roadmap with explicit gates", "summary": "Tracked the path through OpenFHE integration and clarified implementation naming.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-06.html", "text": "2026-11-06 P1 / D1 ← All entries A roadmap with explicit gates ¶ Tracked the path through OpenFHE integration and clarified implementation naming. Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from the linked commits and reports. Outcome ¶ Added the 95-working-day integration plan, GPU selection survey, and clearer implementation naming and formatting. Reasoning ¶ The sequence is NTT, base conversion, Hybrid key switching, then OpenFHE. Each step requires its own exactness and performance gate. Encrypted inference follows integration. RTX 5090 is the provisional primary target and RTX 4090 the control; the hardware report requires workload-specific benchmarks before claiming a winner. Changes ¶ Roadmap and hardware survey . Readability changes . Tracked plan . Verification ¶ The plan separates the CPU/OpenFHE baseline from the first real GPU gate. No new GPU timing is recorded here. Open questions ¶ Representation, ordering, buffer ownership, and the OpenFHE operation graph still need explicit architecture contracts. Next step ¶ Map the forward column kernel's indices and memory accesses for N=4096 and N=65536."}, {"title": "Exactness before GPU speed", "summary": "Established the OpenFHE transform gate and recorded the GPU NTT research backlog.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-05.html", "text": "2026-11-05 P0 baseline ← All entries Exactness before GPU speed ¶ Established the OpenFHE transform gate and recorded the GPU NTT research backlog. Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from the linked commits and reports. This records documented outcomes, not a contemporaneous account of experiments. Outcome ¶ Added exact transform compatibility against pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1, a GPU NTT survey, and a systematic benchmark plan. Reasoning ¶ CKKS decryption within a tolerance would not establish array-level equality. The gate compares transform values and ordering element by element. The survey identifies modular division, strided access, and host transfers as optimization candidates, not measured speedups. Changes ¶ OpenFHE gate . Survey and tuning plan . Compatibility test . Benchmark methodology . Verification ¶ The gate covers N=4096 through 65536, 50- and 59-bit moduli, and deterministic input patterns. The CPU schedule simulator is a separate correctness check. Real GPU execution remained outstanding. Open questions ¶ How costly are device modular remainder and the column memory transactions? Both require named-device profiling. Next step ¶ Define the implementation sequence and architecture contracts."}, {"title": "Building the GPU path for CKKS", "summary": "The open engineering notebook for ntt-gpu: progress, reasoning, and exactness.", "group": "Start here", "url": "index.html", "text": "An open engineering notebook Notes on encrypted computation. Learn the NTT, inspect the CUDA schedule, and follow the path to exact OpenFHE integration. Browse all pages ↓ Follow our progress RTX 5090 u64 / u32 N = 65,536 Working day D11 ⌁ Current checkpoint P2 — first RTX GPU baseline (offline preparation complete) Validate and record the real GPU environment; D11 hardware gate remains open. Explore the working-day plan Verified baseline CPU / OpenFHE baseline Exact array checks and CPU simulation. GPU execution Hardware gate pending CUDA source exists; RTX validation is next. Current record 2026-11-12 Latest published journal entry. All pages Choose a lesson, reference, plan, or dated journal entry. Start here Project & setup Build, test, and understand the correctness-first CKKS GPU project. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/project.html Learn NTT & butterflies Direct sums, recursive butterflies, handwritten derivations, and an interactive stage/twiddle calculator. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/butterfly-explained.html From CPU to CUDA Threads, blocks, shared memory, and this repository's two-pass GPU schedule. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/gpu-ntt-explained.html 65536: work distribution Explore the 65536-point forward/inverse polynomial, two-pass blocks, threads, and global access patterns. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/ntt-65536-work-distribution.html CUDA buffers & dataflow D5: constructor lifecycle, buffer ownership, host/device copies, fused operations, and integration gaps. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/cuda-buffer-dataflow.html Arithmetic bounds & tests D6: canonical residues, overflow proofs, bigint checks, full-size adversarial cases, and remaining exactness gates. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/modular-arithmetic-contract.html OpenFHE representation D7: exact coefficient/evaluation ordering, root selection, DCRT tower packing, format APIs, and executable compatibility checks. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/openfhe-representation-contract.html Hybrid operation graph D8: exact digit extension, Q/P tower addressing, key MAC, ModDown, delayed operations, and full-size OpenFHE checks. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/openfhe-hybrid-operation-graph.html Device backend contract D9: checked buffer views, stream dependencies, resource leases, cache identities, and the proposed OpenFHE adapter. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/device-backend-contract.html Run & debug Run the N=8 example and step through each butterfly in VS Code. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/vscode-debugging.html Research GPU NTT implementations Primary-source survey, performance limits, and techniques to evaluate. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/gpu-ntt-survey.html Choosing the GPU Compare hardware for exact u32/u64 modular arithmetic and CKKS workloads. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/gpu-selection-survey.html Plans Integration roadmap The tracked 95-working-day plan through exact OpenFHE Hybrid integration. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/openfhe-integration-plan.html Architecture decision D10: accepted representation, backend ownership, adapter/fallback policy, resource budgets and P1 review. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/architecture-decision.html Implementation backlog D10: dependency-ordered native GPU, resident runtime, cache, conversion, Hybrid and adapter acceptance gates. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/implementation-backlog.html Before renting a GPU Completed compiler-only preparation, baseline safety fixes, tested session reporting and the first real-GPU handoff. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/offline-gpu-preparation.html Benchmarks & tuning Workload matrix, profiling evidence, and per-device kernel selection. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/benchmark-tuning-plan.html Daily journal All journal entries The complete daily progress timeline. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal.html 2026-11-12 · Define and accept the device backend architecture D9 and D10 close P1; compiler-only CUDA preparation is ready while D11 hardware verification remains open. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-12.html 2026-11-11 · Freeze the OpenFHE NTT and DCRT representation contract Completed D7 with full-size OpenFHE representation checks and prepared the accumulated D3–D7 notebook for publication. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-11.html 2026-11-10 · Prove inverse correspondence and complete stage coverage Completed D3–D6: inverse/stage proofs, CUDA dataflow, arithmetic bounds, and independent adversarial checks. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-10.html 2026-11-09 · Make the 4096-point CUDA schedule tangible Added an interactive explanation of polynomial layout and block/thread ownership. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-09.html 2026-11-08 · Open the engineering notebook Unified learning pages, research, plans, and daily reasoning in a navigable HTML site. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-08.html 2026-11-07 · Make the butterfly implementation inspectable Added step debugging, clearer CPU variables, and rendered NTT derivations. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-07.html 2026-11-06 · A roadmap with explicit gates Tracked the path through OpenFHE integration and clarified implementation naming. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-06.html 2026-11-05 · Exactness before GPU speed Established the OpenFHE transform gate and recorded the GPU NTT research backlog. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/journal/2026-11-05.html Contribute Writing & publishing Preview the site, add a daily journal entry, and publish the HTML documentation. https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/contributing-docs.html Implementation sequence Tracked plan NTT Exactness, GPU validation, tuning Base conversion Q ↔ QP Hybrid key switching Decompose, multiply, reduce OpenFHE Integration and CPU fallback Encrypted inference After integration"}, {"title": "Daily engineering journal", "summary": "Dated outcomes, reasoning, verification, and next steps.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal.html", "text": "THE DAILY JOURNAL The work, as it happens. Outcomes, decisions, and the evidence behind them. Follow the project one entry at a time. How to read the journal Dates record when work was documented. Roadmap days track deliverables. Reconstructed history is labeled; CPU checks and GPU execution are recorded separately. Entries 12 Nov 2026 P1 complete / P2 D11 preparation Define and accept the device backend architecture D9 and D10 close P1; compiler-only CUDA preparation is ready while D11 hardware verification remains open. Read the entry 11 Nov 2026 P1 / D7 → D8 Freeze the OpenFHE NTT and DCRT representation contract Completed D7 with full-size OpenFHE representation checks and prepared the accumulated D3–D7 notebook for publication. Read the entry 10 Nov 2026 P1 / D3 → D7 Prove inverse correspondence and complete stage coverage Completed D3–D6: inverse/stage proofs, CUDA dataflow, arithmetic bounds, and independent adversarial checks. Read the entry 09 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 → D3 Make the 4096-point CUDA schedule tangible Added an interactive explanation of polynomial layout and block/thread ownership. Read the entry 08 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 Open the engineering notebook Unified learning pages, research, plans, and daily reasoning in a navigable HTML site. Read the entry 07 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 Make the butterfly implementation inspectable Added step debugging, clearer CPU variables, and rendered NTT derivations. Read the entry 06 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 A roadmap with explicit gates Tracked the path through OpenFHE integration and clarified implementation naming. Read the entry 05 Nov 2026 P0 baseline Exactness before GPU speed Established the OpenFHE transform gate and recorded the GPU NTT research backlog. Read the entry"}];
